var tafsirData_6 = {
  "1": "'All praise is for Allah Who created the heavens and the earth and made darkness and light. Yet the disbelievers set up equals to their Lord ˹in worship˺.' (Quran 6:1). The surah opens with praise and turns immediately to the contradiction.\n\n'All praise is for Allah' — al-hamdu lillah. Five surahs of the Quran begin this way, and each time the praise is attached to something specific. Here it is attached to creation.\n\n'Created the heavens and the earth' — khalaqa al-samawati wa al-ard. And then: 'made darkness and light' — wa ja'ala al-zulumati wa al-nur.\n\nNotice the difference in the two verbs. 'Khalaqa' for the heavens and earth; 'ja'ala' for darkness and light. And notice the number: darkness is plural — zulumat — while light is singular — nur.\n\nThat pattern runs through the Quran. There are many ways to be lost and one way to be guided, and the surah will spend much of its length on precisely that point.\n\n'Yet the disbelievers set up equals to their Lord' — thumma alladhina kafaru bi rabbihim ya'dilun. 'Ya'dilun' is to make something equal, to balance one thing against another.\n\nThe word 'thumma' — then, afterwards — is the sting. After all that creating, after the heavens and the earth and the light itself, they place something else alongside Him.\n\nThe whole argument of Surah al-An'am is contained in this sentence. It is a surah about one question: who made this, and what follows from the answer.",
  "2": "'He is the One Who created you from clay, then appointed a term ˹for your death˺ and another known only to Him ˹for your resurrection˺ —yet you continue to doubt!' (Quran 6:2). From the heavens and the earth in 6:1 down to the person reading.\n\n'Created you from clay' — khalaqakum min tin. The material is deliberately unimpressive. The Quran describes it elsewhere as 'sounding clay like pottery' (Quran 55:14).\n\nAnyone tempted to think of himself as a special case is directed to the raw material.\n\n'Then appointed a term' — thumma qada ajalan. A fixed span, set in advance. The word 'ajal' is a deadline, not an estimate.\n\n'And another known only to Him' — wa ajalun musamman 'indah. Two terms, and the second is held back. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, reads the first as the term of this life, ending at death, and the second as the term of the whole creation, ending at the Resurrection.\n\nBoth are decided. Neither is disclosed.\n\n'Yet you continue to doubt' — thumma antum tamtarun. And here is the logic the verse is built on. The One who made you from clay set both dates. Doubt about the second makes no sense once the first is accepted, because the harder act — bringing a person out of dirt — is already behind us.\n\nThe surah returns to this argument repeatedly. Creation is not a claim about the past. It is evidence about the future.",
  "3": "'He is the Only True God in the heavens and the earth. He knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal, and knows whatever you do.' (Quran 6:3). Ownership in 6:1, then the term of your life in 6:2, and now knowledge.\n\n'He is the Only True God in the heavens and the earth' — wa huwa Allahu fi al-samawati wa fi al-ard. One authority across both domains. There is no region of reality under different management.\n\nThen three things He knows, and the order is deliberate: 'whatever you conceal' first, 'whatever you reveal' second, and 'whatever you do' third.\n\nThe hidden is named before the visible. That reverses the way people naturally think about themselves, and it is meant to.\n\n'Whatever you conceal' — sirrakum. What you keep private, including from those closest to you. The intention behind the action, the reason behind the reason.\n\n'And whatever you do' — ma taksibun. The word is from 'kasb', earning. Deeds are described as things you acquire and accumulate, which is why the verse can end here and leave the consequence unstated.\n\nThe same pairing appeared at the close of the previous surah: 'Allah fully knows what you reveal and what you conceal' (Quran 5:99), where it explained why a messenger's job ends at delivery.\n\nHere it does something else. It answers the person who has just been told his lifespan is fixed and asks what will be measured when it runs out.\n\nEverything. Including the part nobody saw.",
  "4": "'Whenever a sign comes to them from their Lord, they turn away from it.' (Quran 6:4). A short verse describing a settled habit.\n\n'Whenever' — wa ma ta'tihim min ayatin. The construction covers every instance. Not some signs, not the difficult ones. Any sign at all.\n\n'A sign' — ayah. The word covers both a verse of revelation and an event in the world. Both are evidence, and the Quran uses one word for them because it treats them as the same kind of thing.\n\n'They turn away from it' — illa kanu 'anha mu'ridin. 'I'rad' is turning your face aside. Not arguing, not refuting — declining to look.\n\nThat is the specific behaviour this verse names, and it explains why the demand for more signs, which appears in 6:8 and 6:37, is not a real request. A person who turns away from what has arrived will turn away from what arrives next.\n\nThe pattern is important for reading the rest of the surah. Much of al-An'am is evidence: the heavens, the earth, the term of life, the rain, the crops, the stars. Verse after verse presents something observable.\n\nAnd this verse, placed at the start, states plainly that evidence is not the missing ingredient.\n\nWhat is missing is willingness to look. Every argument the surah makes afterwards is offered to a reader who has been warned, in advance, that arguments alone do not move anyone who has already turned his face.",
  "5": "'They have indeed rejected the truth when it came to them, so they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule.' (Quran 6:5). The behaviour of 6:4 named for what it is.\n\n'They have indeed rejected the truth' — fa qad kadhdhabu bi al-haqq. 'Takdhib' is declaring something false — a step beyond the turning away described in the previous verse.\n\n'When it came to them' — lamma ja'ahum. The truth was not something they went looking for and failed to find. It arrived.\n\n'The consequences of their ridicule' — anba'u ma kanu bihi yastahzi'un. The news of what they mocked. What they treated as a joke will arrive as information they cannot dismiss.\n\nNotice that mockery is treated here as a form of argument, and the Quran's response is not to argue back. It says: wait.\n\nThat is a particular kind of confidence. Ridicule works by making something look unserious, and it cannot be answered by explanation, because the mocker was never listening to explanations. It can only be answered by events.\n\n'They will soon' — sawfa. The word implies certainty about the fact and vagueness about the timing.\n\nThis is the third verse in a row about response rather than content. Verse 6:3 established what Allah knows; 6:4 described people turning away; this names the turning away as rejection and states what follows.\n\nAnd the next verse produces the evidence that it does follow, from history rather than from the future.",
  "6": "'Have they not seen how many ˹disbelieving˺ peoples We destroyed before them? We had made them more established in the land than you...' (Quran 6:6). The argument moves from warning to record.\n\n'Have they not seen' — a lam yaraw. The evidence is available. Ruins were on the trade routes the Meccans travelled.\n\n'More established in the land than you' — makkannahum fi al-ardi ma lam numakkin lakum. Better settled, more securely placed. The Quran is comparing the audience unfavourably with people who are gone.\n\nThat is a pointed thing to say to a community proud of its standing. Whatever advantage you think protects you, they had more of it.\n\n'We sent down abundant rain for them and made rivers flow at their feet' — the description is of prosperity, and of prosperity as a gift rather than an achievement. Rain sent down; rivers made to flow.\n\n'Then We destroyed them for their sins' — fa ahlaknahum bi dhunubihim. The cause is stated. Not bad luck, not decline, not the ordinary rise and fall of nations.\n\n'And replaced them with other peoples' — wa ansha'na min ba'dihim qarnan akharin. And the replacements are the ones now being addressed. The audience of this verse is itself a link in the chain it is being asked to examine.\n\nThe Quran repeats this argument at 30:9 and 40:82, both times with the same instruction: travel and look. Comfort is not evidence of approval. It is what the people in the ruins also had.",
  "7": "'Had We sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ a revelation in writing and they were to touch it with their own hands, the disbelievers would still have said, \"This is nothing but pure magic!\"' (Quran 6:7). The demand for proof, tested.\n\nThe scenario is designed to remove every objection. Not a recitation to be doubted, but a physical document sent down from the sky. Not seen from a distance, but handled — 'touch it with their own hands', fa lamasuhu bi aydihim.\n\nSight can be disputed. Touch is the sense people fall back on when they want to be sure of something.\n\nAnd the verse says the answer would be the same: 'This is nothing but pure magic.'\n\nMagic is the standing explanation for anything undeniable. It concedes that something real happened and denies what it means. That is why the accusation appears so often — at 5:110 the Children of Israel said it of 'Isa's miracles, and it is said of the Quran itself here.\n\nThe verse is not claiming these people are unusually stubborn. It is describing how anyone behaves when the conclusion is unacceptable in advance. The evidence gets reclassified.\n\nThis also explains something about how the Quran argues. It does not compete to produce more spectacular proof, because the problem was never the quality of the proof.\n\nVerse 6:4 said they turn away from every sign. This verse shows what happens when a sign is made impossible to turn away from: it gets renamed instead.",
  "8": "'They say, \"Why has no ˹visible˺ angel come with him?\" Had We sent down an angel, the matter would have certainly been settled ˹at once˺, and they would have never been given more time ˹to repent˺.' (Quran 6:8). A request, answered with what granting it would cost.\n\n'Why has no angel come with him' — law la unzila 'alayhi malak. The demand is for a messenger who is visibly not human. The same objection is quoted at 25:7: 'What kind of messenger is this who eats food and goes about in market-places?'\n\nThe reply does not dismiss the request. It explains the consequence: 'the matter would have certainly been settled at once' — la qudiya al-amr.\n\nOnce an angel appears in its own form, the matter is no longer a question. It becomes a fact, and the time for accepting or refusing is over. The Quran states the rule directly at 15:8: 'We do not send the angels down except for a just cause, and then the end of the disbelievers will not be delayed.'\n\n'They would have never been given more time' — thumma la yunzarun. No reprieve, no interval.\n\nSo the delay they are complaining about is the mercy keeping them alive. They are asking for the thing that would end the possibility of their repentance.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's most useful reframings. A person who demands undeniable proof is asking to have his choice taken away, and imagining that this would be a favour.\n\nCertainty of that kind arrives eventually. Verse 6:158 describes the day it does, and says belief will no longer help.",
  "9": "'And if We had sent an angel, We would have certainly made it ˹assume the form of˺ a man—leaving them more confused than they already are.' (Quran 6:9). The second half of the answer to the demand in 6:8.\n\nSuppose the request were granted in a way that left room for choice. The angel would have to appear as a man, because a human audience cannot deal with an angel in its own form.\n\n'We would have certainly made it a man' — la ja'alnahu rajulan. And then the objection returns exactly where it started. They would be looking at a man and told he is a messenger from Allah, which is the situation they are already objecting to.\n\n'Leaving them more confused than they already are' — wa la labasna 'alayhim ma yalbisun. The verb 'labasa' is to obscure or make ambiguous, and the sentence turns it back on them: We would have confused them with the same confusion they are creating for themselves.\n\nThat is the sharpest part of the verse. The confusion is their own product. Granting the request would simply hand it back to them in a new form.\n\nTaken together, 6:8 and 6:9 close both exits. An angel in its own form ends the test. An angel in human form recreates the objection.\n\nThere was never a version of the demand that could be satisfied, which means the demand was not really about angels.\n\nAl-Tabari, commenting on this verse, notes that the request was made as a challenge rather than an inquiry — and 6:4 said as much before the demand was even quoted.",
  "10": "'˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.' (Quran 6:10). A word to the one delivering the message.\n\nThe surah has been addressing the deniers. Here it turns, briefly, to the Prophet.\n\n'Had already been ridiculed before you' — wa laqad istuhzi'a bi rusulin min qablik. What is happening to you has happened to every messenger sent. This is the pattern, not a sign that something has gone wrong.\n\nThere is real comfort in that, and it is a specific kind. Not 'this will pass', but 'this is normal'. Mockery is what the work attracts.\n\n'Were overtaken by what they used to ridicule' — fa haqa bi alladhina sakhiru minhum ma kanu bihi yastahzi'un. 'Haqa' is to surround and close in. The thing they laughed at came round and enclosed them.\n\nNote the precision of the outcome. They are not punished with something unrelated; they are overtaken by the very thing they mocked. They laughed at a warning of destruction, and destruction arrived. The joke and the verdict are the same object.\n\nThis restates 6:5 — 'they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule' — but with the past tense added. It has already happened, more than once.\n\nFor anyone carrying an unpopular truth, the verse offers a method: do not answer ridicule. Deliver, and let the outcome answer it.\n\nThe next verse says where to look for the outcome.",
  "11": "'Say, \"Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers.\"' (Quran 6:11). An instruction, not an argument.\n\n'Travel throughout the land' — siru fi al-ard. Go and look. The Quran issues this command repeatedly, at 30:9 and 40:82 among other places, and every time the object is the same: the remains of people who were more powerful than you.\n\nConsider what kind of evidence is being offered. Not a doctrine, not a text, not a miracle. Ruins.\n\nThat is a claim any traveller can check, and the Meccans were traders whose caravans passed the sites of vanished peoples on the routes north and south. The verse is telling them to notice what they already drive past.\n\n'The fate of the deniers' — 'aqibatu al-mukadhdhibin. ''Aqibah' is the end of a thing, what it comes to.\n\nThis is the Quran's answer to the demand for a sign in 6:8. You asked for an angel; here is a whole landscape of evidence, and it requires no miracle to examine.\n\nThe instruction also carries a method for reading history. Nations are not simply strong or weak. They are accountable, and their ends are informative.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, treats the command as including reflection as well as travel — that the point is not the journey but what the traveller lets himself conclude.\n\nWhich returns to 6:4. The ruins are visible to anyone. Seeing them is a separate act.",
  "12": "'Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"To whom belongs everything in the heavens and the earth?\" Say, \"To Allah!\" He has taken upon Himself to be Merciful...' (Quran 6:12). A question the Prophet is told to answer himself.\n\n'Ask them... Say, \"To Allah!\"' The question is put and then answered in the same breath, and that is deliberate. The Meccans did not deny that Allah created and owned everything. They agreed on the premise and refused the conclusion.\n\nSo the verse does not wait for their reply. It states the answer they already hold and moves on to what follows from it.\n\n'He has taken upon Himself to be Merciful' — kataba 'ala nafsihi al-rahmah. Literally: He has written mercy upon Himself. An obligation, self-imposed, by the One who owns everything and owes nothing.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'When Allah created the Creation, He wrote in His Book — and He wrote (that) about Himself, and it is placed with Him on the Throne — \"Verily My Mercy overcomes My Anger\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 7404).\n\n'He will certainly gather all of you together for the Day of Judgment' — the ownership and the mercy lead here. Gathering is stated as a fact, not a threat.\n\n'But those who have ruined themselves will never believe' — alladhina khasiru anfusahum. The loss is reflexive. They lost themselves, and the verse names no other party as responsible.\n\nOwnership, mercy, resurrection, self-inflicted loss. Four claims, one sentence.",
  "13": "'To Him belongs whatever exists in the day and night. And He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 6:13). Ownership stated again, now measured in time rather than space.\n\nVerse 6:12 asked who owns the heavens and the earth. This asks nothing; it simply extends the answer.\n\n'Whatever exists in the day and night' — ma sakana fi al-layli wa al-nahar. The verb 'sakana' means to dwell or come to rest. Everything that settles anywhere, in any hour, belongs to Him.\n\nPlacing ownership in time rather than place is unusual and worth noticing. Space can be pictured as a container with contents. Time cannot be surveyed, and nothing in it can be held. Yet it is populated — every moment has its occupants — and all of them are owned.\n\nThere is no hour that belongs to someone else. No part of your day is off the record.\n\n'And He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing' — al-Sami'u al-'Alim. The same two names that closed 5:76, and again they are the names that matter to a person who is alone.\n\nHearing covers what is said, including at night when no one else is awake. Knowing covers what is never said at all — which is what 6:3 established at the start of the surah.\n\nThe verse is short and it is doing something specific in the argument. Verse 6:12 raised the Day of Judgment. This explains how a complete account is possible: every moment was witnessed as it happened.",
  "14": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Will I take any guardian other than Allah, the Originator of the heavens and the earth, Who provides for all and is not in need of provision?\"...' (Quran 6:14). The Prophet is made to answer for himself.\n\nThe question is rhetorical, and its force comes from the descriptions packed into it.\n\n'The Originator' — fatir. The one who split something open and brought it into being from nothing. The word carries the sense of an initial tearing, a first act with nothing before it.\n\n'Who provides for all and is not in need of provision' — wa huwa yut'imu wa la yut'am. He feeds and is not fed. This is the argument of 5:75 in its general form: what eats, depends. What depends is not divine.\n\nAnd it identifies exactly what is wrong with taking any other guardian. A 'wali' is someone you rely on. Every candidate other than Allah is itself being fed by Him.\n\n'I have been commanded to be the first to submit' — awwalu man aslam. The Prophet is not exempted from the message he brings. He is placed first in line under it.\n\nThat matters for how the whole surah reads. He is not arguing for a position that privileges him. He is describing an arrangement he is inside.\n\n'And not be one of the polytheists' — wa la takunanna mina al-mushrikin. The instruction is given to him directly, which is the strongest way to state that no one is above the requirement.",
  "15": "'Say, \"I truly fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the torment of a tremendous Day.\"' (Quran 6:15). The Prophet speaks about himself again, as he did in 6:14.\n\n'I truly fear' — inni akhafu. He is instructed to say this out loud, in public, to people who were pressing him to compromise.\n\nThat is the function of the verse. The Meccans wanted concessions — soften the message, leave our gods alone. The answer given is not a refusal on grounds of stubbornness. It is a statement of fear.\n\n'If I were to disobey my Lord' — in 'asaytu rabbi. He places himself under the same command he is delivering. Verse 6:14 said he was ordered to be 'the first to submit'; this says what happens if he is not.\n\n'The torment of a tremendous Day' — 'adhaba yawmin 'azim. The Day is described as tremendous, not the torment. The scale belongs to the occasion.\n\nThere is something worth noticing here about who fears. This is the man closest to Allah among all people, and fear is what he is told to express.\n\nThe Quran uses this pattern often: the more a person knows, the more seriously he takes the warning. Complacency is not a sign of high standing.\n\nFor the reader, the verse removes a common excuse. Nobody can say the Prophet was speaking from a position of guaranteed safety while asking others to take risks. He is standing under the same sky.\n\nAnd the next verse says what the alternative looks like.",
  "16": "'Whoever is spared the torment of that Day will have certainly been shown Allah's mercy. And that is the absolute triumph.' (Quran 6:16). The other side of 6:15.\n\n'Whoever is spared' — man yusraf 'anhu. The verb is passive. It is turned away from him; he does not fight it off.\n\nThat passive form is the whole content of the verse. Escape on that Day is something done for you, not something you accomplish.\n\n'Will have certainly been shown Allah's mercy' — fa qad rahimah. The verse identifies the cause and it is not the person's record. It is mercy.\n\nThis is the balance the Quran keeps. Deeds matter — the surah has already said Allah 'knows whatever you do' (Quran 6:3) — and yet no quantity of them purchases the outcome. They put you in the way of mercy; mercy does the rest.\n\n'And that is the absolute triumph' — wa dhalika al-fawzu al-mubin. The clear, evident triumph.\n\nNote how success is defined. Not entering Paradise, but escaping the Fire. The Quran states it the same way at 3:185: 'Whoever is spared from the Fire and is admitted into Paradise will indeed triumph.'\n\nThat definition is worth carrying around. Most people measure a life by what they gained. This measures it by what they avoided.\n\nVerse 6:12 already said that Allah 'has taken upon Himself to be Merciful'. This verse is what that written mercy looks like on the Day it is applied.",
  "17": "'If Allah touches you with harm, none can undo it except Him. And if He touches you with a blessing, He is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 6:17). A single rule with two applications.\n\n'Touches you' — yamsaska. The verb is used for both harm and blessing, and it is a gentle word — a touch, not a blow. Whatever reaches you, reaches you by contact permitted from one direction only.\n\n'None can undo it except Him' — fa la kashifa lahu illa hu. 'Kashf' is the lifting or removal of something covering you.\n\nNow put this beside 6:14, which asked whether the Prophet would take any guardian other than Allah. This verse is the reason the answer is no. A guardian is worth having only if he can lift what has fallen on you, and the verse says only One can.\n\nThe same principle appears at 10:107 and at 35:2: 'Whatever mercy Allah opens up for people, none can withhold it. And whatever He withholds, none but Him can release it.'\n\nNotice the asymmetry in the wording here. For harm: nobody else can remove it. For blessing: 'He is Most Capable of everything'. The sentence does not bother to say nobody can block it — it simply names His power, and the point is made.\n\nThe practical effect of believing this is a particular kind of steadiness. Whatever the source of your trouble appears to be, the address for relief does not change.\n\nAnd whoever you think is holding back your good, they are not.",
  "18": "'He reigns supreme over His creation. And He is the All-Wise, All-Aware.' (Quran 6:18). Two words of description, and they are chosen to be read together.\n\n'He reigns supreme over His creation' — wa huwa al-qahiru fawqa 'ibadih. 'Al-Qahir' is the Overpowering, the One whose will prevails against all resistance.\n\nThat name alone would be frightening. So it is immediately paired: 'the All-Wise, All-Aware' — al-Hakimu al-Khabir.\n\nConsider what each addition does. Power without wisdom would be arbitrary. Power with wisdom but without awareness would be well-intentioned and badly informed. 'Al-Khabir' is knowledge of the inner detail of things — what is actually going on, beneath the surface.\n\nOverwhelming power, exercised wisely, on the basis of complete information. Remove any one of the three and the picture becomes something a person would reasonably fear.\n\n'Over His creation' — the word used is ''ibadih', His servants. Not subjects, not property in the abstract. The relationship is named even in the verse about domination.\n\nThis follows directly from 6:17. If only He can lift harm, the question that arises is what kind of Being holds that position. This verse answers it.\n\nIt also answers the Meccans' arrangement. Their gods were understood as intermediaries with influence. But influence assumes someone who can be influenced, and 'al-Qahir fawqa 'ibadih' leaves no room above.\n\nThere is nobody standing over Him to be lobbied.",
  "19": "'Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Who is the best witness?\" Say, \"Allah is! He is a Witness between me and you. And this Quran has been revealed to me so that, with it, I may warn you and whoever it reaches...\"' (Quran 6:19). A testimony demanded, given, and refused.\n\nThe Meccans had asked for someone to vouch for him. The answer names the only witness whose testimony would settle anything.\n\n'So that, with it, I may warn you and whoever it reaches' — li undhirakum bihi wa man balagh. That phrase extends the audience past the people standing there. Whoever the Quran reaches is included, which means the reader of this sentence is inside the verse.\n\nThat is a remarkable clause for a text delivered orally in a small city, and it is stated as a matter of fact rather than ambition.\n\n'Do you pagans testify that there are other gods besides Allah?' — the question is put back to them, and the Prophet does not wait for an answer either. 'Say, \"I will never testify to this!\"'\n\nThen the closing: 'There is only One God. And I totally reject whatever idols you associate with Him' — wa innani bari'un mimma tushrikun. 'Bara'ah' is disavowal, cutting all connection.\n\nTwo things are being separated. Allah's witness, which stands whether or not anyone agrees; and the Prophet's own position, which he states in the first person and will not soften.\n\nThis is what 6:15 meant in practice. He fears one Day, so he cannot manage this one by making it comfortable.",
  "20": "'Those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him ˹to be a true prophet˺ as they recognize their own children. Those who have ruined themselves will never believe.' (Quran 6:20). Recognition, and what recognition does not guarantee.\n\n'As they recognize their own children' — kama ya'rifuna abna'ahum. The comparison is exact and it is chosen for a reason. Nobody hesitates over their own child in a crowd. There is no process of deduction.\n\nThe same phrase appears at 2:146: 'Those We have given the Scripture recognize this Prophet as they recognize their own children. Yet a group of them hides the truth knowingly.'\n\nBoth verses put the same two facts side by side. They knew. It did not follow that they accepted.\n\nThat is the hard observation this verse is making, and it applies far beyond its original audience. Knowing something is true and acting on it are separate events, and the gap between them is where most people actually live.\n\n'Those who have ruined themselves will never believe' — alladhina khasiru anfusahum fa hum la yu'minun. The exact phrase from 6:12, repeated here.\n\nNote the order of the sentence. The loss comes first, the disbelief second. They are not ruined because they disbelieve; the verse presents the disbelief as the symptom of a loss already sustained.\n\nSomething has been given up before the refusal is made. This surah names that thing repeatedly, and 6:70 will name it directly: a life taken as play and amusement.",
  "21": "'Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His signs? Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.' (Quran 6:21). A question with no answer available.\n\n'Who does more wrong' — wa man azlamu. The construction appears repeatedly in this surah — at 6:93 and 6:157 as well — and each time it names an offence and asks for anything worse.\n\nTwo are named here, and they are opposites. 'Fabricate lies against Allah' — adding what He did not say. 'Deny His signs' — subtracting what He did.\n\nBoth are misrepresentation. One inflates the message, the other erases it, and the verse ranks them together.\n\nThat pairing has a use. People tend to fear one error and not the other. The cautious person fears inventing; the confident person fears missing out. The verse says both are the same wrong seen from different sides, which is exactly what 5:87 and 5:103 established about self-invented prohibitions.\n\n'The wrongdoers will never succeed' — innahu la yuflihu al-zalimun. 'Falah' is success in the full sense, and the denial is absolute.\n\nThere is an important limit implied in this. The verse describes a fabrication about Allah — speech attributed to Him. It is not describing an honest mistake by someone trying to understand.\n\nAl-Tabari, commenting on this verse, ties the fabrication specifically to the claims the Meccans made about their idols having a share in Allah's authority.\n\nThey were not lying about a small thing. They were signing His name.",
  "22": "'˹Consider˺ the Day We will gather them all together then ask those who associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺, \"Where are those gods you used to claim?\"' (Quran 6:22). A question asked at the only moment when it can be answered honestly.\n\n'Where are those gods you used to claim?' — ayna shuraka'ukumu alladhina kuntum taz'umun. Note the possessive: 'your partners'. They are attributed to the people who invented them, not to Allah, because He never had any.\n\n'You used to claim' — taz'umun. The verb 'za'ama' is used in Arabic for an assertion made without evidence.\n\nThe question is not for information. It is the kind of question that consists entirely of its own answer, asked so that the person hears himself say it.\n\nConsider what is being staged. A lifetime of appeals, offerings and loyalties, and now the objects of all that are simply asked for. Produce them. Bring them here.\n\nThe Quran uses this device often because it works on the reader now, not just on the people in the scene. Whatever a person actually relies on can be tested by the same question: where is it, when everything else is gone?\n\n'We will gather them all together' — yawma nahshuruhum jami'an. This was announced in 6:12: 'He will certainly gather all of you together for the Day of Judgment— about which there is no doubt.'\n\nThe gathering was stated as a fact there. Here it is put to use.\n\nAnd their reply, in the next verse, is not the one anyone would predict.",
  "23": "'Their only argument will be: \"By Allah, our Lord! We were never polytheists.\"' (Quran 6:23). Asked to produce their gods, they deny ever having had any.\n\n'Their only argument' — thumma lam takun fitnatuhum illa an qalu. There was nothing else to say.\n\n'By Allah, our Lord!' — wa Allahi rabbina. They swear. On the Day of Judgment, in front of Allah, they take an oath.\n\nThat detail is extraordinary and it is the point of the verse. Lying has become so habitual that it survives into a setting where it obviously cannot work. This is not a calculated strategy; it is a reflex.\n\n'We were never polytheists' — ma kunna mushrikin. And in a sense they may mean it. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that they had convinced themselves their worship of the idols was not really shirk — it was intercession, honouring the righteous, following the fathers.\n\nThat is the more chilling reading. They are not necessarily lying about the facts. They are repeating the excuse they used all their lives, because they never once let themselves see the act plainly.\n\nThe Quran addressed the excuse itself at 2:170: 'We only follow what we found our forefathers practicing.' It was never a reason. It was a way of not looking.\n\nA person can construct a description of his own behaviour so carefully that he believes it. This verse shows how long that description lasts.\n\nRight up to the moment described in the next verse.",
  "24": "'See how they will lie about themselves and how those ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them!' (Quran 6:24). The verdict on the oath sworn in 6:23.\n\n'Lie about themselves' — kadhabu 'ala anfusihim. Not 'to Allah' — about themselves. That is the precise charge.\n\nThe lie is a lie about their own history, their own choices, their own inner life. And the Quran has already established that this is exactly the area Allah has full access to: 'He knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal' (Quran 6:3).\n\nSo the falsehood is aimed at the one subject on which they have no privacy at all.\n\n'And how those gods they fabricated will fail them' — wa dalla 'anhum ma kanu yaftarun. 'Dalla' is to go astray, to be lost. What they invented wanders off from them.\n\nThe image is one of things quietly disappearing rather than being taken away. Whatever was never real does not need to be destroyed on that Day. It simply is not there.\n\n'See how' — unzur kayfa. The reader is told to watch, which is the second time the surah uses this device.\n\nThere is an instruction hidden in this verse, and it concerns the present. The lie people tell about themselves is told long before the Day of Judgment. It is told daily, in small corrections and favourable descriptions, and by the end a person may believe it completely.\n\nThat is the fabrication being watched here. The idols were only its objects.",
  "25": "'There are some of them who ˹pretend to˺ listen to your recitation ˹of the Quran˺, but We have cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend it—and deafness in their ears...' (Quran 6:25). Listening without hearing.\n\n'Who listen to your recitation' — man yastami'u ilayk. They attend. They are physically present at the recitation, which is more than most of the opponents in this surah do.\n\n'Veils over their hearts' — akinnatan an yafqahuh. 'Akinnah' are coverings, and the purpose clause is stated: so that they do not understand it.\n\nThis raises the hardest question in the verse: who put the veils there? The grammar says Allah did. And the Quran also records these same people saying it of themselves: 'Our hearts are veiled against what you are calling us to, there is deafness in our ears' (Quran 41:5).\n\nBoth are true, and the order matters. Verse 6:4 described them turning away from every sign, freely, repeatedly. The veil is what a habit becomes when it has been practised long enough. A capacity refused is eventually a capacity lost.\n\n'Even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe' — which is what 6:7 already demonstrated with the written revelation they could touch.\n\n'This Quran is nothing but ancient fables' — asatiru al-awwalin. The dismissal they arrive at is quoted at 8:31, 16:24 and 25:5 too. It is a formula, not a response.\n\nCalling something old is the easiest way to avoid saying whether it is true.",
  "26": "'They turn others away from the Prophet and distance themselves as well. They ruin none but themselves, yet they fail to perceive it.' (Quran 6:26). Two actions, and their real target.\n\n'They turn others away' — wa hum yanhawna 'anh. They forbid people from him. 'And distance themselves as well' — wa yan'awna 'anh, they keep away.\n\nBoth at once, and the combination is worth noting. It would be one thing to keep away privately. These are people who also work to keep others away, which means they take the message seriously enough to consider it dangerous.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, records that some read it as describing those who protected the Prophet from harm while refusing to follow him — Abu Talib being the standing example.\n\n'They ruin none but themselves' — wa in yuhlikuna illa anfusahum. The damage lands nowhere else.\n\nThis is the third time in twenty-six verses that loss has been described as self-inflicted: 'those who have ruined themselves' at 6:12 and again at 6:20, and now this.\n\n'Yet they fail to perceive it' — wa ma yash'urun. They cannot feel it happening. That is the nature of this particular injury; it produces no sensation at the time.\n\nAbu Dharr reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, relating that Allah said: 'it is but your deeds that I record for you and then recompense you for. So let him who finds good, praise Allah, and let him who finds other than that blame no one but himself' (Sahih Muslim 2577a).",
  "27": "'If only you could see when they will be detained before the Fire! They will cry, \"Oh! If only we could be sent back, we would never deny the signs of our Lord and we would ˹surely˺ be of the believers.\"' (Quran 6:27). The wish that arrives too late.\n\n'When they will be detained' — idh wuqifu 'ala al-nar. Held there, stopped in front of it. Not yet in it — made to stand and look.\n\nThat detail is the whole scene. They are given a full view before anything happens, and the wish comes out of them at the sight.\n\n'If only we could be sent back' — ya laytana nuraddu. The Quran records this plea in several places, and always in the same shape: 'Our Lord! We have now seen and heard, so send us back and we will do good. We truly have sure faith now!' (Quran 32:12), and at 23:99–100 where it is answered with a single word: 'Never!'\n\n'We would never deny the signs of our Lord' — the promise is specific. It names exactly the behaviour described in 6:4: turning away from every sign that came.\n\nSo they have understood correctly what went wrong. The diagnosis is right; the timing is useless.\n\n'If only you could see' — wa law tara. The verse is addressed to the reader, and the sentence is deliberately left unfinished. If you could see it, then — what? The Quran does not complete it, because whatever would follow is beyond the reader's imagination.\n\nThe plea itself is not the problem. The Quran encourages that plea constantly, at 63:10 and elsewhere, while it can still be acted on.",
  "28": "'But no! ˹They only say this˺ because the truth they used to hide will become all too clear to them. Even if they were to be sent back, they would certainly revert to what they were forbidden. Indeed they are liars!' (Quran 6:28). The wish of 6:27 examined and refused.\n\n'Because the truth they used to hide will become all too clear to them' — bal bada lahum ma kanu yukhfuna min qabl. What they had concealed has been exposed, and the promise is a reaction to exposure rather than a change of heart.\n\nThat is the distinction the verse is drawing, and it is a sharp one. Regret at being caught looks exactly like repentance and is not the same thing.\n\n'Even if they were to be sent back, they would certainly revert' — wa law ruddu la 'adu lima nuhu 'anh. Allah states the outcome of a second chance that will never be given. He knows what they would do because He knows what they are, which 6:3 established at the start of the surah.\n\n'Indeed they are liars' — wa innahum la kadhibun. The same charge as 6:24, where they swore they had never associated anything with Allah. The lying is consistent, and it is consistently about themselves.\n\nThere is a practical test buried in this verse, and it can be applied today. A person who regrets a wrong only when it is discovered has learned about discovery, not about the wrong.\n\nThe way to know the difference is what happens when the pressure lifts. This verse says these people fail that test, and the surah has already shown it: 5:115 warned that seeing does not guarantee believing, and 6:7 showed a sign they could touch being renamed as magic.",
  "29": "'They insisted, \"There is nothing beyond this worldly life and we will never be resurrected.\"' (Quran 6:29). The position underneath everything the surah has described.\n\n'There is nothing beyond this worldly life' — in hiya illa hayatuna al-dunya. This is the belief that produces the behaviour of 6:4, 6:25 and 6:26. If there is nothing after, then signs are irrelevant, mockery costs nothing, and turning others away is not a crime.\n\nThe Quran quotes the same claim at 45:24 — 'There is nothing beyond our worldly life. We die; others are born' — and adds a comment there that applies here too: 'Yet they have no knowledge in support of this claim. They only speculate.'\n\nThat is the flaw being exposed. Denial of the Hereafter is presented as the hard-headed position, the one that sticks to what can be seen. But it is a claim about what does not exist, and no one is in a position to make it.\n\n'They insisted' — wa qalu. They said it, and the surah has shown they will say the opposite when the time comes: 'Oh! If only we could be sent back' (Quran 6:27).\n\nSo the confidence is temporary. Verse 6:30 shows the same people, asked directly, agreeing under oath that it was true all along.\n\nThere is a practical version of this belief that has nothing to do with stated doctrine. Many people affirm the Hereafter and arrange their lives exactly as if 6:29 were correct.\n\nThe verse is describing a way of living before it describes an opinion.",
  "30": "'But if only you could see when they will be detained before their Lord! He will ask ˹them˺, \"Is this ˹Hereafter˺ not the truth?\" They will cry, \"Absolutely, by our Lord!\" He will say, \"Then taste the punishment for your disbelief.\"' (Quran 6:30). The claim of 6:29, put to them.\n\n'If only you could see' — wa law tara. The same opening as 6:27, and the same unfinished sentence.\n\n'Detained before their Lord' — wuqifu 'ala rabbihim. In 6:27 they were detained before the Fire. Here, before their Lord. Two stops, and this is the more serious one.\n\n'Is this not the truth?' — a laysa hadha bi al-haqq. The question quotes their denial back at them. They said 'there is nothing beyond this worldly life'; now they are asked, in that beyond, whether it is real.\n\n'Absolutely, by our Lord!' — bala wa rabbina. They swear again, as they swore in 6:23, but this time to the truth.\n\nThat is the difference between the two oaths, and it is worth pausing on. In 6:23 they swore a lie about themselves. Here they swear a fact, and the fact convicts them.\n\nEverything they denied is now something they affirm under oath. Nothing has changed except that it can no longer be avoided.\n\n'Then taste the punishment for your disbelief' — the word 'taste', dhuqu, is used because tasting is direct knowledge. They wanted evidence they could not dispute; this is it.\n\nVerse 6:8 warned that undeniable proof ends the possibility of choosing. This is what that looks like when it arrives.",
  "31": "'Losers indeed are those who deny the meeting with Allah until the Hour takes them by surprise, then they will cry, \"Woe to us for having ignored this!\" They will bear ˹the burden of˺ their sins on their backs. Evil indeed is their burden!' (Quran 6:31). Loss, defined.\n\n'Deny the meeting with Allah' — kadhdhabu bi liqa'i Allah. Not denial of Allah — denial of the meeting. Many people accept that He exists and live as though there will be no encounter.\n\n'Until the Hour takes them by surprise' — hatta idha ja'athumu al-sa'atu baghtatan. 'Baghtatan' is suddenly, without warning. That is how it arrives for everyone, and the surprise is not a failure of information. Verse 6:2 already said the term was appointed.\n\n'Woe to us for having ignored this' — ya hasratana 'ala ma farratna fiha. 'Hasrah' is regret so deep it becomes grief, and 'tafrit' is negligence — not active hostility, but letting something slide.\n\nThat is a specific and very common failure. Not rejecting, not fighting. Postponing.\n\n'They will bear the burden of their sins on their backs' — yahmiluna awzarahum 'ala zuhurihim. The image is of a load carried, visible, personal. Nobody else's back is available.\n\nDeeds are described in this surah as things acquired — 'ma taksibun' in 6:3 — and what is acquired must be carried.\n\nThe word 'losers' — khasiru — has now appeared three times: 6:12, 6:20 and here. Every time, the loss is their own doing and its object is themselves.",
  "32": "'This worldly life is no more than play and amusement, but far better is the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter for those mindful ˹of Allah˺. Will you not then understand?' (Quran 6:32). The answer to the claim in 6:29.\n\n'Play and amusement' — la'ibun wa lahw. Two words, and they are not synonyms. 'La'ib' is play — activity with no lasting product. 'Lahw' is diversion — what pulls your attention away from what matters.\n\nTogether they describe not a worthless world but a world easily mistaken for the whole story. The Quran repeats the phrase at 29:64 and expands it at 57:20 into 'play, amusement, luxury, mutual boasting, and competition in wealth and children'.\n\nThat list is recognisable. Every item on it is real, absorbing, and finished.\n\n'Far better is the eternal Home of the Hereafter' — wa la al-daru al-akhiratu khayr. The comparison is not between something worthless and something valuable. It is between temporary and lasting.\n\n'For those mindful of Allah' — lilladhina yattaqun. The Home is better for them specifically, because for anyone else it is not a home at all.\n\n'Will you not then understand?' — a fa la ta'qilun. The appeal is to reason, not to fear. Choosing the shorter of two things over the longer is presented as a failure of arithmetic.\n\nThis does not ask anyone to despise the world. The surah is full of rain, crops, stars and cattle given as favours. It asks for a correct estimate of what they are.",
  "33": "'We certainly know that what they say grieves you ˹O Prophet˺. It is not your honesty they question—it is Allah's signs that the wrongdoers deny.' (Quran 6:33). A verse addressed to one man's pain.\n\n'We certainly know' — qad na'lamu. Before anything else, the grief is acknowledged. Not corrected, not minimised. Known.\n\n'What they say grieves you' — la yahzunuka alladhi yaqulun. He was hurt by words. This is the man who endured far worse physically — 'Urwa bin Az-Zubair asked 'Abdullah bin 'Amr for the worst thing the pagans did to the Prophet, and he described 'Uqba bin Abi Mu'ait putting his garment around the Prophet's neck and throttling him violently while he prayed, until Abu Bakr pushed him away saying, 'Do you want to kill a man just because he says, \"My Lord is Allah?\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3856).\n\nAnd what the Quran singles out as his grief is what they said.\n\n'It is not your honesty they question' — fa innahum la yukadhdhibunak. They are not calling you a liar. His personal reputation in Mecca was not actually in dispute; he was known as trustworthy before any of this began.\n\n'It is Allah's signs that the wrongdoers deny' — bi ayati Allahi yajhadun. The verb 'jahada' is to reject something you know to be true.\n\nSo the insult is not aimed at him. It passes through him at a different target, and understanding that is what makes it bearable.\n\nAnyone who has been attacked for saying something true can use this verse the way it was given.",
  "34": "'Indeed, messengers before you were rejected but patiently endured rejection and persecution until Our help came to them. And Allah's promise ˹to help˺ is never broken. And you have already received some of the narratives of these messengers.' (Quran 6:34). The consolation of 6:33, extended.\n\n'Were rejected but patiently endured' — kudhdhibat... fa sabaru. The rejection is stated as fact, and so is the response to it. Sabr here is not passive; it is continuing to deliver while being refused.\n\n'Rejection and persecution' — ma kudhdhibu wa udhu. Two burdens: being disbelieved, and being harmed. The verse names both because they are different injuries.\n\n'Until Our help came to them' — hatta atahum nasruna. The help came. Not immediately, not on their schedule — the word 'until' covers however long it took.\n\n'And Allah's promise is never broken' — wa la mubaddila li kalimati Allah. Nothing alters His words.\n\nThen the last clause, which is easy to skip: 'you have already received some of the narratives of these messengers' — wa laqad ja'aka min naba'i al-mursalin.\n\nThat is the Quran pointing at its own contents as the evidence for what it just said. Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa, 'Isa — the stories were given so they could be used exactly here, at the moment when endurance is required.\n\nThis is why the Quran tells history the way it does. Not as record, but as supply. The accounts of earlier messengers exist so that a later one, grieving in Mecca, has something to hold.",
  "35": "'If you find their denial unbearable, then build—if you can—a tunnel through the earth or stairs to the sky to bring them a ˹more compelling˺ sign. Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all. So do not be one of those ignorant ˹of this fact˺.' (Quran 6:35). A gentle rebuke to the most earnest of intentions.\n\nThe picture is deliberately impossible. Dig a tunnel down; build a staircase up. Go and fetch a sign from somewhere else, if that is what you think is missing.\n\n'If you find their denial unbearable' — wa in kana kabura 'alayka i'raduhum. 'I'rad' is the turning away first named at 6:4. The Prophet finds it hard to bear, which is stated as a fact about him, not a fault.\n\nBut the response the verse addresses is the wish to produce something that would finally convince them. And 6:7 has already settled that: a written revelation they could touch would be called magic.\n\n'Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all' — wa law sha'a Allahu la jama'ahum 'ala al-huda. The point is not that guidance is withheld arbitrarily. It is that forced guidance is not what Allah chose to do, and it was never the messenger's job to supply it.\n\nThe same correction appears at 10:99: 'Had your Lord so willed, all people on earth would have certainly believed, every single one of them! Would you then force people to become believers?'\n\n'So do not be one of those ignorant of this fact' — the ignorance named is not about doctrine. It is about the limits of one's own role, which 5:99 also set: delivery is the duty.\n\nCaring is required. Carrying the outcome is not.",
  "36": "'Only the attentive will respond ˹to your call˺. As for the dead, Allah will raise them up, then to Him they will ˹all˺ be returned.' (Quran 6:36). Who a message can actually reach.\n\n'Only the attentive will respond' — innama yastajibu alladhina yasma'un. Literally: only those who hear respond. Hearing here means listening with the intention of taking something in.\n\n'As for the dead' — wa al-mawta. The people described throughout this surah as veiled, deaf and turning away are now called dead. It is a description of condition, not of body.\n\nThe comparison is exact and it appears elsewhere: 'Nor are the dead and the living equal. Indeed, Allah alone makes whoever He wills hear, but you can never make those in the graves hear your call' (Quran 35:22), and again at 27:80.\n\nA living person can be persuaded. A dead one cannot be reached by argument, however true.\n\nThat sounds like despair, and then the verse turns: 'Allah will raise them up' — yab'athuhumu Allah. What is beyond the messenger is not beyond Allah. Raising the dead is precisely His work.\n\n'Then to Him they will all be returned' — thumma ilayhi yurja'un. The matter is not closed; it is transferred.\n\nRead together with 6:35, the instruction to the Prophet is complete. Do not exhaust yourself trying to force a response. Speak to the living. The dead have an appointment of their own.\n\nThat is not indifference. It is an accurate description of what persuasion can and cannot do.",
  "37": "'They ask, \"Why has no ˹other˺ sign been sent down to him from his Lord?\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Allah certainly has the power to send down a sign\"—though most of them do not know.' (Quran 6:37). The demand for a sign, made again.\n\nThis is the third time the surah has dealt with it: 6:8 asked for an angel, 6:25 showed a sign being renamed as ancient fables, and now this.\n\n'Allah certainly has the power to send down a sign' — inna Allaha qadirun 'ala an yunazzila ayah. The answer concedes everything about ability and says nothing about willingness.\n\nThat is precise. The question implied that no sign had come because none could. The reply removes that inference and leaves the demand unanswered, because it was never a question.\n\n'Though most of them do not know' — wa lakinna aktharahum la ya'lamun. What they do not know is stated in 6:8: sending the sign they want would end the reprieve they are living in.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, notes that Allah's refusal to send the demanded sign is itself a mercy, because earlier peoples who received exactly what they asked for were destroyed when they rejected it. The Quran says this directly at 17:59: 'Nothing keeps Us from sending the demanded signs except that they had already been denied by earlier peoples.'\n\nAnd there is something else in the reply. The Prophet is not made to argue, apologise or produce. He states a fact about Allah's power and stops.\n\nThe next verse then produces a sign anyway — one that was already there, and had been all along.",
  "38": "'All living beings roaming the earth and winged birds soaring in the sky are communities like yourselves. We have left nothing out of the Record. Then to their Lord they will be gathered all together.' (Quran 6:38). The answer to the demand for a sign, delivered as an observation.\n\nThey asked for something extraordinary. The verse points at animals.\n\n'Communities like yourselves' — umamun amthalukum. 'Ummah' is the word used for the community of believers. It is applied here to every creature that walks and every bird that flies.\n\nThat is a remarkable statement and the Quran does not explain it. It leaves the reader with the fact that other species have their own collective existence, their own order, and standing before Allah.\n\n'We have left nothing out of the Record' — ma farratna fi al-kitabi min shay'. The word 'farratna' is the same one used at 6:31 for human negligence. Nothing has been let slide in the Record.\n\n'Then to their Lord they will be gathered' — thumma ila rabbihim yuhsharun. The gathering announced in 6:22 for human beings includes them.\n\nThe practical consequence was drawn by the Prophet himself. Shaddad b. Aws reported him saying: 'Verily Allah has enjoined goodness to everything; so when you kill, kill in a good way and when you slaughter, slaughter in a good way. So every one of you should sharpen his knife, and let the slaughtered animal die comfortably' (Sahih Muslim 1955a).\n\nA surah named 'the Cattle' teaches that cattle are communities, and that how you treat them is recorded.",
  "39": "'Those who deny Our signs are ˹wilfully˺ deaf and dumb—lost in darkness. Allah leaves whoever He wills to stray and guides whoever He wills to the Straight Way.' (Quran 6:39). The condition described in one line, and then its cause.\n\n'Deaf and dumb' — summun wa bukmun. Two faculties, and the second is unexpected. Deafness explains why they do not receive. Dumbness explains why they do not ask.\n\nThat is the more telling of the two. A person who cannot hear might still enquire. These do neither. They have stopped taking in and stopped seeking.\n\n'Lost in darkness' — fi al-zulumat. The plural, exactly as in the first verse of the surah: 'made darkness and light' (Quran 6:1), where the darknesses were many and the light was one.\n\n'Allah leaves whoever He wills to stray and guides whoever He wills' — man yasha'i Allahu yudlilhu wa man yasha' yaj'alhu 'ala siratin mustaqim.\n\nThis is the verse people find hardest, and it should be read with the rest of the surah rather than alone. Verse 6:4 described people turning away from every sign. Verse 6:25 described veils that fell after that turning. Verse 6:28 said that even a second chance would change nothing.\n\nThe Quran presents the choice and the outcome together, and does not resolve how they fit. What it never does is let anyone use this verse as an excuse. Every description of loss in this surah — 6:12, 6:20, 6:26, 6:31 — says the person did it to himself.\n\nBoth facts stand. The verse asks you to hold them.",
  "40": "'Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Imagine if you were overwhelmed by Allah's torment or the Hour—would you call upon any other than Allah ˹for help˺? ˹Answer me˺ if your claims are true!\"' (Quran 6:40). A question about what a person does in genuine emergency.\n\nThe argument shifts here. Verses 6:1 to 6:39 argued from creation, history, and the coming Day. This argues from the reader's own behaviour under pressure.\n\n'Would you call upon any other than Allah' — a ghayra Allahi tad'un. The question is not what you believe. It is who you would shout for.\n\nThat test is chosen because it bypasses everything a person can construct. Doctrines can be defended indefinitely. What comes out of someone in the first second of terror cannot.\n\n'If your claims are true' — in kuntum sadiqin. They claimed their gods had standing. The verse asks them to confirm that claim in the one situation where the answer is known to everyone, including them.\n\nNobody replies, because the answer is obvious and it is against them.\n\nThis is the same instinct the surah has already exposed from the other direction. In 6:23 they will swear on the Day of Judgment that they were never polytheists — the truth surfacing in extremity there too, though as a lie about the past rather than an admission.\n\nUnder enough pressure, a person's actual belief becomes visible to himself.\n\nThe verse invites them to notice it now, while noticing is still worth something.",
  "41": "'No! He is the only One you would call. And if He willed, He could remove the affliction that made you invoke Him. Only then will you forget whatever you associate with Him ˹in worship˺.' (Quran 6:41). The answer to 6:40, supplied for them.\n\n'He is the only One you would call' — bal iyyahu tad'un. Stated flatly, as something not in dispute.\n\nThe Quran returns to this observation repeatedly, and usually at sea. 'If they happen to be aboard a ship caught in a storm, they cry out to Allah alone in sincere devotion. But as soon as He delivers them safely to shore, they associate others with Him once again' (Quran 29:65). The same at 31:32 and 17:67.\n\nWhy the sea? Because it removes every intermediate solution. On land there is always something else to try.\n\n'You will forget whatever you associate with Him' — wa tansawna ma tushrikun. The word is 'forget'. Not renounce, not reject — simply drop, as one drops what was never load-bearing.\n\nThat is the exact test. Under real weight, some things carry and some are quietly let go.\n\n'If He willed, He could remove the affliction' — the verse is careful. The rescue is not guaranteed by the sincerity of the plea; it remains His decision, as 6:17 established.\n\nBut the sincerity itself is the evidence being collected. In the moment of drowning, the polytheist prays like a Muslim.\n\nWhich raises the only remaining question, and the surah has been asking it all along: why wait for the water?",
  "42": "'Indeed, We have sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ to other people who We put through suffering and adversity ˹for their denial˺, so perhaps they would be humbled.' (Quran 6:42). Hardship described as an instrument.\n\n'Suffering and adversity' — al-ba'sa'i wa al-darra'. The pair covers hardship from outside — poverty, war, drought — and hardship in the body: illness, pain.\n\n'So perhaps they would be humbled' — la'allahum yatadarra'un. 'Tadarru'' is to lower yourself and plead. The purpose named is not punishment but the breaking of self-sufficiency.\n\nThat connects directly to 6:41. People call on Allah when the ship is going down. This verse says the storm was sent so they would.\n\nNote that this is described as something done to communities who received messengers. The message came first, was refused, and then the difficulty followed as a second summons.\n\nThe Quran states the same sequence at 7:94: 'Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its disbelieving people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled' — and then 7:95 describes what happened when the hardship was lifted, which is what 6:44 will describe here.\n\nThere is a reading of one's own difficulties available in this, and it should be handled carefully. The verse does not license anyone to interpret another person's suffering. Believers are tested too, and 2:214 says so explicitly.\n\nWhat it offers is a question to ask about your own hard seasons: what did they make me do?",
  "43": "'Why did they not humble themselves when We made them suffer? Instead, their hearts were hardened, and Satan made their misdeeds appealing to them.' (Quran 6:43). What happened when the hardship of 6:42 arrived.\n\n'Why did they not humble themselves' — fa law la idh ja'ahum ba'suna tadarra'u. The purpose of the affliction was stated in the previous verse: 'so perhaps they would be humbled.' It did not work.\n\n'Their hearts were hardened' — wa lakin qasat qulubuhum. 'Qaswah' is hardness in the sense of a stone — something that cannot be worked or shaped.\n\nThis is the second thing hardship can do. It softens or it hardens, and which one it does is not determined by the hardship. Two people lose the same thing and one turns to Allah while the other decides there is nobody there.\n\n'Satan made their misdeeds appealing to them' — wa zayyana lahumu al-shaytanu ma kanu ya'malun. 'Tazyin' is decoration — making something look attractive from outside.\n\nNotice when this happens. Not before the suffering, but after it, at the moment the person could have changed direction. That is when the case for continuing gets dressed up.\n\nThe Quran is describing a mechanism most people can recognise. A warning arrives, is felt, and then the mind gets to work explaining why nothing needs to change.\n\n'Their misdeeds' — the very thing that brought the difficulty is what now looks good to them.\n\nAnd the next verse describes what follows when the warnings stop coming.",
  "44": "'When they became oblivious to warnings, We showered them with everything they desired. But just as they became prideful of what they were given, We seized them by surprise, then they instantly fell into despair!' (Quran 6:44). The most frightening verse in the surah.\n\n'We showered them with everything they desired' — fatahna 'alayhim abwaba kulli shay'. We opened upon them the doors of everything.\n\nThat sentence describes a punishment. It is placed after 6:43, where hardship failed to humble them, and it says the hardship was withdrawn and replaced with abundance.\n\nThe Quran names this process directly at 7:182: 'As for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend' — istidraj, being led on by degrees. And at 23:55–56: 'Do they think, since We provide them with wealth and children, that We hasten to honour them with all kinds of good? No! They are not aware.'\n\nSo difficulty was the mercy. Its removal was the sentence.\n\n'Just as they became prideful of what they were given' — hatta idha farihu bima utu. 'Farah' here is delighted self-congratulation, treating the gift as a verdict in their favour.\n\n'We seized them by surprise' — akhadhnahum baghtatan. The same word used in 6:31 for the Hour.\n\nThis verse should unsettle anyone who reads their own comfort as approval. Prosperity proves nothing about your standing. Verse 6:6 already said the destroyed peoples were 'more established in the land than you'.\n\nThe useful question is not how well things are going. It is what the good season is doing to you.",
  "45": "'So the wrongdoers were utterly uprooted. And all praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds.' (Quran 6:45). The end of the sequence that began at 6:42.\n\n'Utterly uprooted' — fa quti'a dabiru al-qawmi alladhina zalamu. 'Dabir' is the last part, the hindmost. To cut it off is to leave no remainder, no tail of survivors.\n\nThe sequence is now complete: messengers sent (6:42), hardship applied (6:42), hearts hardened (6:43), abundance given (6:44), seizure (6:44), and here the erasure.\n\n'And all praise is for Allah' — wa al-hamdu lillahi rabbi al-'alamin.\n\nThat closing is startling, and it is meant to be considered rather than passed over. Praise, immediately after the destruction of a people.\n\nIt is the same phrase the surah opened with in 6:1, where it was attached to creation. Here it is attached to judgment, and the pairing is the point. The One praised for making is the same One praised for ending, and the second is not a lapse from the first.\n\n'Lord of all worlds' — rabbi al-'alamin. Not the Lord of one side. The people destroyed were also His, which is why 6:18 called them ''ibadih', His servants, even while describing His power over them.\n\nJustice completed is a thing to be grateful for. That is a hard idea in a culture that treats consequence as regrettable, and the verse states it without softening.\n\nThe wrongdoing described was not private. It ruined others, as 6:26 said.",
  "46": "'Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Imagine if Allah were to take away your hearing or sight, or seal your hearts—who else other than Allah could restore it?\" See ˹O Prophet˺ how We vary the signs, yet they still turn away.' (Quran 6:46). A question about faculties nobody thinks about.\n\nThe argument of 6:40 was about emergencies. This one is about equipment.\n\n'Your hearing or sight' — sam'akum wa absarakum. Not wealth, not safety. The two senses through which every sign in this surah has to arrive.\n\n'Or seal your hearts' — wa khatama 'ala qulubikum. The third item is the one that matters most, and it is the one described in 6:25 as already sealed for some.\n\n'Who else other than Allah could restore it?' — man ilahun ghayru Allahi ya'tikum bih. Nobody claims their gods could. The question is unanswerable in the same way 6:40 was, and for the same reason: everyone knows.\n\nWhat makes this argument distinctive is where it points. Hearing and sight are not gifts people notice, because they have never been without them. They are assumed, the way a floor is assumed.\n\nThe verse asks the reader to consider them as things that were given and could be withdrawn.\n\n'See how We vary the signs' — nusarrifu al-ayat. 'Tasrif' is turning something over, presenting it from different angles. The surah has now argued from creation, history, ruins, animals, emergencies, and now the senses.\n\n'Yet they still turn away' — thumma hum yasdifun. Which 6:4 predicted before the first argument was made.",
  "47": "'Ask, \"Imagine if Allah's punishment were to overwhelm you with or without warning—who would be destroyed other than the wrongdoers?\"' (Quran 6:47). The third question in this series, and the one about fairness.\n\n'With or without warning' — baghtatan aw jahrah. Suddenly, or openly — arriving unannounced, or visibly approaching.\n\nBoth are covered because people imagine they would handle one of them differently. Given warning, they would repent. The verse does not argue with that; it simply includes both cases and moves to the real question.\n\n'Who would be destroyed other than the wrongdoers?' — hal yuhlaku illa al-qawmu al-zalimun.\n\nThat is the answer to an objection nobody has voiced yet. Verse 6:45 described a people 'utterly uprooted', and the natural discomfort is about indiscriminate destruction.\n\nThe verse addresses it directly: the destruction lands on the wrongdoers. No one else is swept up.\n\nThis connects to 6:31, where the losers 'bear the burden of their sins on their backs'. Consequence in this surah is always personal and always earned.\n\nThere is also something in the phrasing worth noticing. The question is put to the audience as a hypothetical they must answer themselves. They are being asked to work out where they would stand.\n\nThat is the method of this whole passage — 6:40, 6:46, and now this. Three questions, none of them answered by the Prophet, all of them designed so that the listener supplies the answer and hears himself do it.",
  "48": "'We have sent messengers only as deliverers of good news and as warners. Whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.' (Quran 6:48). The job description, after three verses of hard questions.\n\n'Only as deliverers of good news and as warners' — illa mubashshirina wa mundhirin. Two functions and no third. Not enforcers, not judges, not producers of miracles on demand.\n\nThis is the answer running through the surah to every demand made of the Prophet: bring an angel (6:8), bring another sign (6:37), do something about their refusal (6:35). None of it is in the description.\n\n'Whoever believes and does good' — fa man amana wa aslah. Belief and repair. 'Islah' is to put right what is broken, which is more active than merely doing good deeds.\n\n'There will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' — fa la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun. The formula appears throughout the Quran, at 2:262 among many places.\n\nLook at the two things removed. Fear points forward, grief points back. What is promised is not a life without difficulty but freedom from the two forms of suffering that live in the mind rather than in events.\n\nThat promise is placed here deliberately. Verses 6:42 to 6:47 have been about affliction, hardened hearts, sudden seizure and destruction. The reader has been shown a great deal to be afraid of.\n\nAnd the answer to all of it is two conditions, stated in three words.",
  "49": "'But those who deny Our signs will be afflicted with punishment for their rebelliousness.' (Quran 6:49). The other half of 6:48, as short as the promise was.\n\nThe two verses are built to be read as a pair. Belief and repair on one side; denial and rebellion on the other. Nothing in between is described, because the passage is drawing a line rather than mapping a territory.\n\n'For their rebelliousness' — bima kanu yafsuqun. 'Fisq' is stepping outside the boundary, breaking away.\n\nNote that the punishment is attached to the rebellion rather than to the denial by itself. The two are linked: denying the signs is what licenses the stepping out.\n\nThis connects to how the surah has described these people. They were not confused. Verse 6:33 said 'it is Allah's signs that the wrongdoers deny' — using 'jahada', rejection of what is known. Verse 6:20 said they recognised the truth 'as they recognize their own children'.\n\n'Will be afflicted' — yamassuhumu al-'adhab. The verb is the same one used in 6:17: 'If Allah touches you with harm.' The word for a touch, used for both a passing difficulty and the final consequence.\n\nThere is a restraint in this verse that is easy to miss. It states the outcome in a single clause and does not elaborate. The surah has already described the scene at length in 6:27 and 6:30.\n\nHere it is simply the balance to the promise before it. Two outcomes, two sentences, and the reader is left to notice which one describes him.",
  "50": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"I do not say to you that I possess Allah's treasuries or know the unseen, nor do I claim to be an angel. I only follow what is revealed to me.\"...' (Quran 6:50). Three denials, and each one refuses a claim he was never making.\n\n'Allah's treasuries' — khaza'ina Allah. Some had asked why he was not wealthy, why the message did not come with visible resources.\n\n'Nor know the unseen' — wa la a'lamu al-ghayb. He is not a fortune-teller. The Quran has him say this even more plainly at 7:188: 'If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me.'\n\n'Nor do I claim to be an angel' — this answers 6:8 and 6:9 directly, where the demand was for an angel messenger.\n\nThe same three denials, in the same order, are put in the mouth of Nuh at 11:31. It is the standing answer of prophets to inflated expectations.\n\n'I only follow what is revealed to me' — in attabi'u illa ma yuha ilayy. He is a follower of the revelation, not its author and not above it. The same position 6:14 put him in: 'the first to submit'.\n\n'Are those blind to the truth equal to those who can see?' — the question that closes the verse shifts the ground entirely. They wanted credentials. He offers a comparison instead.\n\nSight is the only qualification at issue, and it belongs to the listener, not the speaker.\n\nThe claim being made is small. What is asked of the audience is not.",
  "51": "'Warn with this ˹Quran˺ those who are awed by the prospect of being gathered before their Lord—when they will have no protector or intercessor besides Him—so perhaps they will be mindful ˹of Him˺.' (Quran 6:51). Who the warning is actually for.\n\n'Those who are awed by the prospect of being gathered' — alladhina yakhafuna an yuhsharu ila rabbihim. The audience is defined by an existing fear.\n\nThat is a narrower target than one might expect. The warning is aimed at people who already suspect it is true, not at those who deny it outright.\n\nIt follows what 6:36 established: 'Only the attentive will respond.' A warning works on someone who can be alarmed. It bounces off everyone else.\n\n'No protector or intercessor besides Him' — laysa lahum min dunihi waliyyun wa la shafi'. This removes the exact arrangement the Meccans relied on. Their idols were understood as intermediaries who would speak for them.\n\nThe Quran does not deny that intercession exists; it locates the permission. 'Who could possibly intercede with Him without His permission?' (Quran 2:255), and 'All intercession belongs to Allah alone' (Quran 39:44).\n\n'So perhaps they will be mindful' — la'allahum yattaqun. Taqwa is the goal, and it grows out of the fear the verse began with.\n\nThere is guidance here for anyone who carries a message. Direct it at those who are already uneasy. The person with a flicker of concern is reachable; the one who has decided nothing is coming has been described in this surah for fifty verses.",
  "52": "'˹O Prophet!˺ Do not dismiss those ˹poor believers˺ who invoke their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure...' (Quran 6:52). An instruction with a specific occasion behind it.\n\nSa'd bin Abi Waqqas reported: 'This verse was revealed in relation to six persons and I and Ibn Mas'ud were amongst them. The polytheists said to him: Do not keep such persons near you. It was upon this that this verse was revealed' (Sahih Muslim 2413a).\n\nThat is the situation. The Meccan leaders were willing to sit and listen, on condition that the poor Companions were sent away first.\n\n'Do not dismiss' — wa la tatrud. Do not drive them off.\n\n'Seeking His pleasure' — yuriduna wajhah. Wanting His face. That is their only credential, and the verse treats it as the only one that counts.\n\n'You are not accountable for them whatsoever, nor are they accountable for you' — the exchange being proposed was a trade, and the verse refuses it by cancelling the accounting on both sides.\n\n'Or you will be one of the wrongdoers' — fa takuna mina al-zalimin. The warning is severe, and it is addressed to the Prophet, over a matter of who is allowed to sit in a gathering.\n\nThe same instruction, with a similar occasion, is at 18:28: 'And patiently stick with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure.'\n\nA movement that trades its poor members for influential ones has already lost the thing it was recruiting for.",
  "53": "'In this way We have tested some by means of others, so those ˹disbelievers˺ may say, \"Has Allah favoured these ˹poor believers˺ out of all of us?\" Does Allah not best recognize the grateful?' (Quran 6:53). Why the arrangement of 6:52 exists.\n\n'We have tested some by means of others' — fatanna ba'dahum bi ba'd. Each group is a test for the other, and the test is mutual.\n\nThe rich are tested by the poor: can they accept a truth that arrived through people beneath them socially? The poor are tested by the rich: can they hold their position while being looked down on?\n\n'Has Allah favoured these out of all of us?' — a ha'ula'i manna Allahu 'alayhim min bayanina. The objection quoted is one of status. Of all people, these?\n\nThat question contains its own answer, which the verse then supplies: 'Does Allah not best recognize the grateful?' — a laysa Allahu bi a'lama bi al-shakirin.\n\nHe selects by gratitude, not by standing. And the objectors, in asking the question, revealed exactly why they were not selected.\n\nThis is the principle stated in general terms at 49:13: 'the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most righteous among you.'\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'A Muslim is the brother of a Muslim. He neither oppresses him nor humiliates him nor looks down upon him. The piety is here' — and while saying so he pointed towards his chest three times — 'It is a serious evil for a Muslim that he should look down upon his brother Muslim' (Sahih Muslim 2564a).",
  "54": "'When the believers in Our revelations come to you, say, \"Peace be upon you! Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful...\"' (Quran 6:54). The opposite of dismissal.\n\nVerse 6:52 forbade sending them away. This tells the Prophet what to do instead, and it is more than tolerance.\n\n'Say, \"Peace be upon you!\"' — fa qul salamun 'alaykum. He greets them first. The greeting comes from him to them, which reverses the social order the Meccan leaders were trying to enforce.\n\n'Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful' — kataba rabbukum 'ala nafsihi al-rahmah. The same phrase as 6:12, repeated here word for word, and this time addressed to the poor believers personally: your Lord.\n\nThen the reason it is said to them: 'Whoever among you commits evil ignorantly or recklessly then repents afterwards and mends their ways, then Allah is truly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.'\n\n'Ignorantly' — bi jahalah. Not necessarily lacking information. Ibn Kathir, commenting on the parallel at 4:17, takes 'jahalah' to include anyone who acts against what he knows, since the act itself is a kind of ignorance.\n\n'Then repents afterwards and mends their ways' — wa aslaha. Repair again, as in 6:48. Repentance is not complete as a feeling.\n\nNotice who receives this reassurance. Not the powerful, who were being courted. The poor believers already in the room, who apparently needed to hear that their mistakes were forgivable.",
  "55": "'This is how We make Our signs clear, so the way of the wicked may become distinct.' (Quran 6:55). The purpose of everything the surah has been doing.\n\n'This is how We make Our signs clear' — wa kadhalika nufassilu al-ayat. 'Tafsil' is to separate into parts, to set out in detail. The word for detailed explanation shares its root with the word for separation, and both meanings are working here.\n\n'So the way of the wicked may become distinct' — wa li tastabina sabilu al-mujrimin. 'Tastabina' is to become clearly visible, to stand out.\n\nConsider what the verse says the clarity is for. Not so that everyone will follow the right path — the surah has been honest that many will not. So that the wrong path becomes recognisable.\n\nThat is a different kind of achievement, and a necessary one. Before revelation arrives, the two roads look similar. Everyone is doing what their fathers did, and nobody has a way to tell.\n\nDetailed explanation separates them. Afterwards, a person walking the wrong way knows he is, which is the condition 6:20 described: recognising the truth as one recognises one's own children.\n\n'Sabil' — a path, a way. Not a list of offences but a direction, because that is how the surah has been describing wrongdoing throughout: not isolated acts, but a road with a destination shown in 6:27 and 6:30.\n\nClarity is the gift. What is done with it is the test, and 6:1 named the two outcomes at the very start: darkness and light.",
  "56": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"I have been forbidden to worship those you invoke besides Allah.\" Say, \"I will not follow your desires, for I then would certainly be astray and not one of those ˹rightly˺ guided.\"' (Quran 6:56). A refusal, stated twice.\n\nTwo 'say' commands in one verse. The first refuses the act; the second refuses the reasoning behind the request.\n\n'I have been forbidden' — inni nuhitu. He does not say 'I refuse' or 'I disagree'. He reports an instruction he is under, which is the same position taken in 6:14 and 6:50.\n\nThat framing removes the possibility of negotiation. A person acting on his own judgement can be argued with. A person under orders cannot.\n\n'I will not follow your desires' — la attabi'u ahwa'akum. The word is 'ahwa'' — desires, inclinations. The Meccans had presented their position as ancestral tradition and social good sense. The verse renames it.\n\nThat renaming is one of the Quran's most consistent moves, and it appeared repeatedly in the previous surah — at 5:49 and 5:70, where rejection was traced to 'hawa' rather than to ignorance.\n\n'I then would certainly be astray' — qad dalaltu idhan. He states the consequence for himself, exactly as 6:15 did: 'I truly fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the torment of a tremendous Day.'\n\nThe offer on the table was influence in exchange for compromise. The answer given is not defiance. It is a man explaining, calmly, that he cannot afford the trade.",
  "57": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Indeed, I stand on a clear proof from my Lord—yet you have denied it. That ˹torment˺ you seek to hasten is not within my power. It is only Allah Who decides ˹its time˺. He declares the truth. And He is the Best of Judges.\"' (Quran 6:57). A reply to people daring him to produce the punishment.\n\n'I stand on a clear proof from my Lord' — 'ala bayyinatin min rabbi. 'Bayyinah' is evidence that makes something plain. He has it; they have denied it. Both halves are stated in one breath.\n\n'That torment you seek to hasten' — ma tasta'jiluna bih. They had begun taunting him: if this punishment is real, bring it.\n\n'Is not within my power' — ma 'indi. He does not have it. The same limit as 6:50, where he denied holding Allah's treasuries or knowing the unseen.\n\nThis is worth noticing because a person under mockery is tempted to imply he has more authority than he does. The answer refuses that comfort.\n\n'It is only Allah Who decides' — in al-hukmu illa lillah. Judgment is His.\n\n'He declares the truth' — yaqussu al-haqq. He recounts what is true. Not merely rules, but narrates the actual state of things.\n\n'And He is the Best of Judges' — khayru al-fasilin. 'Fasl' is separation — the one who separates right from wrong. The same root as 'tafsil' in 6:55, where the signs were made distinct.\n\nThey asked for a deadline. They are told the timing is held by Someone who does not take requests.",
  "58": "'Say ˹also˺, \"If what you seek to hasten were within my power, the matter between us would have already been settled. But Allah knows the wrongdoers best.\"' (Quran 6:58). The honest admission behind the reply of 6:57.\n\n'The matter between us would have already been settled' — la qudiya al-amru bayni wa baynakum. If it were up to him, this would be over.\n\nThat is a remarkable thing to have a prophet say. It admits an impulse. Faced with years of mockery, throttling, boycott and the ridicule of his message, he would have ended it.\n\nAnd the verse presents that as precisely the reason such power is not given to human beings. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that Allah withheld the timing of punishment from the Prophet as a mercy — both to the deniers, who were given more time, and to him.\n\n'But Allah knows the wrongdoers best' — wa Allahu a'lamu bi al-zalimin. Better than the Prophet does. His judgement of who deserves what is not the standard.\n\nThe Quran states the general principle at 16:61: 'If Allah were to punish people immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not have left a single living being on earth. But He delays them for an appointed term.'\n\nThat delay is the space in which every conversion in history happened, including 'Umar's and Khalid's.\n\nThe verse also quietly reassures anyone who has ever wished a swift end on people who wronged them. The wish is human. Being denied the power to act on it is the mercy.",
  "59": "'With Him are the keys of the unseen—no one knows them except Him. And He knows what is in the land and sea. Not even a leaf falls without His knowledge, nor a grain in the darkness of the earth or anything—green or dry—but is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record.' (Quran 6:59). Knowledge described from the largest scale to the smallest.\n\n'The keys of the unseen' — mafatihu al-ghayb. Keys, because the unseen is a locked thing and access is held by one party.\n\nThe Quran lists some of what is behind those doors at 31:34: the Hour, the rain, what is in the wombs, what a soul will earn tomorrow, and the land in which a person will die.\n\nThen the verse moves from the unseen to the visible, and it keeps narrowing. Land and sea. Then a single falling leaf. Then a grain buried in soil.\n\n'Not even a leaf falls' — wa ma tasqutu min waraqatin illa ya'lamuha. Consider the scale. Every leaf, on every tree, in every season, for the whole history of the earth. Each one individually known as it drops.\n\n'Nor a grain in the darkness of the earth' — fi zulumati al-ard. In the plural darknesses again, as in 6:1 and 6:39. Buried, invisible, unobserved by anyone.\n\n'Or anything—green or dry' — wa la ratbin wa la yabis. Living or dead. The category is made deliberately exhaustive.\n\n'But is written in a perfect Record' — fi kitabin mubin. The same Record named at 6:38, where nothing had been left out.\n\nNothing is too small to be noticed. Which is either the most frightening sentence in the surah or the most comforting, depending entirely on what you have been doing when nobody was watching.",
  "60": "'He is the One Who calls back your souls by night and knows what you do by day, then revives you daily to complete your appointed term. To Him is your ˹ultimate˺ return, then He will inform you of what you used to do.' (Quran 6:60). Sleep described as a small death.\n\n'Calls back your souls by night' — yatawaffakum bi al-layl. The verb 'tawaffa' is the same one the Quran uses for death, and it was used of 'Isa at 5:117: 'when You took me'.\n\nSo sleep is presented as a nightly instance of the thing everyone fears. It happens to every person, every night, and no one is alarmed by it.\n\nThe Quran makes the connection explicit at 39:42: 'Allah calls back the souls of people upon their death as well as the souls of the living during their sleep. Then He keeps those for whom He has ordained death, and releases the others until their appointed time.'\n\n'Then revives you daily' — thumma yab'athukum fih. The verb 'ba'atha' is the word for resurrection.\n\nThat is the argument, made without stating it. Every morning is a small resurrection, performed by the One who is being told He cannot perform the large one.\n\n'To complete your appointed term' — li yuqda ajalun musamma. The term set in 6:2, running down one waking at a time.\n\n'Then He will inform you of what you used to do' — thumma yunabbi'ukum bima kuntum ta'malun. The same closing as 5:105.\n\nWaking up is a loan being extended. The verse asks the reader to notice the transaction he sleeps through every night.",
  "61": "'He reigns supreme over all of His creation, and sends recording-angels, watching over you. When death comes to any of you, Our angels take their soul, never neglecting this duty.' (Quran 6:61). The system behind the accounting of 6:60.\n\n'He reigns supreme over all of His creation' — wa huwa al-qahiru fawqa 'ibadih. The exact words of 6:18, repeated here.\n\n'And sends recording-angels, watching over you' — wa yursilu 'alaykum hafazah. 'Hafazah' are guardians who preserve — here, preservers of the record. The Quran describes them at 82:10–11 as 'vigilant, honourable angels, recording everything', and at 50:18: 'not a word does a person utter without having a vigilant observer ready to write it down.'\n\nVerse 6:59 said everything is in the Record. This says how it gets there.\n\n'When death comes to any of you, Our angels take their soul' — tawaffathu rusuluna. The same verb used for sleep in the previous verse, now for the real thing.\n\n'Never neglecting this duty' — wa hum la yufarritun. 'Tafrit' — negligence, letting something slide. The same word used at 6:31 for the human failure that produces the cry 'Woe to us for having ignored this!'\n\nThat repetition is pointed. Human beings are negligent about their term; the angels handling it are not. Nobody is missed, nobody is late, nobody's file is misplaced.\n\nThe comfort and the warning are the same fact. You are being attended to, continuously, by beings who do not lapse.",
  "62": "'Then they are ˹all˺ returned to Allah— their True Master. Judgment is His ˹alone˺. And He is the Swiftest Reckoner.' (Quran 6:62). Where the sequence of 6:60 and 6:61 ends.\n\n'Their True Master' — mawlahumu al-haqq. 'Mawla' is the patron, the one to whom a person belongs and to whom he turns. 'Al-Haqq' — the real one, as against every other claimant.\n\nThat phrasing settles a question the surah has raised repeatedly. People had other patrons: idols, ancestors, tribal standing. All of them were arrangements. This one is a fact.\n\n'Judgment is His alone' — a la lahu al-hukm. The same statement as 6:57, and it is placed here because judgment is what the return is for.\n\n'And He is the Swiftest Reckoner' — asra'u al-hasibin. Reckoning is what an accountant does — adding up a record.\n\nThe speed is the striking detail. A lifetime of deeds, for every person who ever lived, and the calculation is instantaneous. Verse 6:59 explains why: nothing has to be gathered, because nothing was ever missed.\n\nThere is no investigation on that Day. There is only a total.\n\nNotice also what this does to the delay discussed in 6:57 and 6:58. They wanted the punishment hastened, and were told the timing is not theirs. The reckoning itself, when it comes, will not be slow.\n\nWhat is slow is the mercy that holds it back.",
  "63": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Who rescues you from the darkest times on land and at sea? He ˹alone˺ you call upon with humility, openly and secretly: \"If You rescue us from this, we will be ever grateful.\"\"' (Quran 6:63). The argument of 6:40 and 6:41, now with the words of the prayer included.\n\n'The darkest times' — zulumati al-barri wa al-bahr. Darknesses again, plural, and now they are situations rather than states of the heart.\n\n'Openly and secretly' — tadarru'an wa khufyah. Two ways of praying: out loud in the pleading of a crowd, and silently, where nobody can hear what you are promising.\n\nThe second is the one that matters for the argument. In the private version there is no audience to perform for.\n\n'If You rescue us from this, we will be ever grateful' — la'in anjana min hadhihi la nakunanna mina al-shakirin. The Quran quotes the same vow at 10:22, also made at sea.\n\nEveryone who has been genuinely frightened has made this bargain. That is why the verse quotes it rather than describing it — the reader is meant to recognise his own words.\n\nAnd the vow is specific. Not 'we will believe' but 'we will be grateful'. Gratitude was the missing thing all along, which is why 6:53 identified the grateful as the ones Allah recognises.\n\nThe question 'who rescues you' is left unanswered again, as in 6:40 and 6:46. Three times now the surah has asked something whose answer the audience knows and will not say.",
  "64": "'Say, \"˹Only˺ Allah rescues you from this and any other distress, yet you associate others with Him ˹in worship˺.\"' (Quran 6:64). The answer to 6:63, supplied because they would not supply it.\n\n'Allah rescues you from this and any other distress' — Allahu yunajjikum minha wa min kulli karb. 'Karb' is anguish — distress that grips.\n\nThe verse extends the claim beyond the storm. Not only the dramatic rescue at sea, but every difficulty a person has ever come out of.\n\nThat extension is the real argument. People concede the extraordinary rescue and forget the ordinary ones — the illness that passed, the debt that was covered, the accident that did not happen. All of it from the same source, none of it acknowledged.\n\n'Yet you associate others with Him' — thumma antum tushrikun. The word 'thumma' — then, afterwards — carries the whole weight. First the rescue, then the return to the idols.\n\nVerse 6:41 described this as forgetting: 'Only then will you forget whatever you associate with Him.' Under the water they forget the idols; on the shore they forget the rescue.\n\nWhichever direction the forgetting runs, it always favours the same conclusion.\n\nThe surah is building a case here that does not depend on scripture or history at all. It relies entirely on what the listener has already lived through. Verse 6:11 said go and look at ruins; this says look at your own life.\n\nBoth are evidence, and neither requires an angel.",
  "65": "'Say, \"He ˹alone˺ has the power to unleash upon you a torment from above or below you or split you into ˹conflicting˺ factions and make you taste the violence of one another.\" See how We vary the signs, so perhaps they will comprehend.' (Quran 6:65). Three possibilities, and the Prophet's reaction to each.\n\nJabir reported: 'When this Verse was revealed: \"Say: He has power to send torment on you from above,\" Allah's Messenger said, \"O Allah! I seek refuge with Your Face.\" And when the verse: \"or send torment from below your feet\" was revealed, Allah's Messenger said, \"I seek refuge with Your Face.\"' Then when the third came — splitting into factions and tasting each other's violence — 'Allah's Messenger said, \"This is lighter (or, this is easier)\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 4628).\n\nHe sought refuge from the first two and did not seek refuge from the third.\n\nThat detail has been discussed by commentators ever since, because the third is the one that actually happened, repeatedly, to this community. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, records that the two supplications were answered and the third was not.\n\n'Split you into conflicting factions' — yalbisakum shiya'an. 'Shiya'' are parties, sects, factions. The verb 'labasa' is the same one used in 6:9 for confusion.\n\n'Make you taste the violence of one another' — wa yudhiqa ba'dakum ba'sa ba'd.\n\nThe Prophet called it lighter than fire from the sky. It is still a punishment, and it is the one this community was left with.\n\n'See how We vary the signs' — the same phrase as 6:46.",
  "66": "'Still your people ˹O Prophet˺ have rejected this ˹Quran˺, although it is the truth. Say, \"I am not a keeper over you.\"' (Quran 6:66). Rejection acknowledged, and responsibility returned.\n\n'Your people' — qawmuk. Not strangers. The people he grew up among, who had called him trustworthy before any of this.\n\n'Although it is the truth' — wa huwa al-haqq. The verse states the fact of the rejection and the fact of the truth in the same sentence, without treating the first as evidence against the second.\n\nThat is a habit worth learning. Being disbelieved by people who know you is painful precisely because it feels like data.\n\n'I am not a keeper over you' — lastu 'alaykum bi wakil. A 'wakil' is one entrusted with another's affairs, responsible for managing them.\n\nThis is the same release given at 6:35, 6:48 and 6:52. He is a warner and a bringer of good news, and that is the whole job.\n\nThere is also a boundary being drawn for the listener. If he is not your keeper, then you are, and the decision sits with you rather than with your relationship to him.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that it was revealed before the command to fight and describes the Prophet's position in Mecca — delivering, and having no power beyond that.\n\nThe verse leaves both parties where they actually stand. He has said it. They have heard it. Nothing else is owed.",
  "67": "'Every ˹destined˺ matter has a ˹set˺ time to transpire. And you will soon come to know.' (Quran 6:67). Eleven words in Arabic, and it closes the argument.\n\n'Every matter has a set time' — li kulli naba'in mustaqarr. 'Mustaqarr' is a settling place — where something comes to rest.\n\nThe word choice is precise. Every piece of news has a moment at which it lands. Until then it is a claim; afterwards it is a fact.\n\nThat applies to everything the surah has been arguing about. The resurrection denied in 6:29, the punishment demanded in 6:57, the promise of help in 6:34. Each is en route to its settling place.\n\n'And you will soon come to know' — wa sawfa ta'lamun. The same 'sawfa' as 6:5: 'they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule.'\n\nThere is no argument in this verse at all. It simply says: wait.\n\nThat is the appropriate ending to the exchange in 6:57 and 6:58, where they demanded the punishment now and were told the timing was not available. Here they are told it exists.\n\nThe verse works on the believer too, and perhaps more usefully. Everything Allah has said is scheduled. The absence of visible consequence is not evidence of anything, and neither is the presence of comfort, as 6:44 warned.\n\nNobody in this world sees the end of the story. That is not a defect in the story.",
  "68": "'And when you come across those who ridicule Our revelations, do not sit with them unless they engage in a different topic. Should Satan make you forget, then once you remember, do not ˹continue to˺ sit with the wrongdoing people.' (Quran 6:68). A practical instruction about company.\n\n'Those who ridicule Our revelations' — alladhina yakhudun fi ayatina. 'Khawd' is to wade in, to plunge into talk carelessly. It describes a manner of speaking rather than a considered position.\n\n'Do not sit with them' — fa a'rid 'anhum. Turn away. Notice the verb: it is the same root as 'i'rad', the turning away that this surah has condemned in the deniers since 6:4.\n\nThe one turning-away that is required is from mockery of revelation.\n\n'Unless they engage in a different topic' — hatta yakhudu fi hadithin ghayrih. The restriction is on the conversation, not on the people. When the subject changes, the objection ends.\n\nThat precision matters. This is not an instruction to shun anyone permanently or to refuse ordinary dealings.\n\n'Should Satan make you forget, then once you remember' — the verse anticipates failure and gives the remedy in the same breath. You will slip; leave when you notice.\n\nThe parallel at 4:140 states what is at stake: 'do not sit in that company unless they engage in a different topic, or else you will be like them.'\n\nPresence is participation. Sitting through it, silently, is read by everyone present — including yourself — as acceptance.",
  "69": "'Those mindful ˹of Allah˺ will not be accountable for those ˹who ridicule it˺ whatsoever—their duty is to advise, so perhaps the ridiculers will abstain.' (Quran 6:69). A qualification on the instruction of 6:68.\n\nThe command to leave the gathering raises an immediate question: what about the person who has a reason to be there — to speak, to correct, to represent the truth?\n\n'Will not be accountable for those who ridicule it whatsoever' — ma 'ala alladhina yattaquna min hisabihim min shay'. The sin of the mockers does not attach to the God-conscious person who is present for a purpose.\n\n'Their duty is to advise' — wa lakin dhikra. Literally: but a reminder. The word is left bare, and it names the only reason to stay.\n\n'So perhaps the ridiculers will abstain' — la'allahum yattaqun. The hope is stated, and it is only a hope.\n\nPut 6:68 and 6:69 together and a workable rule emerges. Do not sit through mockery as a passive audience; that makes you part of it. But being present in order to say something is a different act, and it is not held against you.\n\nThis is the same balance the previous surah struck. Verse 5:105 said 'you are accountable only for yourselves', and Abu Bakr corrected the reading that turned it into permission for silence.\n\nNeither verse licenses withdrawal from the world. Both distinguish between attending and endorsing.\n\nThe test is what you are doing there.",
  "70": "'And leave those who take this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere play and amusement and are deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life...' (Quran 6:70). The instruction of 6:68 widened.\n\n'As mere play and amusement' — la'iban wa lahwa. The exact phrase used in 6:32 for the worldly life itself.\n\nThat repetition is the sharpest thing in the verse. Verse 6:32 said this life is play and amusement; here are people who have taken religion as play and amusement instead. They have inverted the two, treating the temporary as serious and the serious as a game.\n\n'Deluded by their worldly life' — wa gharrathumu al-hayatu al-dunya. 'Ghurur' is deception by appearance — something that looks like what it is not.\n\n'Yet remind them by this Quran' — wa dhakkir bih. Leave them, and still remind them. The same balance as 6:69: withdrawal from the mockery, not abandonment of the people.\n\n'So no one should be ruined for their misdeeds' — an tubsala nafsun bima kasabat. 'Ibsal' is to be handed over, given up as forfeit.\n\nThen the closing: 'They will have no protector or intercessor other than Allah. Even if they were to offer every possible ransom, none will be accepted from them.'\n\nAnas reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying that Allah will ask the person with the lightest punishment in the Fire whether he would ransom himself with everything on earth, and he will say yes; and Allah will say, 'I asked you a much easier thing than this while you were in the backbone of Adam, that is, not to worship others besides Me' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6557).\n\nThe easy thing was asked first.",
  "71": "'Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Should we invoke, other than Allah, those ˹idols˺ which cannot benefit or harm us, and turn back to disbelief after Allah has guided us?...\"' (Quran 6:71). A refusal, illustrated with a picture.\n\n'Which cannot benefit or harm us' — ma la yanfa'una wa la yadurruna. The same test applied at 5:76 and repeated throughout this surah: what does it actually control?\n\nThen the image, and it is unusual for the Quran. 'Like those disoriented by devils in the wilderness, while their companions call them to guidance, ˹saying˺, \"Come to us!\"'\n\nPicture the scene. A person wandering in open country, pulled off course, while people who know the way stand calling him. The voices are audible. He does not turn.\n\n'Disoriented' — istahwathu al-shayatin. The verb shares its root with 'hawa' — desire. He was not dragged. He was drawn.\n\nThat is the diagnosis this surah keeps returning to, and the previous one made at 5:49 and 5:70. What pulls a person away is wanting, not confusion.\n\n'Allah's guidance is the only true guidance' — inna huda Allahi huwa al-huda. There is one path, as 6:1 said there is one light and many darknesses.\n\n'And we are commanded to submit to the Lord of all worlds' — the answer ends where 6:14 did: under orders, not offering an opinion.\n\nThe verse is also an answer to social pressure. Turning back would be the easy thing, and the wanderer in the image is going the way that feels natural.",
  "72": "'Establish prayer, and be mindful of Him. To Him you will all be gathered together.' (Quran 6:72). Two commands and a reason, in one short verse.\n\n'Establish prayer' — wa an aqimu al-salah. The verb 'aqama' is to make something stand, to keep it upright. It is not merely performing prayer but maintaining it as a standing structure in a life.\n\nThe same verb was used in the previous surah for keeping a scripture standing — 'aqamu' at 5:66 and 5:68. What you keep upright is what holds you up.\n\n'And be mindful of Him' — wa ittaquh. Taqwa, the awareness that shapes behaviour when nobody is checking.\n\nThe pairing is deliberate. Prayer is the visible act, taqwa the invisible state, and neither is offered as a substitute for the other.\n\n'To Him you will all be gathered' — wa huwa alladhi ilayhi tuhsharun. The reason given is the gathering, announced in 6:12 and 6:22 and now used as motivation rather than warning.\n\nWhat makes this verse notable is where it sits. After seventy verses of argument — creation, ruins, animals, storms, keys of the unseen, recording angels — the instruction that follows is short and practical.\n\nAll the reasoning arrives here: pray, and watch yourself.\n\nThat is the shape of the Quran's method. Long stretches of evidence, and then a small, doable thing. Conviction that produces nothing has not finished.",
  "73": "'He is the One Who created the heavens and the earth in truth. On the Day ˹of Judgment˺ He will say, \"Be!\" And there will be! His command is truth...' (Quran 6:73). Creation and resurrection described with the same word.\n\n'In truth' — bi al-haqq. Not as a game. This answers 6:32 and 6:70, where the mistake was treating serious things as play. The universe was not made idly.\n\n'He will say, \"Be!\" And there will be' — yaquluu kun fa yakun. The Quran uses this phrase for the original creation at 2:117 and 36:82, and here for the resurrection.\n\nThat is the argument, made by repeating a phrase. Whatever the first act cost — and the verse implies it cost nothing but a word — the second costs the same.\n\n'All authority is His alone on the Day the Trumpet will be blown' — lahu al-mulku yawma yunfakhu fi al-sur. Authority is His now too, as 6:12 and 6:62 said. What changes on that Day is not the fact but the visibility of it.\n\nThe Trumpet is described at 39:68: blown once and everyone falls, blown again and they rise.\n\n'The Knower of all—seen or unseen' — 'alimu al-ghaybi wa al-shahadah. Both categories, which 6:59 detailed at length: the keys of the unseen, and every falling leaf.\n\n'And He is the All-Wise, All-Aware' — al-Hakimu al-Khabir. The exact closing of 6:18.\n\nThe surah is gathering its threads before turning, in the next verse, to a story.",
  "74": "'And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said to his father, Ȃzar, \"Do you take idols as gods? It is clear to me that you and your people are entirely misguided.\"' (Quran 6:74). The surah turns to a man who asked the question it has been asking.\n\nEverything from 6:1 to 6:73 has argued against idols. Now it shows someone doing it, and the first person he says it to is his own father.\n\n'Do you take idols as gods?' — a tattakhidhu asnaman alihah. He asks rather than declares. The Quran preserves several versions of Ibrahim's questions to his father, and they are consistently gentle in form: 'O dear father! Why do you worship what can neither hear nor see, nor benefit you at all?' (Quran 19:42).\n\nThe content is uncompromising and the manner is respectful. Both at once, which is harder than either alone.\n\n'It is clear to me' — inni araka. Literally: I see you. He speaks from his own vision rather than accusing from a height.\n\n'You and your people are entirely misguided' — fi dalalin mubin. Clear error. He does not soften the conclusion, and he includes the whole community in it.\n\nThis is the hardest form the surah's argument can take. It is one thing to reject the idols of strangers. This is a young man telling his father, in a society organised around these objects, that the arrangement is wrong.\n\nThe Meccan audience hearing this understood the parallel exactly. Their claim to Ibrahim as ancestor was central to their identity, and here he is, breaking with his fathers.",
  "75": "'We also showed Abraham the wonders of the heavens and the earth, so he would be sure in faith.' (Quran 6:75). What was given to him before the argument that follows.\n\n'We showed Abraham' — wa kadhalika nuri Ibrahim. It was shown to him. His insight is presented as a gift, not an achievement of unusual intelligence.\n\n'The wonders of the heavens and the earth' — malakuta al-samawati wa al-ard. 'Malakut' is a heightened form of 'mulk', dominion. It suggests not just the things but the working of them — the government of the universe rather than its furniture.\n\n'So he would be sure in faith' — wa li yakuna mina al-muqinin. 'Yaqin' is certainty, the state 5:113 described the disciples asking for when they wanted their hearts to settle.\n\nNotice the sequence. He had already rejected the idols in 6:74. The certainty comes after the rejection, not before it.\n\nThat order appears often in the Quran. Acting on what you already see is what opens what you cannot yet see. The disciples asked for the table after they had believed.\n\n'Malakut' also sets up what follows. He is about to look at stars, moon and sun — the most impressive objects available to the eye — and reason his way past them.\n\nHe can do that because he has been shown the working, and the working is what tells you that a bright object rising and setting is being operated rather than operating.\n\nThe next four verses are him thinking out loud.",
  "76": "'When the night grew dark upon him, he saw a star and said, \"This is my Lord!\" But when it set, he said, \"I do not love things that set.\"' (Quran 6:76). The first step of the argument.\n\nThe obvious question is whether Ibrahim actually believed the star was his Lord. Al-Tabari, commenting on this passage, records the reading that he was arguing with his people rather than reporting his own belief — presenting their position and following it to its conclusion in front of them.\n\nThat reading fits the setting. Verse 6:74 has him already certain the idols are false, and 6:83 will call the whole passage 'the argument We gave Abraham against his people'.\n\nSo this is a method, not a diary. He takes the position seriously enough to test it properly.\n\n'But when it set' — fa lamma afala. 'Uful' is the disappearance of a heavenly body below the horizon.\n\n'I do not love things that set' — la uhibbu al-afilin. Note the verb: love, not worship. He rejects it as an object of attachment before rejecting it as a deity.\n\nThat is a more penetrating criterion than it looks. What sets, leaves. Whatever governs the universe cannot be something that is regularly unavailable.\n\nAnd there is a wider application, which is why the phrase has stayed in the memory of Muslims for fourteen centuries. Everything a person can attach himself to in this life sets: health, beauty, wealth, standing, people.\n\nIbrahim's objection is not only theological. It is about where a heart is safe to rest.",
  "77": "'Then when he saw the moon rising, he said, \"This one is my Lord!\" But when it disappeared, he said, \"If my Lord does not guide me, I will certainly be one of the misguided people.\"' (Quran 6:77). The second step, with something new added.\n\nThe moon is a better candidate than the star. Bigger, brighter, and the object of actual worship in that region.\n\nAnd it sets too. The same test applied to a stronger case gives the same result.\n\nWhat is new is his response. After the star he stated a principle: 'I do not love things that set.' After the moon he says something about himself: 'If my Lord does not guide me, I will certainly be one of the misguided people.'\n\n'If my Lord does not guide me' — la in lam yahdini rabbi. He acknowledges that reasoning alone will not get him there.\n\nThat is a striking admission in the middle of a demonstration of reasoning. The man is thinking his way through the sky and stops to say that thinking is not enough.\n\nIt matches what 6:75 already told us: 'We also showed Abraham the wonders of the heavens and the earth.' The showing came from outside.\n\nThere is a caution here for anyone confident in their own reasoning. Ibrahim, in the middle of the finest argument in the surah, expresses the fear of going astray.\n\nCompare that with the people described in 6:43, whose hearts hardened and whose deeds were made to look good to them. They had no such fear, which is precisely what allowed the error.",
  "78": "'Then when he saw the sun shining, he said, \"This must be my Lord—it is the greatest!\" But again when it set, he declared, \"O my people! I totally reject whatever you associate ˹with Allah in worship˺.\"' (Quran 6:78). The final step and the conclusion.\n\n'It is the greatest' — hadha akbar. He grants the sun the best case. Largest, brightest, the source of daylight itself, and worshipped across the ancient world.\n\nThe concession is deliberate. He is not knocking down the weakest candidate; he takes the strongest one available and applies the same test.\n\n'But again when it set' — fa lamma afalat. Third time. The pattern is complete, and the audience has watched it three times.\n\nThat repetition is why the passage works as an argument rather than an assertion. Anyone following along has worked out the conclusion before Ibrahim states it.\n\n'O my people!' — ya qawmi. He turns and addresses them, which confirms that the whole demonstration was performed for their benefit.\n\n'I totally reject whatever you associate' — inni bari'un mimma tushrikun. 'Bara'ah' — the same disavowal the Prophet was made to declare at 6:19, word for word.\n\nThe parallel is deliberate. The Meccans traced their descent to Ibrahim. The verse shows Ibrahim using the Prophet's exact formula against people exactly like them.\n\nNotice also that the argument required no scripture and no miracle. It needed a night, a horizon, and the willingness to follow a thought to its end.\n\nWhich is what 6:11 asked for when it said: travel and see.",
  "79": "'I have turned my face towards the One Who has originated the heavens and the earth—being upright—and I am not one of the polytheists.\"' (Quran 6:79). The positive statement after the rejection.\n\nVerse 6:78 said what he rejects. This says what he turns to, and the order matters — the Quran consistently clears the ground before building.\n\n'I have turned my face' — wajjahtu wajhiya. The face is the whole of a person's attention and direction, not merely a posture.\n\n'The One Who has originated' — lilladhi fatara. 'Fatir' — the same word used of Allah at 6:14, the One who split things into being from nothing.\n\nThat is the answer to the sky. The star, the moon and the sun all set because all three were made. The one who made them does not belong to their category.\n\n'Being upright' — hanifa. This word carries a specific meaning in the Quran. A 'hanif' is one who turns away from everything false toward the single truth, by inclination rather than by argument. It is the word used of Ibrahim at 3:67 and 16:120, and it is connected to the 'fitrah' — the natural way Allah instilled in people, described at 30:30.\n\n'And I am not one of the polytheists' — wa ma ana mina al-mushrikin. The same closing given to the Prophet at 6:14: 'not be one of the polytheists'.\n\nTwo men, separated by millennia, ending their statements identically. The surah is making a point about continuity that it will state outright in 6:161.",
  "80": "'And his people argued with him. He responded, \"Are you arguing with me about Allah, while He has guided me? I am not afraid of whatever ˹idols˺ you associate with Him...\"' (Quran 6:80). The argument turns into a confrontation.\n\n'And his people argued with him' — wa hajjahu qawmuh. They did not concede. Watching a demonstration and accepting it are different acts, as 6:7 established.\n\n'Are you arguing with me about Allah, while He has guided me?' — the reply does not restate the evidence. He points to the change in himself.\n\nThat is the appropriate response once evidence has been rejected. Verse 6:35 told the Prophet not to exhaust himself producing more.\n\n'I am not afraid of whatever idols you associate with Him' — wa la akhafu ma tushrikuna bih. This tells us what their argument actually was. Not a case for the idols' truth, but a warning about their power. Reject them and something will happen to you.\n\nThreats of that kind are the last argument of a false system, and they were made to the Prophet too.\n\n'Unless my Lord so wills' — illa an yasha'a rabbi shay'a. He does not claim invulnerability. Harm can reach him — but only from one direction, which is exactly what 6:17 established: 'If Allah touches you with harm, none can undo it except Him.'\n\n'My Lord encompasses everything in His knowledge' — the knowledge described in 6:59, down to the falling leaf.\n\nFear rightly placed removes every other fear. That is the whole of his answer.",
  "81": "'And how should I fear your associate-gods, while you have no fear in associating ˹others˺ with Allah—a practice He has never authorized? Which side has more right to security? ˹Tell me˺ if you really know!' (Quran 6:81). The question turned around.\n\nHe was being warned to be afraid. He asks why they are not.\n\n'A practice He has never authorized' — ma lam yunazzil bihi 'alaykum sultana. 'Sultan' here is authorising proof — a warrant sent down. They have none, and the absence is the point.\n\nThat criterion runs through the surah. Verse 6:21 condemned fabricating lies against Allah; 6:148 will demand that they produce knowledge rather than conjecture.\n\n'Which side has more right to security?' — ayyu al-fariqayni ahaqqu bi al-amn. Two parties: one who fears things that have no authorisation, and one who does not fear the Author.\n\n'If you really know' — in kuntum ta'lamun. The same challenge as 6:40: answer, if your claims are true. And again the answer is left to them.\n\nWhat makes this verse land is the reversal of who is exposed. They presented themselves as the safe ones, holding to the arrangement everyone knew. He points out that safety is not determined by numbers, and 6:116 will say so explicitly.\n\nSecurity — 'amn' — becomes the theme, and the next verse defines who has it.\n\nThe argument has moved from the sky to the ground. It is no longer about which object is God. It is about which position a sensible person would rather be standing in.",
  "82": "'It is ˹only˺ those who are faithful and do not tarnish their faith with falsehood who are guaranteed security and are ˹rightly˺ guided.' (Quran 6:82). The answer to the question of 6:81.\n\n'Do not tarnish their faith with falsehood' — wa lam yalbisu imanahum bi zulm. The word rendered 'falsehood' here is 'zulm' — wrongdoing, injustice.\n\nThat word caused immediate alarm among the Companions. 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported: 'When the following Verse was revealed: \"It is those who believe and confuse not their belief with wrong\" (6:83), the companions of Allah's Messenger asked, \"Who is amongst us who had not done injustice?\" Allah revealed: \"No doubt, joining others in worship with Allah is a great injustice indeed\" (31:13)' (Sahih al-Bukhari 32).\n\nSo the 'zulm' meant here is shirk specifically, not every sin. Without that clarification the verse would leave nobody secure, and the Companions understood it that way until they were corrected.\n\nThe clarification matters enormously for how a believer reads this surah. Failing is not the same as forfeiting.\n\n'Who are guaranteed security' — lahumu al-amn. The security Ibrahim asked about in the previous verse belongs to them.\n\n'And are rightly guided' — wa hum muhtadun. Both things together: safety and direction.\n\nNote the verb 'yalbisu' — to mix or confuse. The same root used in 6:9 and 6:65. The danger is not open rejection but contamination, faith held alongside something that cancels it.",
  "83": "'This was the argument We gave Abraham against his people. We elevate in rank whoever We please. Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.' (Quran 6:83). The passage named and attributed.\n\n'This was the argument We gave Abraham' — wa tilka hujjatuna ataynaha Ibrahim. The reasoning of 6:76 to 6:81 is described as something given to him.\n\nThat settles how the star, moon and sun passage should be read. It was an argument — 'hujjah' — and it was supplied.\n\nIbrahim's greatness in this surah is real and it is not self-generated. He was shown the working of the heavens (6:75), guided (6:80), and handed the argument (6:83). What was his own was the willingness to use all three, starting with his own father.\n\n'We elevate in rank whoever We please' — narfa'u darajatin man nasha'. Rank is assigned, not seized. The same principle that 6:53 stated when the Meccan elite objected to poor believers being favoured: 'Has Allah favoured these out of all of us?'\n\n'Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing' — 'Alimun Hakim. The elevation is not arbitrary. He knows who to raise and why, which is the answer to anyone who finds the distribution unfair.\n\nThere is a caution in this verse for the reader who has just admired Ibrahim. The admiration is right, and the conclusion to draw from it is not that he was exceptional by nature.\n\nHe was given things. So is everyone, in some measure, and 6:53 already named what Allah recognises: gratitude.",
  "84": "'And We blessed him with Isaac and Jacob. We guided them all as We previously guided Noah and those among his descendants: David, Solomon, Job, Joseph, Moses, and Aaron. This is how We reward the good-doers.' (Quran 6:84). The line that came from the man who stood alone.\n\nVerse 6:74 showed Ibrahim breaking with his father. This shows what was given to him instead.\n\n'And We blessed him with Isaac and Jacob' — wa wahabna lahu Ishaqa wa Ya'qub. The verb is 'wahaba', to give as a gift, and it is the standard word in the Quran for children granted after long waiting.\n\nThat is the answer to what he gave up. He lost a father and a people, and was given a lineage.\n\n'As We previously guided Noah' — the list then reaches backwards past Ibrahim to Nuh, placing him inside a chain rather than at its head.\n\nEight names in this verse, and 6:85 and 6:86 will add ten more. Eighteen prophets listed in three verses, which is the longest such list in the Quran.\n\nWhy here? Because the Meccans claimed Ibrahim, and the surah is establishing that the line he founded is defined by what he did in 6:74 to 6:79 — rejecting inherited falsehood — and not by descent.\n\n'This is how We reward the good-doers' — kadhalika najzi al-muhsinin. The same closing used in the previous surah at 5:85 and 5:93.\n\nReward, not entitlement. The chain continues by conduct, and 2:124 already recorded Allah's reply when Ibrahim asked about his offspring: 'My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers.'",
  "85": "'Likewise, ˹We guided˺ Zachariah, John, Jesus, and Elias, who were all of the righteous.' (Quran 6:85). Four more names added to the list begun in 6:84.\n\nThe grouping is significant. Zakariyya and his son Yahya, then 'Isa, then Ilyas — figures from the later stretch of the Israelite prophetic line.\n\n'Jesus' — 'Isa. His inclusion in a list of guided prophets is quietly doing something. The previous surah spent its closing passage establishing exactly this: that he was a messenger who said 'Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord' (Quran 5:72).\n\nHere he appears without argument, simply as one name among many, which is the position the Quran has been arguing for.\n\n'Who were all of the righteous' — kullun mina al-salihin. One description covers the four of them. No hierarchy is given, no distinctions drawn.\n\nThat flattening is the purpose of the whole list. Verses 6:84 to 6:87 name eighteen prophets in four verses and give them a single collective description, guided by one Guide.\n\nNotice what is absent. No community is named after any of them. No follower group is credited. Just the men and the guidance they received.\n\nThe Meccans, and the People of the Book listening in Medina later, both organised their identity around belonging to a particular strand. This list dissolves the strands.\n\n'Likewise' — the verse begins by attaching itself to the previous one. The reward described at the end of 6:84 continues here.",
  "86": "'˹We also guided˺ Ishmael, Elisha, Jonah, and Lot, favouring each over other people ˹of their time˺.' (Quran 6:86). Four more, and the first of them matters especially to this audience.\n\n'Ishmael' — Isma'il. The Meccans traced their ancestry through him. He is placed here in a list, between Elisha and Jonah, with no special treatment.\n\nThat placement is the argument. They claimed distinction through descent from Isma'il; the verse shows Isma'il himself receiving what he received as a gift, alongside prophets they had no connection to.\n\n'Jonah' — Yunus. His inclusion is worth pausing over. His story is the one in which a prophet left his post in frustration and was disciplined for it.\n\nHe is on the list. The Quran does not curate its prophets to remove the difficult episode, and the surah has already shown a prophet admitting he would have ended the matter early if he could (Quran 6:58).\n\n'Lot' — Lut. A prophet whose mission ended with almost no one believing, and whose own wife was among those destroyed.\n\nSuccess by numbers is not the criterion for being on this list. Verse 6:100 will make the same point from the other direction.\n\n'Favouring each over other people of their time' — wa kullan faddalna 'ala al-'alamin. The favour is stated as given, not earned, exactly as in 6:83: 'We elevate in rank whoever We please.'",
  "87": "'And ˹We favoured˺ some of their forefathers, their descendants, and their brothers. We chose them and guided them to the Straight Path.' (Quran 6:87). The list closes by widening and then narrowing.\n\n'Some of their forefathers, their descendants, and their brothers' — wa min aba'ihim wa dhurriyyatihim wa ikhwanihim. Three generations named, and the operative word is 'min' — some of.\n\nThat partitive is doing serious work. Not all the fathers, not all the sons, not all the brothers. The line is not automatic.\n\nVerse 6:84 established the family chain; this verse immediately qualifies it. Prophethood ran through households, and it did not run to everyone in them. Ibrahim's father is the standing example, named in 6:74.\n\n'We chose them' — wa ijtabaynahum. 'Ijtiba'' is selection — picking out from among others.\n\n'And guided them to the Straight Path' — wa hadaynahum ila siratin mustaqim. The same singular path named at 6:39 and 6:153, against the many darknesses of 6:1.\n\nTwo verbs, chosen and guided, both with Allah as the subject and the prophets as the object. Eighteen names, one Actor.\n\nThis is the answer to any community that treats prophetic descent as inheritance. The Meccans did it through Isma'il; others did it through Ishaq. The verse says selection was individual and partial in every generation.\n\nYou are not carried by your line. That was already the message of 5:18 and it is the message here.",
  "88": "'This is Allah's guidance with which He guides whoever He wills of His servants. Had they associated others with Him ˹in worship˺, their ˹good˺ deeds would have been wasted.' (Quran 6:88). A warning attached to the greatest names in history.\n\n'Had they associated others with Him' — wa law ashraku. The pronoun refers to the eighteen prophets just named.\n\nRead that again. The Quran raises, as a hypothetical, the possibility of shirk from Ibrahim, Musa, 'Isa and the rest, and states what the consequence would have been.\n\n'Their good deeds would have been wasted' — la habita 'anhum ma kanu ya'malun. 'Habita' is used for something that swells and then bursts, leaving nothing.\n\nEverything they did — the preaching, the endurance, the sacrifice — void, on that one condition.\n\nThe principle is stated to the Prophet directly at 39:65: 'if you associate others with Allah, your deeds will certainly be void.'\n\nThe purpose of putting it this way is to remove any idea that shirk is a beginner's error, forgiven in advanced practitioners. If it would have cancelled the record of prophets, it cancels anyone's.\n\n'He guides whoever He wills of His servants' — the guidance is His to give, which the whole list has demonstrated.\n\nThere is a mercy in this severity, and 6:82 already stated it: the security belongs to those who do not mix their faith with shirk. What that verse promised, this verse protects. One condition, applied without exception at every level.",
  "89": "'Those were the ones to whom We gave the Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood. But if these ˹pagans˺ disbelieve in this ˹message˺, then We have already entrusted it to a people who will never disbelieve in it.' (Quran 6:89). The consequence of refusal.\n\n'Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood' — al-kitaba wa al-hukma wa al-nubuwwah. Three gifts, given to the eighteen named.\n\n'But if these disbelieve in this' — fa in yakfur biha ha'ula'. The Meccans are being addressed, and the verse contemplates their refusal as a real possibility.\n\n'Then We have already entrusted it to a people who will never disbelieve in it' — fa qad wakkalna biha qawman laysu biha bi kafirin.\n\nThe tense is worth noticing: 'we have already entrusted'. The arrangement is in place before the refusal is complete. There is no gap in which the message is left without a carrier.\n\nThat is the point being made to a group who believed the whole matter depended on their acceptance. It did not. Their refusal would cost them the trust, not cost the trust its existence.\n\nAl-Tabari, commenting on this verse, reads the entrusted people as the Emigrants and Helpers who did carry it — and the Prophet had been given a similar forecast at the very beginning. Waraqa bin Naufal told him, when the first revelation came, 'Never did a man come with something similar to what you have brought but was treated with hostility' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6982).\n\nHostility was expected. Failure of the message was not.\n\n'Wakkalna' shares its root with 'wakil' — the word used at 6:66, where the Prophet was told he was not a keeper over them. He is not the keeper. The message has one.",
  "90": "'These ˹prophets˺ were ˹rightly˺ guided by Allah, so follow their guidance. Say, \"I ask no reward of you for this ˹Quran˺—it is a reminder to the whole world.\"' (Quran 6:90). The instruction drawn from the whole list.\n\n'So follow their guidance' — fa bi hudahumu iqtadih. Follow the guidance they had, not their persons.\n\nThat distinction is exact, and it is the correction the previous surah made at 5:77 about going to extremes. The prophets are to be imitated, not elevated.\n\nAnd what is there to imitate across eighteen men separated by thousands of years? One thing: they received guidance and submitted to it. Ibrahim did it against his father, Yunus after his own lapse, Lut with almost no followers.\n\n'I ask no reward of you for this' — la as'alukum 'alayhi ajra. The Prophet is made to state the terms.\n\nThat is placed here for a reason. A man asking people to change their entire way of life will be suspected of wanting something. The Quran has him remove the suspicion explicitly, as it does at 25:57.\n\nNothing he asked for was for himself. He had already denied holding treasuries or knowledge of the unseen at 6:50.\n\n'It is a reminder to the whole world' — in huwa illa dhikra lil-'alamin. Not a possession of the Arabs, not a tribal document. The audience is stated as everyone, which 6:19 already said: 'whoever it reaches'.",
  "91": "'And they have not shown Allah His proper reverence when they said, \"Allah has revealed nothing to any human being.\"...' (Quran 6:91). A denial that proves too much.\n\n'They have not shown Allah His proper reverence' — wa ma qadaru Allaha haqqa qadrih. They did not estimate Him at His true measure. The same phrase appears at 39:67.\n\nTheir claim was that Allah does not speak to human beings. The verse treats this not as an error about prophets but as an insult to Allah — a claim that He is unable or unwilling to communicate.\n\n'Who then revealed the Book brought forth by Moses as a light and guidance for people?' — the reply names something they already accept. Musa's book was not in dispute among them.\n\n'Which you split into separate sheets—revealing some and hiding much' — taj'alunahu qaratisa tubdunaha wa tukhfuna kathira. The handling of scripture is described as selective disclosure.\n\nThe Quran makes the same charge at 2:79 and 3:78.\n\n'You have been taught what neither you nor your forefathers knew' — a reminder that the Quran itself supplied knowledge they did not have, which is evidence on the very question they are denying.\n\n'Say, \"Allah revealed it!\"' — the answer is given by the Prophet because they would not give it, exactly as in 6:12 and 6:64.\n\n'Then leave them to amuse themselves with falsehood' — dharhum fi khawdihim yal'abun. 'Khawd' again, the careless wading of 6:68, and 'play' again, from 6:32 and 6:70.",
  "92": "'This is a blessed Book which We have revealed—confirming what came before it—so you may warn the Mother of Cities and everyone around it. Those who believe in the Hereafter ˹truly˺ believe in it and guard their prayers.' (Quran 6:92). The Book described in its own words.\n\n'Confirming what came before it' — musaddiqu alladhi bayna yadayh. The claim is continuity, not replacement, which is why 6:84 to 6:87 listed eighteen earlier prophets without apology.\n\n'The Mother of Cities' — Umm al-Qura, Mecca. 'And everyone around it' — wa man hawlaha.\n\nThose two phrases together answer a question about scope. The warning starts somewhere specific and does not stop there. The same wording is at 42:7, and 6:90 has just called the Book 'a reminder to the whole world'.\n\n'Those who believe in the Hereafter truly believe in it' — the link between the two beliefs is stated as a fact rather than an argument.\n\nIt follows what the surah established at 6:29. Denial of the Hereafter was the belief underneath every refusal. Someone who accepts that there is an accounting has a reason to attend to a Book about it; someone who does not, has none.\n\n'And guard their prayers' — wa hum 'ala salatihim yuhafizun. 'Muhafazah' is protecting something, keeping watch over it.\n\nBelief in the Hereafter is tested, in this verse, by whether prayer is guarded. Not by whether it is affirmed.\n\nThat is the second time the surah has landed on prayer as the practical outcome of a long argument, after 6:72.",
  "93": "'Who does more wrong than the one who fabricates lies against Allah or claims, \"I have received revelations!\"—although nothing was revealed to them...' (Quran 6:93). The question of 6:21, asked again with new offences.\n\nThree are named. Fabricating lies against Allah. Falsely claiming revelation. And saying: 'I can reveal the like of Allah's revelations!'\n\nThe second and third are the mirror image of the denial in 6:91. There, people said Allah revealed nothing to anyone. Here, people claim revelation they never received, or claim they could produce its equal.\n\nBoth are failures to take revelation seriously — one by denying it exists, the other by treating it as something anyone could do.\n\nThen the scene: 'If you could only see the wrongdoers in the throes of death while the angels are stretching out their hands ˹saying˺, \"Give up your souls!\"'\n\n'The throes of death' — ghamarat al-mawt. 'Ghamrah' is a flood that covers over.\n\nThis is the fourth time the surah has begun a sentence with 'if you could only see' — after 6:27, 6:30 and 6:31 — and again it is left unfinished.\n\n'For telling lies about Allah and for being arrogant towards His revelations' — two charges, and the second is 'istikbar', the arrogance that 5:82 identified as the single barrier to faith.\n\nThe angels who take the soul are the ones described in 6:61, who never neglect the duty. Here they are shown arriving.",
  "94": "'˹Today˺ you have come back to Us all alone as We created you the first time —leaving behind everything We have provided you with...' (Quran 6:94). What the person of 6:93 finds on arrival.\n\n'All alone' — furada. Singly, one at a time.\n\n'As We created you the first time' — kama khalaqnakum awwala marrah. The comparison is to birth: no possessions, no status, no company.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported the Prophet saying in a sermon: 'O people! You will be gathered before Allah barefooted, naked and not circumcised' (Sahih al-Bukhari 4625).\n\n'Leaving behind everything We have provided you with' — wa taraktum ma khawwalnakum wara'a zuhurikum. Behind your backs. The verb 'khawwala' means to grant the use of something, which is precisely what possessions turn out to have been.\n\n'We do not see your intercessors with you' — this is the arrangement 6:51 warned about and 6:22 tested: 'Where are those gods you used to claim?'\n\n'All your ties have been broken' — laqad taqatta'a baynakum. Every connection — tribe, patron, family standing — cut.\n\n'And all your claims have let you down' — wa dalla 'ankum ma kuntum taz'umun. The same verb as 6:24: what they invented simply wanders off.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Allah will take the whole earth in His Hand and will roll up the Heaven in His right Hand, and then He will say, \"I am King! Where are the kings of the earth?\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6519).",
  "95": "'Indeed, Allah is the One Who causes seeds and fruit stones to sprout. He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living. That is Allah! How can you then be deluded ˹from the truth˺?' (Quran 6:95). After the deathbed and the gathering, the surah returns to a seed.\n\n'Seeds and fruit stones' — al-habbi wa al-nawa. Grain, and the hard stone inside a date or an olive.\n\nConsider why these two. Both are objects that look finished. A dry seed and a stone are the least promising things a person handles, and both contain a tree.\n\n'He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living' — yukhriju al-hayya mina al-mayyiti wa mukhriju al-mayyiti mina al-hayy.\n\nThe sentence runs both directions, and that is what makes it an argument rather than an observation. Anyone can see life come from a seed. The verse insists the reverse is equally His doing, which means death is not a failure of the system.\n\nPlaced immediately after 6:93 and 6:94 — the throes of death, the soul arriving alone — this is the answer to the fear those verses produce. The One who takes life out of the living is the One who takes it out of a seed, and He does the reverse just as easily.\n\n'That is Allah!' — dhalikumu Allah. Stated as a conclusion, as if pointing.\n\n'How can you then be deluded?' — fa anna tu'fakun. 'Ifk' is being turned away from the obvious, and the question closes a section that began with the demand for a sign in 6:37.\n\nThe sign was in the kitchen the whole time.",
  "96": "'He causes the dawn to break, and has made the night for rest and ˹made˺ the sun and the moon ˹to travel˺ with precision. That is the design of the Almighty, All-Knowing.' (Quran 6:96). The argument moves from a seed to the sky.\n\n'He causes the dawn to break' — faliqu al-isbah. The verb 'falaqa' is to split open — the same verb used in the previous verse for splitting a seed.\n\nThat repetition is deliberate. The dawn is described as a seed splitting: the same act, at a different scale.\n\nIbn 'Abbas explained 'al-Asbah' as 'the light of the sun during the day and the light of the moon at night' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6982).\n\n'The night for rest' — al-layla sakana. 'Sakan' is stillness, a place of settling — the same root used in 6:13 for everything that dwells in the day and night.\n\n'The sun and the moon with precision' — husbanan. By exact reckoning, by calculation.\n\nThat single word is remarkable in a seventh-century text addressed to people who navigated by them. The heavenly bodies do not merely move; they move to a calculation, which is what makes calendars and navigation possible. The Quran says the same at 10:5 and 36:38–39.\n\n'That is the design of the Almighty, All-Knowing' — taqdiru al-'Azizi al-'Alim. 'Taqdir' is measuring out, determining an exact amount.\n\nAnd notice which objects have been chosen: the sun and the moon. The two that Ibrahim watched set in 6:77 and 6:78. What he rejected as lords, this verse presents as instruments.",
  "97": "'And He is the One Who has made the stars as your guide through the darkness of land and sea. We have already made the signs clear for people who know.' (Quran 6:97). The third object from Ibrahim's night sky, given its actual function.\n\n'As your guide' — li tahtadu biha. So that you find your way by them.\n\nThe stars had two roles in that society. They were worshipped, and they were used for navigation. The verse keeps the second and cancels the first by keeping it.\n\nThat is an efficient argument. A thing being used as a tool is not a thing being served as a lord, and everyone in the audience had used them as tools.\n\n'Through the darkness of land and sea' — fi zulumati al-barri wa al-bahr. The same phrase as 6:63, where the darknesses were crises and people prayed to be rescued.\n\nHere the darkness is navigational, and the provision is different. In 6:63 He rescues; here He gave you the means to find your own way before the emergency.\n\nBoth are mercy. One is dramatic and one is quiet, and the quiet one is easier to miss, which is the whole difficulty this section is addressing.\n\n'For people who know' — li qawmin ya'lamun. The signs are clear; the qualification is on the audience.\n\nVerse 6:97 ends with 'who know', 6:98 with 'who comprehend', 6:99 with 'who believe'. Three different capacities, three verses in a row.\n\nThe evidence is one. What people bring to it is not.",
  "98": "'And He is the One Who originated you all from a single soul, then assigned you a place to live and another to ˹be laid to˺ rest. We have already made the signs clear for people who comprehend.' (Quran 6:98). From the stars to human origins.\n\n'From a single soul' — min nafsin wahidah. The whole species from one origin, stated as it is at 4:1 and 7:189.\n\nThe implication is one the Quran draws elsewhere and leaves implicit here: the divisions people rank each other by are recent. Verse 6:53 has already shown Meccan leaders objecting to poor believers, and 49:13 states the principle directly.\n\n'A place to live and another to be laid to rest' — fa mustaqarrun wa mustawda'. The two words are read in more than one way. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the reading that 'mustaqarr' is the womb and 'mustawda'' the grave, and also the reading that one is the settled dwelling of this life and the other the place of temporary deposit.\n\nBoth readings share the same shape: one location where you rest, one where you are stored. Neither is permanent, and the word 'mustawda'' — a deposit — implies retrieval.\n\n'Mustaqarr' also appeared at 6:67: 'Every matter has a set time to transpire' — a settling place. The same word for a moment and for a location.\n\n'For people who comprehend' — li qawmin yafqahun. 'Fiqh' is understanding that goes beneath the surface of a thing.\n\nKnowing where you came from and where you are going is presented here as the deepest of the three capacities named in this passage.",
  "99": "'And He is the One Who sends down rain from the sky—causing all kinds of plants to grow—producing green stalks from which We bring forth clustered grain...' (Quran 6:99). The longest sign in the surah, and the most ordinary.\n\nThe verse moves in stages: rain, then plants, then a green stalk, then grain packed in a head. Each step is described as a separate act.\n\n'And from palm trees come clusters of dates hanging within reach' — qinwanun daniyah. Low enough to pick. The detail is about convenience, which is not something a person notices about a date palm until it is pointed out.\n\n'Similar ˹in shape˺ but dissimilar ˹in taste˺' — mushtabihan wa ghayra mutashabih. Grapes, olives and pomegranates drink the same water from the same soil and produce three entirely different things.\n\nThat is the actual argument of the verse, and it is a good one. Identical inputs, divergent outputs. Something is directing the difference.\n\n'Look at their fruit as it yields and ripens!' — unzuru ila thamarihi idha athmara wa yan'ih. An instruction to look at fruit.\n\nThe surah asked people to travel and look at ruins in 6:11. Here it asks them to look at a fig.\n\n'Indeed, in these are signs for people who believe' — li qawmin yu'minun. The third of the three closings, after 'who know' in 6:97 and 'who comprehend' in 6:98.\n\nThey demanded a sign in 6:37. Four verses have now answered, and every one of them was already in front of them.",
  "100": "'Yet they associate the jinn with Allah ˹in worship˺, even though He created them, and they falsely attribute to Him sons and daughters out of ignorance. Glorified and Exalted is He above what they claim!' (Quran 6:100). After the seeds and the rain, the contradiction.\n\n'They associate the jinn with Allah' — wa ja'alu lillahi shuraka'a al-jinn. Some Arabs sought protection from jinn, a practice the Quran mentions at 72:6: 'some men used to seek refuge with some jinn—so they increased each other in wickedness.'\n\n'Even though He created them' — wa khalaqahum. The whole refutation, in three words. A created thing cannot be a partner to its Creator.\n\n'Sons and daughters' — banina wa banat. Two claims are covered. The attribution of a son, addressed at length in the previous surah at 5:72 to 5:75. And daughters — the Arabs' claim that the angels were Allah's daughters, which 16:57 and 43:19 both address.\n\n'Out of ignorance' — bi ghayri 'ilm. Without knowledge. Not a conclusion from evidence but an assumption inherited, which 6:104 will describe as a choice to be blind.\n\n'Glorified and Exalted is He above what they claim' — subhanahu wa ta'ala 'amma yasifun. 'Tanzih' — declaring Allah free of every defect ascribed to Him.\n\nNote the placement. Verses 6:95 to 6:99 built a case from seeds, dawn, stars and fruit, and this verse describes what people did with all that evidence.\n\nThe gap between the two is the subject of the whole surah. The signs were sufficient. The response did not follow from them.",
  "101": "'˹He is˺ the Originator of the heavens and earth. How could He have children when He has no mate? He created all things and has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of everything.' (Quran 6:101). The claim of 6:100 examined logically.\n\n'How could He have children when He has no mate?' — anna yakunu lahu waladun wa lam takun lahu sahibah.\n\nThe argument works by following the idea of offspring to what it requires. A child is produced by a pair. To say Allah has a child is to imply a companion, and nobody had claimed that.\n\nSo the belief collapses on its own terms rather than needing external refutation. It was never thought through, which is what 6:100 meant by 'out of ignorance'.\n\n'The Originator' — badi'. One who brings something into being with no precedent and no model. That word alone answers the question, because origination and reproduction are opposite kinds of act.\n\n'He created all things' — wa khalaqa kulla shay'. Including whatever anyone might nominate as His offspring. A created thing is on one side of the line and cannot cross to the other.\n\nSurah al-Ikhlas states the same conclusion without argument: 'He has never had offspring, nor was He born. And there is none comparable to Him' (Quran 112:3–4).\n\nThe Quran's method here is worth noticing. It does not appeal to authority or to revelation. It takes the claim, asks what would have to be true for it to work, and lets the audience see the answer.\n\nIbrahim used exactly this method with the star, the moon and the sun in 6:76 to 6:78.",
  "102": "'That is Allah—your Lord! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. ˹He is˺ the Creator of all things, so worship Him ˹alone˺. And He is the Maintainer of everything.' (Quran 6:102). The conclusion of everything since 6:95.\n\n'That is Allah—your Lord!' — dhalikumu Allahu rabbukum. The same pointing phrase as 6:95, and here it gathers the whole passage: the seed, the dawn, the stars, the single soul, the rain, the fruit.\n\n'The Creator of all things, so worship Him alone' — khaliqu kulli shay'in fa'buduh. Notice the 'so'. The command follows from the description, and the verse presents the step as obvious.\n\nThat is the shape of the Quran's reasoning about worship. It never argues that Allah deserves worship because He is powerful or because worship is required. It argues from making. Whoever made a thing has the claim on it.\n\n'And He is the Maintainer of everything' — wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in wakil. 'Wakil' — the one in charge of the affair, who manages and sustains it.\n\nThat word has appeared twice in this surah already, and both times as a denial: the Prophet is 'not a keeper over you' at 6:66, and will be told the same at 6:107.\n\nThe office he does not hold is named here as belonging to Allah. Nobody is managing the outcome of your life except the One who made it.\n\nWhich is why the surah keeps releasing the Prophet from a responsibility he kept trying to carry.",
  "103": "'No vision can encompass Him, but He encompasses all vision. For He is the Most Subtle, All-Aware.' (Quran 6:103). A statement about the limits of sight.\n\n'No vision can encompass Him' — la tudrikuhu al-absar. The verb 'adraka' is to catch up with, to take in fully, to grasp.\n\nThat precision matters, because the Quran also says that on the Day of Judgment some faces will be 'looking at their Lord' (Quran 75:23). The two are not in conflict: seeing is not the same as encompassing. What this verse denies is comprehension, the ability to take Him in.\n\nMusa asked to see and was told: 'You cannot see Me! But look at the mountain' — and the mountain was levelled (Quran 7:143).\n\n'But He encompasses all vision' — wa huwa yudriku al-absar. The relationship runs one way. Every eye is within His grasp; He is within none.\n\n'The Most Subtle' — al-Latif. The name means both extremely fine, beyond the reach of perception, and gentle in the way He acts — reaching into situations by means too delicate to notice.\n\n'All-Aware' — al-Khabir. Knowledge of the inner detail, as at 6:18.\n\nThe two names together answer the discomfort the verse creates. Not being able to see Him could feel like distance. 'Al-Latif' says the opposite: what cannot be perceived is not absent but too fine to catch.\n\nThe surah has been asking people to read signs. This verse explains why signs are the method: the Maker is not directly available to the eye.",
  "104": "'Indeed, there have come to you insights from your Lord. So whoever chooses to see, it is for their own good. But whoever chooses to be blind, it is to their own loss. And I am not a keeper over you.' (Quran 6:104). The responsibility handed over.\n\n'Insights' — basa'ir. Things that give sight. Not merely evidence, but evidence that opens the eye.\n\n'So whoever chooses to see, it is for their own good' — fa man absara fa li nafsih. And its opposite: 'whoever chooses to be blind, it is to their own loss' — wa man 'amiya fa 'alayha.\n\nThe Arabic is compact and symmetrical: for himself, against himself. Nothing is added to either side, because nothing needs to be.\n\nThe verse describes blindness as something done rather than suffered — the same construction as 5:71, where a people 'became blind and deaf' by their own act.\n\n'And I am not a keeper over you' — wa ma ana 'alaykum bi hafiz. The same release as 6:66, with a different word for keeper.\n\nNote the switch to first person. The whole verse until now has been Allah speaking, and then the last clause is the Prophet's voice.\n\nThat shift puts the two things side by side: the insights come from your Lord, and the man delivering them holds no authority over what you do with them.\n\nIt is a lonely verse, in a way. Everyone reading it has been handed something and left to decide.",
  "105": "'And so We vary our signs to the extent that they will say, \"You have studied ˹previous scriptures˺,\" and We make this ˹Quran˺ clear for people who know.' (Quran 6:105). An accusation predicted.\n\n'We vary our signs' — nusarrifu al-ayat. The third occurrence of this phrase, after 6:46 and 6:65.\n\n'To the extent that they will say, \"You have studied\"' — wa li yaqulu darasta. The accusation is that he learned it from somewhere — from Jewish or Christian teachers, from earlier books.\n\nWhat is striking is that the verse names this reaction as an outcome of the variety of the signs. The more the Quran demonstrates knowledge of earlier scriptures — the eighteen prophets of 6:84 to 6:86, the account of Ibrahim, the confirmation of Musa's Book in 6:91 — the more the charge of borrowing becomes available.\n\nSo the strength of the evidence supplies the objection. That is the same dynamic as 6:7, where a document they could touch would be called magic.\n\n'You have studied' is not a refutation. It is a way of accounting for something impressive without accepting it, which is exactly what 'ancient fables' did in 6:25.\n\n'And We make this clear for people who know' — wa li nubayyinahu li qawmin ya'lamun. The same audience named at 6:97.\n\nThe identical presentation produces an accusation in one listener and clarity in another. The surah has said this from 6:4 onward, and here it says it about its own text.",
  "106": "'˹O Prophet!˺ Follow what is revealed to you from your Lord—there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—and turn away from the polytheists.' (Quran 6:106). An instruction in three parts.\n\n'Follow what is revealed to you' — ittabi' ma uhiya ilayk. The same verb used in 6:50: 'I only follow what is revealed to me.' The Prophet's own description of his position is now given back to him as a command.\n\n'There is no god worthy of worship except Him' — placed in the middle of the sentence, between the instruction to follow and the instruction to turn away. It is the reason for both.\n\n'And turn away from the polytheists' — wa a'rid 'ani al-mushrikin. The verb is ''a'rid', from the same root as the 'i'rad' that this surah has condemned in the deniers since 6:4.\n\nThat is worth noticing carefully. Turning away is wrong when it is turning away from evidence, and right when it is turning away from those who have finally refused it. The act is the same; the object determines everything.\n\nThis is not an instruction to withdraw from people. Verse 6:70 gave the same command — 'leave those who take this faith as mere play' — and immediately added 'yet remind them by this Quran'.\n\nWhat is being ended is the argument, not the relationship.\n\nThere comes a point in every exchange where continuing to argue is no longer service to the truth but attachment to winning. The Prophet is being told, gently, that he has reached it with these particular people.",
  "107": "'Had Allah willed, they would not have been polytheists. We have not appointed you as their keeper, nor are you their maintainer.' (Quran 6:107). The reason behind the instruction of 6:106.\n\n'Had Allah willed, they would not have been polytheists' — wa law sha'a Allahu ma ashraku. The same statement as 6:35: 'Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all.'\n\nThe purpose of saying this is specific, and it is not to explain the metaphysics of guidance. It is to relieve the Prophet of a burden. Their state is not a gap left by his effort.\n\n'We have not appointed you as their keeper' — wa ma ja'alnaka 'alayhim hafiza. The same word as 6:104, and 'wakil' — maintainer — is the same word used at 6:66 and given to Allah at 6:102.\n\nThree times this surah has told him he is not the keeper. Once at 6:66, once at 6:104 in his own voice, and here.\n\nRepetition on that scale is not decoration. It suggests a man who kept picking the responsibility back up.\n\nAnyone who has cared about someone's refusal to see something obvious will recognise the pattern. You explain again, differently, better. You assume the failure is yours.\n\nThe verse takes the file out of his hands. He is neither the guard of their souls nor the manager of their affairs, and the release is given by the One who actually holds both offices.\n\nDeliver. Then let go. That has been the instruction since 5:99.",
  "108": "'˹O believers!˺ Do not insult what they invoke besides Allah or they will insult Allah spitefully out of ignorance...' (Quran 6:108). A rule about how to disagree.\n\nThe command is remarkable for what it protects. Muslims are told not to abuse idols — objects the whole surah has argued are worthless — because of what the abuse would provoke.\n\n'Or they will insult Allah spitefully out of ignorance' — fa yasubbu Allaha 'adwan bi ghayri 'ilm. The calculation is about consequence. Insulting their gods costs them nothing real and results in Allah being insulted.\n\nThat is a striking priority. The truth of the position is not in question; the tactic is. And the tactic is judged by its outcome rather than by how satisfying it feels.\n\nThere is a broader principle here about argument, and it applies far beyond idols. Being right does not license every method of expressing it. Mockery hardens the person it targets, and this surah has shown at 6:43 how easily hearts harden.\n\nAbu Dharr reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'If somebody accuses another of Fusuq or accuses him of Kufr, such an accusation will revert to him if his companion is innocent' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6045).\n\n'This is how We have made each people's deeds appealing to them' — zayyanna li kulli ummatin 'amalahum. The same decoration described at 6:43.\n\n'Then to their Lord is their return' — the judgment is deferred to the one who will make it. Which is also the reason not to pre-empt it with insults.",
  "109": "'They swear by Allah their most solemn oaths that if a sign were to come to them, they would certainly believe in it. Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Signs are only with Allah.\" What will make you ˹believers˺ realize that even if a sign were to come to them, they still would not believe?' (Quran 6:109). The demand for a sign, sworn to this time.\n\n'Their most solemn oaths' — jahda aymanihim. The strongest form of oath available to them.\n\nThis is the fourth time the surah has handled the request: 6:8, 6:37, 6:105 and now. What is new is the sincerity, or the appearance of it. They are not taunting; they are promising.\n\n'Signs are only with Allah' — innama al-ayatu 'inda Allah. The same answer as 6:37, and the same refusal to be drawn.\n\nThen the verse turns and addresses the believers: 'What will make you realize that even if a sign were to come to them, they still would not believe?'\n\nThat turn is significant. The Muslims apparently wanted the sign to be given. They believed the oath, and hoped this would finally work.\n\nThe verse tells them it would not. It is speaking to disappointed hope, not to hostility.\n\nAnyone who has wished for one clear event to convince a person they love has stood exactly where those Companions stood. The verse does not scold them for the wish.\n\nIt tells them what the surah has demonstrated since 6:7, when a revelation they could touch was still called magic. The obstacle was never the evidence.",
  "110": "'We turn their hearts and eyes away ˹from the truth˺ as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance.' (Quran 6:110). The explanation of 6:109.\n\n'As they refused to believe at first' — kama lam yu'minu bihi awwala marrah. This clause is the key to the verse and it should not be read past.\n\nThe turning away of hearts and eyes is stated as a consequence, and the cause named is their own first refusal. The order is: they refused, then this happened.\n\nThat is the same sequence the surah gave at 6:25, where veils were cast over hearts after the behaviour described in 6:4 — turning away from every sign that came.\n\n'We turn their hearts and eyes' — wa nuqallibu af'idatahum wa absarahum. The verb is 'qallaba', to overturn, to flip.\n\nSo the capacity is not merely blocked. It is inverted, which explains 6:7 and 6:105: evidence arrives and is processed into an objection.\n\n'Leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance' — wa nadharuhum fi tughyanihim ya'mahun. 'Tughyan' is overflowing past a boundary. 'Ya'mahun' is to wander without direction.\n\nNote that they are 'left'. Not pushed, not driven — released to continue.\n\nThat is the most frightening possibility this surah raises, and it was raised already at 6:44: doors opened, everything given, no interruption. Being left alone with your own direction is a form of judgment that feels, at the time, like freedom.",
  "111": "'Even if We had sent them the angels, made the dead speak to them, and assembled before their own eyes every sign ˹they demanded˺, they still would not have believed— unless Allah so willed. But most of them are ignorant ˹of this˺.' (Quran 6:111). The oath of 6:109 tested against three impossibilities.\n\nAngels sent down — the exact demand of 6:8. The dead made to speak — the strongest evidence conceivable for the resurrection they denied in 6:29. And then every sign at once, gathered in front of them.\n\n'They still would not have believed' — ma kanu li yu'minu.\n\nThat is a hard claim, and the surah has spent a hundred verses earning it. Verse 6:7 showed a touchable revelation being called magic. Verse 6:28 said a second chance would change nothing. Verse 6:110 explained the mechanism.\n\n'Unless Allah so willed' — illa an yasha'a Allah. The exception is preserved, and it is the same exception as 6:107. Nothing here says any individual is beyond reach.\n\nWhat it says is that evidence does not do the work people imagine it does. Belief is not the automatic product of sufficient data.\n\n'But most of them are ignorant of this' — wa lakinna aktharahum yajhalun. What they do not know is this fact about themselves.\n\nEveryone believes that they would accept the truth if only it were shown clearly enough. The verse says this is the most common thing people are wrong about.\n\nAnd 41:53 gives the promise that answers it: the signs will be shown, in the universe and within themselves.",
  "112": "'And so We have made for every prophet enemies—devilish humans and jinn—whispering to one another with elegant words of deception. Had it been your Lord's Will, they would not have done such a thing. So leave them and their deceit,' (Quran 6:112). Opposition described as standard equipment.\n\n'For every prophet' — li kulli nabiyyin 'aduwwan. Not a misfortune particular to this mission. The same statement is at 25:31.\n\nThat is the consolation offered at 6:10 and 6:34, now given a structural explanation. Enemies are part of the arrangement.\n\n'Devilish humans and jinn' — shayatina al-insi wa al-jinn. The category 'shaytan' is applied to human beings here. Whoever does the work is named for the work.\n\n'Whispering to one another with elegant words of deception' — yuhi ba'duhum ila ba'din zukhrufa al-qawli ghurura. 'Zukhruf' is gilding, ornamentation applied to a surface.\n\nThe description is precise and unflattering. Opposition to truth rarely arrives looking crude. It arrives well phrased, and the verse says the phrasing is the point — decoration over an empty claim.\n\nThat is the same 'ghurur' — deception by appearance — that 6:70 said the worldly life produces.\n\n'Had it been your Lord's Will, they would not have done such a thing' — the same clause as 6:107, and the same purpose: to stop the Prophet from treating the opposition as his failure.\n\n'So leave them and their deceit' — fa dharhum wa ma yaftarun. The instruction of 6:106, repeated.\n\nElegant falsehood cannot be out-argued by anyone determined to win. It can only be left.",
  "113": "'so that the hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter may be receptive to it, be pleased with it, and be persistent in their evil pursuits.' (Quran 6:113). The purpose of the gilded speech described in 6:112.\n\nThree stages are named, in order.\n\n'May be receptive to it' — li tasgha ilayhi. 'Sagha' is to incline the ear toward something. First, a leaning.\n\n'Be pleased with it' — wa li yardawh. Then approval. What was merely interesting becomes something they endorse.\n\n'And be persistent in their evil pursuits' — wa li yaqtarifu ma hum muqtarifun. Then the behaviour continues, now with a justification attached.\n\nThat is the whole architecture of self-deception in three steps, and it is worth memorising because it describes something universal. Nobody adopts a false position all at once. They listen, then they like, then they act, and by the last stage the position feels like their own conclusion.\n\n'Those who disbelieve in the Hereafter' — alladhina la yu'minuna bi al-akhirah. This is the fourth time the surah has identified denial of the Hereafter as the root, after 6:29, 6:31 and 6:92.\n\nThe decorated speech does not create the disposition; it finds it. Verse 6:112 said the whisperers were human and jinn, and here it is clear why they succeed: they are offering people what they already wanted to hear.\n\nThe defence, then, is not cleverness. It is caring about the outcome enough that flattering arguments have nothing to grip.",
  "114": "'˹Say, O Prophet,˺ \"Should I seek a judge other than Allah while He is the One Who has revealed for you the Book ˹with the truth˺ perfectly explained?\"...' (Quran 6:114). A question after the descriptions of falsehood in 6:112 and 6:113.\n\n'A judge' — hakaman. Someone to arbitrate the dispute. They wanted a third party, a standard both sides could appeal to.\n\nThe reply asks what could possibly serve, given that the Book has already been sent.\n\n'Perfectly explained' — mufassalan. From 'tafsil', the same word used at 6:55 for making the signs distinct. The Book is not a set of hints requiring an interpreter with special access.\n\n'Those who were given the Scripture know that it has been revealed from your Lord in truth' — the same claim as 6:20, where they recognised it 'as they recognize their own children'.\n\nThat is offered here as corroboration, and it is a specific kind. The people with the most reason to reject a new prophet are cited as knowing.\n\n'So do not be one of those who doubt' — fa la takunanna mina al-mumtarin. Addressed to the Prophet, as 6:35 and 6:114 both are.\n\nThe Quran does not shy from addressing him this way. It did the same at the end of 6:14 — 'not be one of the polytheists' — and at 6:35. The instruction is not an accusation; it is what it sounds like to be steadied by someone who knows how heavy the work is.\n\nDoubt in this setting is not intellectual. It is the erosion that comes from years of being disbelieved.",
  "115": "'The Word of your Lord has been perfected in truth and justice. None can change His Words. And He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 6:115). The answer to the search for a judge.\n\n'Has been perfected' — tammat. Completed, brought to fullness. Nothing is missing from it and nothing further is required.\n\n'In truth and justice' — sidqan wa 'adla. Two qualities, covering two things a judgment must have. 'Sidq' is correspondence to reality — the account is accurate. ''Adl' is fairness — the ruling is right.\n\nA statement can be true and unjust in its application, or well-intentioned and false. The verse claims both.\n\n'None can change His Words' — la mubaddila li kalimatih. The same clause appeared at 6:34, where it guaranteed His promise of help. Here it guarantees the standard itself.\n\nThat matters after 6:91, which described people who split scripture into sheets, 'revealing some and hiding much'. Handling can be corrupted; the Word cannot.\n\n'And He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing' — al-Sami'u al-'Alim. The third appearance in this surah, after 6:13.\n\nThe two names are placed at the end of a verse about judgment for a reason. A judge needs to hear the case and know the facts. Human courts fail on both counts routinely, which is why people seek arbitrators and then dispute the arbitrators.\n\nThe verse says the search can stop. The standard is complete, unalterable, and held by One who missed nothing — down to the falling leaf of 6:59.",
  "116": "'˹O Prophet!˺ If you were to obey most of those on earth, they would lead you away from Allah's Way. They follow nothing but assumptions and do nothing but lie.' (Quran 6:116). A warning about majorities.\n\n'Most of those on earth' — akthara man fi al-ard. The verse names sheer numbers as the danger, and it is one of the most quietly radical statements in the Quran.\n\nEvery society treats the majority view as a form of evidence. This verse says it is not evidence of anything.\n\n'They follow nothing but assumptions' — in yattabi'una illa al-zann. 'Zann' is supposition — belief held without grounds. The Quran repeats the diagnosis at 10:36: 'surely assumptions can in no way replace the truth.'\n\nThat is the mechanism. Most people do not investigate; they absorb. Verse 6:104 said the same: they inherited their positions 'out of ignorance'.\n\n'And do nothing but lie' — wa in hum illa yakhrusun. 'Kharasa' is to guess at a quantity, as one estimates the yield of a date palm by eye. Guesswork presented as knowledge.\n\nThe previous surah made the same point from another angle at 5:100: 'Good and evil are not equal, though you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil.'\n\nThere is a hard discipline in this for anyone who wants to be reasonable. Counting is easy and thinking is not, and the verse withdraws the comfort of the crowd entirely.\n\nWhat replaces it is named in the next verse: the only tally that exists is Allah's.",
  "117": "'Indeed, your Lord knows best who has strayed from His Way and who is ˹rightly˺ guided.' (Quran 6:117). The correction to 6:116, and to the reader's use of it.\n\nVerse 6:116 has just said most people are wrong. The natural next thought is to start sorting people, and this verse arrives before that thought can settle.\n\n'Your Lord knows best' — inna rabbaka huwa a'lam. The knowledge of who is astray belongs to Him.\n\nThat is a necessary limit on the previous verse. A statement that the majority is misguided could easily become a licence for contempt, and the surah has already forbidden contempt in practice at 6:52 — do not dismiss the poor believers — and at 6:108 — do not insult their gods.\n\nSo 6:116 tells you not to follow the crowd. This tells you not to judge it.\n\nThose two instructions together are harder than either alone. Independence without arrogance is a narrow path, and most people manage one or the other.\n\n'Who has strayed from His Way and who is rightly guided' — both categories are named, which means the uncertainty runs both ways. You cannot be certain about another person's ruin or about their safety.\n\nThe Prophet was told at 6:107 that he was not their keeper, and this is the epistemological form of the same release. Not only is their state not your responsibility; it is not even your information.\n\nWhat remains yours is your own conduct, which the next verses turn to immediately.",
  "118": "'So eat only of what is slaughtered in Allah's Name if you truly believe in His revelations.' (Quran 6:118). After a hundred verses of argument, a rule about meat.\n\nThe shift seems abrupt and it is not. The whole surah has been about who has the right to declare things lawful and forbidden, and 6:119 to 6:150 will show the pagans doing exactly that with their livestock.\n\n'What is slaughtered in Allah's Name' — mimma dhukira ismu Allahi 'alayh. Literally: over which Allah's name was mentioned.\n\nThe requirement is a spoken sentence at the moment of killing. Nothing about the animal changes; what changes is the acknowledgement.\n\nThat is the entire content of the ruling, and it is a training in the surah's main theme. Verse 6:102 said He is 'the Creator of all things, so worship Him alone'. Naming Him over the animal is that principle applied at the point of eating.\n\nRafi' bin Khadij reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'If the killing tool causes blood to gush out and if Allah's Name is mentioned, eat of the slaughtered animal. But do not slaughter with a tooth or a nail' (Sahih al-Bukhari 5498).\n\n'If you truly believe in His revelations' — in kuntum bi ayatihi mu'minin. The condition is stated as a test of the belief, not as a threat.\n\nBelief that never reaches the kitchen is what this verse is checking for.",
  "119": "'Why should you not eat of what is slaughtered in Allah's Name when He has already explained to you what He has forbidden to you—except when compelled by necessity?...' (Quran 6:119). The rule of 6:118 protected from over-application.\n\nThe question is aimed at scrupulousness, not laxity. Some believers were becoming hesitant about meat generally, and the verse asks why.\n\n'He has already explained to you what He has forbidden' — wa qad fassala lakum ma harrama 'alaykum. The prohibited list is short and it is closed. It is given at 2:173, 5:3 and 16:115: carrion, blood, swine, and what is dedicated to other than Allah.\n\nEverything outside that list is permitted, and treating it otherwise is the error 5:87 named: 'Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you.'\n\n'Except when compelled by necessity' — illa ma udturirtum ilayh. Even the closed list opens under duress. There is no situation in which a person must starve.\n\n'Many certainly mislead others by their whims out of ignorance' — bi ahwa'ihim bi ghayri 'ilm. 'Hawa' again — desire, not reasoning. The same diagnosis as 6:56 and 6:71.\n\nNote that the misleading here is in the direction of extra prohibition. Desire does not only pull toward indulgence; it also pulls toward the satisfaction of being stricter than everyone else.\n\n'Surely your Lord knows the transgressors best' — the same closing as 6:117.\n\nThe line is drawn by revelation. Both sides of it are wrong to move.",
  "120": "'Shun all sin—open and secret. Indeed, those who commit sin will be punished for what they earn.' (Quran 6:120). A single sentence placed in the middle of the food rulings.\n\n'Open and secret' — zahirahu wa batinah. Its outside and its inside.\n\nThe pairing is the point, and it has run through the surah since 6:3: 'He knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal.'\n\nPlacing it here, between rules about slaughtering, is deliberate. A person can become scrupulous about the visible requirement — the name spoken over the animal — and careless about everything unseen.\n\nThat is a recognisable religious failure. Verse 6:118 gave a rule that is public and checkable, and this verse immediately widens the frame so that observable compliance cannot become the whole of piety.\n\n'Sin' — ithm. The general word, not a specific offence. Nothing is excluded.\n\n'Will be punished for what they earn' — sayujzawna bima kanu yaqtarifun. 'Iqtiraf' is acquisition, the same verb used at 6:113 for persisting in evil pursuits.\n\nThe Quran's consistent metaphor for deeds in this surah is earning and carrying. Verse 6:31 had them borne on the back; 6:3 called them 'ma taksibun'.\n\nWhat you do in private is not a smaller version of what you do in public. It is the same acquisition, entered in the same account, described in 6:59 as a Record that misses nothing 'green or dry'.",
  "121": "'Do not eat of what is not slaughtered in Allah's Name. For that would certainly be ˹an act of˺ disobedience. Surely the devils whisper to their ˹human˺ associates to argue with you. If you were to obey them, then you ˹too˺ would be polytheists.' (Quran 6:121). The rule restated, and the argument against it named.\n\n'Surely the devils whisper to their human associates to argue with you' — la yuhuna ila awliya'ihim li yujadilukum. This is the whispering of 6:112, now applied to a specific dispute.\n\nThe argument they were being fed is recorded by the commentators: you eat what you kill yourselves, but not what Allah kills? Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, records the pagans challenging the Muslims over carrion in precisely those terms.\n\nIt is a clever argument. It sounds pious, and it puts the believer on the defensive.\n\n'If you were to obey them, then you too would be polytheists' — innakum la mushrikun. The consequence is stated at maximum severity, and the reason is the surah's whole theme.\n\nAccepting a ruling on what is lawful from someone other than Allah is treating them as a legislator. That is what shirk is in the domain of law, and 6:114 has just asked: 'Should I seek a judge other than Allah?'\n\nThe severity is not about the meat. It is about who decides.\n\nNotice too that the verse does not answer the clever argument. It identifies the source and the consequence and stops, which is the same treatment given to the demand for signs.\n\nSome arguments are not answered because answering them concedes the premise.",
  "122": "'Can those who had been dead, to whom We gave life and a light with which they can walk among people, be compared to those in complete darkness from which they can never emerge?...' (Quran 6:122). A comparison, and a description of what guidance actually does.\n\n'Those who had been dead' — man kana maytan fa ahyaynah. The believer's earlier state is called death, the same word used at 6:36 for those who cannot respond.\n\nThat is a striking way to describe a person's life before guidance, and it is stated as personal history rather than insult. Whoever is now walking was once lying still.\n\n'A light with which they can walk among people' — nuran yamshi bihi fi al-nas. Note where the light is used: among people. Not in seclusion, not in the mosque. It is a light for navigating ordinary human company, which is exactly what the surah has been teaching — how to sit with mockers, how to argue, when to leave, whom not to insult.\n\nLight and darkness were the surah's opening image at 6:1: one light, many darknesses.\n\n'From which they can never emerge' — laysa bi kharijin minha. The darkness here is described as enclosed, which recalls 24:40: 'Darkness upon darkness! If one stretches out their hand, they can hardly see it.'\n\n'That is how the misdeeds of the disbelievers have been made appealing to them' — the decoration of 6:43 and 6:108, appearing a third time.\n\nA person in the dark who thinks he can see has no reason to look for a lamp.",
  "123": "'And so We have placed in every society the most wicked to conspire in it. Yet they plot only against themselves, but they fail to perceive it.' (Quran 6:123). A pattern in how communities go wrong.\n\n'The most wicked' — akabira mujrimiha. Literally: its greatest criminals. And 'akabir' also means its great ones, its leaders.\n\nBoth senses are active, and the overlap is the observation. In a corrupted society, the people at the top and the people doing the most damage tend to be the same people.\n\n'To conspire in it' — li yamkuru fiha. 'Makr' is scheming — planning that works by concealment.\n\nThis explains something the surah has been showing since 6:52, where the Meccan elite demanded the poor believers be sent away. Opposition to prophets is consistently led from the top, because the message threatens an arrangement that the top benefits from.\n\n'Yet they plot only against themselves' — wa ma yamkuruna illa bi anfusihim. The scheming lands on the schemer.\n\n'But they fail to perceive it' — wa ma yash'urun. The exact phrase used at 6:26, of people who turned others away from the Prophet and 'ruin none but themselves'.\n\nThat is the fourth or fifth time the surah has insisted that harm done to truth returns to the doer. Verses 6:12, 6:20, 6:26, 6:31 and now this.\n\nWhat makes it bearable to read is the phrase 'in every society'. This is not a charge against one people. It is a description of how power behaves.",
  "124": "'Whenever a sign comes to them, they say, \"We will never believe until we receive what Allah's messengers received.\" Allah knows best where to place His message...' (Quran 6:124). The last form of the demand for a sign.\n\n'Until we receive what Allah's messengers received' — hatta nu'ta mithla ma utiya rusulu Allah. They want prophethood themselves.\n\nThe Quran records the objection in its blunter form at 43:31: 'If only this Quran was revealed to a great man from one of the two cities!' The complaint was about the recipient.\n\n'Allah knows best where to place His message' — Allahu a'lamu haythu yaj'alu risalatah. The reply is a single sentence and it settles the matter.\n\n'Where to place' — the word 'haythu' is about location. Prophethood is placed somewhere, by someone who can see the whole terrain, which 6:59 described as knowing every seed 'in the darkness of the earth'.\n\nThey were assessing candidates by wealth and clan standing. Verse 6:53 already exposed that instinct — 'Has Allah favoured these out of all of us?' — and answered it: 'Does Allah not best recognize the grateful?'\n\n'The wicked will soon be overwhelmed by humiliation from Allah' — saghar. Smallness, being made little.\n\nThe punishment fits precisely. Their complaint was about status, and what comes is the removal of it.\n\nWanting to be the one chosen is a very old form of refusing the one who was.",
  "125": "'Whoever Allah wills to guide, He opens their heart to Islam. But whoever He wills to leave astray, He makes their chest tight and constricted as if they were climbing up into the sky...' (Quran 6:125). Guidance described as a physical sensation.\n\n'He opens their heart' — yashrah sadrahu lil-islam. 'Sharh' is expansion, opening something out. The same word is used of the Prophet at 94:1: 'Have We not uplifted your heart for you?'\n\nThat is what accepting truth feels like from inside. Not the arrival of information but the widening of a space.\n\n'He makes their chest tight and constricted' — dayyiqan harajan. Two words for narrowness, stacked.\n\n'As if they were climbing up into the sky' — ka annama yassa''adu fi al-sama'. This is the striking part. The image is of someone ascending, and the higher he goes the less he can breathe.\n\nA seventh-century audience knew this from mountains. The comparison describes the specific suffocation of a person for whom every step toward the truth makes the air thinner.\n\nThat is an accurate description of what religion feels like to someone resisting it. Every requirement is a weight, every reminder an oppression. The same instruction that opens one chest closes another.\n\nNote the sequence the surah has already established at 6:110: this constriction follows refusal rather than preceding it.\n\n'This is how Allah dooms those who disbelieve' — yaj'alu Allahu al-rijsa 'ala alladhina la yu'minun.\n\nThe state is the sentence. Nothing further needs to be added.",
  "126": "'That is your Lord's Path—perfectly straight. We have already made the signs clear to those who are mindful.' (Quran 6:126). The path named after the two conditions of 6:125.\n\n'Perfectly straight' — mustaqiman. The same word used at 6:39 and 6:87 for the Straight Path, and it is the request of every Muslim in every prayer.\n\n'Straight' means without deviation, and it also means direct — the shortest route between two points. A straight path is not the easiest; it is the one that does not waste the traveller's steps.\n\n'That is your Lord's Path' — hadha siratu rabbika. Not a path to your Lord but His path, belonging to Him, which is why 6:153 will forbid taking any other.\n\nThe singular is doing the same work it has done since 6:1: one light, many darknesses. One straight road, many that wander.\n\n'We have already made the signs clear' — qad fassalna al-ayat. 'Tafsil' for the fourth time in this surah, after 6:55, 6:97 to 6:99, and 6:114.\n\n'To those who are mindful' — li qawmin yadhdhakkarun. Those who take a reminder — the fourth capacity named in this series, after knowing (6:97), comprehending (6:98) and believing (6:99).\n\nEach of those closings pairs the same evidence with a different faculty. The signs do not change. What the audience brings decides what they yield.\n\nThat is the honest summary of everything from 6:95 to here. The clarity has been supplied. The receiving is not automatic, and 6:125 has just described what it feels like when it happens.",
  "127": "“They will have the Home of Peace with their Lord, Who will be their Guardian because of what they used to do” announces the reward for those who travel the perfectly straight path that has been made clear to the mindful. After describing the straight path of your Lord and calling people to heed its varied signs, the Quran now shows where that path ends. The “Home of Peace,” or “Dar al-Salam,” is a name for Paradise, the abode where peace is complete, free from fear, grief, torment, and constriction of the chest. “Salam” is also one of Allah’s names, the Source of Peace, so the Home is His home of peace, sheltered by His own attribute. Its opposite, the boiling drink and painful punishment for those who take faith as play, was warned earlier; here is its contrast for the guided.\n\nThe verse adds “with their Lord,” or “inda Rabbihim,” near their nurturing Lord, implying nearness, honor, and special companionship, not merely residence. And “Who will be their Guardian because of what they used to do,” or “wa huwa waliyyuhum bima kanu ya‘maloon,” shows reciprocal loyalty. In this world they took no guardian other than Allah, as Abraham and the Prophet were taught, and they invoked their Lord morning and evening seeking His Face. In return, Allah becomes their Wali, ally and protector, in the Home where no protector other than Him exists on the Day of Gathering. The phrase “because of what they used to do,” or “bima kanu ya‘maloon,” links reward to deed, not as wages that force payment but as the fitting recompense for a path walked sincerely, where faith untainted by shirk was paired with righteous action, like guarding prayers and shunning sin open and secret.\n\nThis promise connects deeply to the earlier assurance that whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them nor will they grieve. The Home of Peace is that promise fulfilled: no fear of what is ahead, no grief for what is behind, only lasting peace. A common misunderstanding is to think peace in the Hereafter is automatic regardless of path. The verse shows peace is the home of a road traveled, the straight path kept while signs were clear, when the heart remained spacious rather than tight. Remembering this home makes constriction feel temporary and opening feel like a foretaste, encouraging daily choices, from mentioning Allah’s Name over food to walking with a light among people, that lead to the Guardian whose maintenance never fails and whose Word is perfected in justice and truth.",
  "128": "“˹Consider˺ the Day He will gather them ˹all˺ together and say, ‘O assembly of jinn! You misled humans in great numbers’” returns to the gathering that has been the surah’s constant warning, now exposing the partnership between misleading jinn and deceived humans. After promising the Home of Peace for the mindful who walk the straight path and have Allah as their Guardian, the Quran shows the opposite gathering for those who followed devils’ elegant deception. The Day He will gather them all together, or “yawma yahshuruhum jamee‘an,” is the Day about which there is no doubt, when every leaf and grain’s record is brought and when they are returned to their True Master for the swiftest reckoning. On that comprehensive gathering, the call goes first to the hidden misleaders: “O assembly of jinn! You misled humans in great numbers,” or “ya ma‘shara al-jin qad-istakthartum min al-ins,” you took many of the humans, you multiplied them around you, you made their following abundant.\n\nTheir human associates answer, confessing the mutual benefit that made misguidance pleasant: “And their human associates will say, ‘Our Lord! We benefited from each other’s company, but now we have reached the term which You appointed for us.’” “Awliya’uhum min al-ins” means the humans who were allies of jinn, close associates who served each other. Humans benefited from jinn through promised protection, fortune-telling, and decorated arguments that made evil seem wise; jinn benefited from humans through worship, obedience, and the spread of their whispering. Now, at the term, or “ajal,” which You appointed, the appointed time that was already described as a set place for every matter, the benefit has ended and only liability remains.\n\nThe verdict follows: “Then He will say, ‘The Fire is your home, yours to stay in forever, except whoever Allah wills to spare.’ Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.” “Mathwakum al-Nar,” the Fire is your abode to dwell, the opposite of the Home of Peace just promised. “Khalideena fiha,” to stay forever, indicates long, enduring stay, with the exception “except whoever Allah wills,” preserving divine will, as earlier noted that signs are only with Him and that opening and constricting chests are by His wise will. The closing names seal the judgment: the sentence issues from the All-Wise who knows where His message is placed and whose Word is perfected, and the All-Knowing who knows best who strayed.\n\nA mistaken notion is that involvement with hidden beings is trivial amusement. This verse shows it creates alliance and liability that ends in a common gathering where both sides confess mutual use and then are sent to a shared Fire, while the mindful find peace with their Guardian instead of this alliance.",
  "129": "“This is how We make the wrongdoers ˹destructive˺ allies of one another because of their misdeeds” explains the divine law behind the joint gathering of jinn and humans just shown confessing mutual benefit before being sent to the Fire. The previous scene had the jinn accused of misleading many humans, and humans admitting they profited from the alliance until their appointed term. Now the Quran states the pattern behind it: “This is how We make the wrongdoers allies of one another,” or “wa kathalika nuwalli ba‘da al-zalimeena ba‘dan.” The word “nuwalli” means to make allies, to put in charge over one another, to let protect and direct one another. Wrongdoers, or “zalimeen,” those who fabricate lies, deny signs, and follow whim without knowledge, are allowed to become each other’s guardians, reinforcing one another’s wrong.\n\nThe cause is then named: “because of their misdeeds,” or “bima kanu yaksiboon,” due to what they used to earn. The earning of sin, earlier called acquiring sin and earning with claims, produces a social consequence: those who earn similar wrong find each other, validate each other, and entrench each other. Earlier the verse said misdeeds were made appealing to disbelievers and that each people’s deeds were made appealing to them; the ally-ship consolidates that appeal into a network where a human devil and a jinn devil whisper elegantly to one another, and where wicked chiefs conspire in every town.\n\nBut the same law also implies security for the opposite earners. As wrongdoers ally with wrongdoers, the faithful ally with the faithful, and ultimately Allah Himself is the Guardian of those headed for the Home of Peace because of what they used to do. The choice of ally therefore shapes destination. A common misunderstanding is to view bad company as incidental or harmless entertainment. This verse shows company has a structuring power by divine leave; consistent earning of wrong naturally leads to being made the ally of those similar, which then multiplies the earning, until on the Day the company is gathered together before the True Master as a bloc.\n\nConnecting back, this confirms why obeying most people who follow assumption would lead away from Allah’s Way, why leaving mocking gatherings preserves the heart, and why seeing with insights from your Lord is for your own good. Choosing the straight path early prevents being placed under the guardianship of fellow wrongdoers later, keeping the heart open to Islam rather than tight as if climbing into the sky, and keeping the Home of Peace within reach through the One who alone is judge.",
  "130": "“O assembly of jinn and humans! Did messengers not come from among you, proclaiming My revelations and warning you of the coming of this Day of yours?” is the questioning that follows the exposure of wrongdoers made allies of one another, now holding both groups to account together. The previous verse explained how persistent earning of sin produces mutual alliance; this verse shows what proof was given to both allies while they were earning. The caller is Allah, the True Master to whom all are returned. He asks both assemblies together, “Did messengers not come from among you,” or “alam ya’tikum rusulun minkum.” Messengers were human, but their message was for jinn as well, as authentic accounts show jinn listening to the Prophet recite and then warning their own people. The phrase “from among you” touches the mercy that messengers share language, culture, and human experience with their immediate audience, removing excuse that guidance was foreign.\n\nWhat did those messengers do? “Proclaiming My revelations,” or “yaqussoona alaikum ayati,” recounting, narrating My signs clearly, as earlier said Allah declares the truth and is the Best of Judges who recounts it, and “warning you of the coming of this Day,” or “wa yunthiroonakum liqa’a yawmikum hatha.” “Liqa” means meeting, encounter, the same meeting earlier called the meeting with Allah that losers deny and that the mindful believe will come. That meeting is now called “this Day of yours,” making it imminent, as if standing before them.\n\nTheir answer is immediate and self-incriminating: “They will say, ‘We confess against ourselves!’ For they have been deluded by their worldly life. And they will testify against themselves that they were disbelievers,” or “shahidna ala anfusina… wa shahidoo ala anfusihim annahum kanoo kafireen.” The worldly life, earlier described as play and amusement, had deluded, or “gharrathum,” tricked them, making deeds seem appealing and whispering seem elegant. On the Day, deception is stripped away, and testimony that matches reality emerges, just as earlier their fabricated gods failed and their ties were broken, and they arrived alone leaving behind everything they were given.\n\nA common self-deception is to think that being unaware excuses persistent wrong, but the verse shows messengers did come and warnings were delivered through the blessed Book that warns the Mother of Cities. Proof was established before alliance with wrongdoers hardened, confirming that Allah does not destroy a society while its people are unaware, and that the Word perfected in truth and justice leaves no one without a warner.",
  "131": "“This ˹sending of the messengers˺ is because your Lord would never destroy a society for their wrongdoing while its people are unaware ˹of the truth˺” gives the divine reason for the question just asked on the Day when jinn and humans testified against themselves. The previous verse showed every denier confessing that warners had indeed come, proclaiming revelations and warning of the meeting. Now the Quran explains why warners inevitably come before punishment. The phrase “This sending is because your Lord would never destroy,” or “thalika an lam yakun Rabbuka muhlika al-qura bi-zulmin wa ahluha ghafilon,” contains two intertwined assurances. “Qura” means towns, societies, the broader communities that otherwise might be uprooted as earlier wrongdoers were uprooted and praised for. Destruction, whether worldly uprooting or ultimate Fire after the gathering, never happens “while its people are unaware,” or “wa ahluha ghafilon,” heedless, without having been informed, warned, and made able to respond.\n\nThe wording “while its people are unaware” does not mean they are declared innocent if they were never formally addressed; it means Allah in His justice ensures that the apparatus of awareness is provided. Messengers come, insights arrive from the Lord, the Book is revealed perfectly explained, signs are varied so the way of the wicked becomes distinct and so people who comprehend will grasp. Even if a formal messenger did not physically reach an isolated person, natural signs and the innate call to the Originator provide awareness that only Allah fully judges, as He knows best where to place His message and who is truly receptive. The principle establishes fairness: no society is ruined for wrongdoing without first being given a clear chance to choose the straight path that is your Lord’s Path.\n\nThis explains the repeated patience in the surah, where messengers were rejected but endured until help came, where the Prophet was told he is not a keeper but a warner with this Quran for the Mother of Cities and around it, where hearts tight as if climbing into the sky were warned before being left to wander. The mercy of the Lord who took mercy upon Himself is shown precisely in sending warners before destroyers.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think divine justice is like human hasty retaliation. The Quran shows the opposite: even the plotting of the greatest wicked, the alliance of wrongdoers with each other, and the following of most people’s assumptions are met first with detailed clarification, not immediate uprooting. Only after the reminder is forgotten, rejoiced pride appears, and ties break at the appointed term does the final destruction or Fire become the due home, and even then the ranks assigned are exactly according to what each earned, as the next verse clarifies.",
  "132": "“They will each be assigned ranks according to their deeds. And your Lord is not unaware of what they do” follows the assurance that no society is destroyed while its people remain unaware. After establishing that messengers come proclaiming revelations and warning of the meeting, the Quran now explains how that awareness translates into precise justice. The phrase “They will each be assigned ranks according to their deeds,” or “wa li-kullin darajatun mimma amiloo,” pictures a ladder of degrees, like steps in a building where height corresponds exactly to what was built. The same word “darajat” was used when Abraham was elevated in rank and when wrongdoers were uprooted. Here it becomes universal: every person, jinn and human, will receive a degree that matches what they earned, whether that earning was shunning sin open and secret, establishing prayer, or fabricating lies and taking faith as play.\n\nThis ranking gives meaning to the diversity of deeds described throughout the surah. The attentive who respond, the ones who invoke morning and evening seeking His Face, those given light to walk among people, and those who persist in elegant deception each occupy different steps, and the steps are not assigned by tribe or wealth but by deeds known to the most precise observer. The second phrase confirms that precision: “And your Lord is not unaware of what they do,” or “wa ma Rabbuka bi-ghafilin amma ya‘maloon.” “Ghafil” means heedless, forgetful, inattentive. Your nurturing Lord is never heedless, unlike the heedless societies who forget reminders. He knows what is done by day, what is called back by night, what is whispered among devils, and what is hidden in chests tight as if climbing into the sky.\n\nThe connection to the next verse about being able to replace one generation with another shows that ranking does not affect His self-sufficiency. Whether people rise or fall in rank, He remains the Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think ranking in the Hereafter is vague or collective, as if people are simply sorted as good or bad. This verse shows it is finely graded, with each rank reflecting exact deeds. Another mistake is to imagine that forgotten deeds are lost. The verse says they are not, because the Lord who encompasses all vision and whose word is perfected in truth and justice writes even the fall of a leaf. Remembering assigned ranks encourages daily attentiveness: every mention of Allah’s Name over food, every turn away from mockery, every shunning of secret sin, shifts one’s rank slightly upward toward the Home of Peace, while every shared lie shared with wrongdoers shifts it downward, and none of it escapes the One who is never unaware.",
  "133": "“Your Lord is the Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy. If He wills, He can do away with you and replace you with whoever He wills, just as He produced you from the offspring of other people” places the newly stated principle of ranked justice within the larger reality of divine independence and mercy. After saying each will have ranks according to deeds and that the Lord is never unaware, the Quran reminds listeners that ranking is not needed to benefit Allah. The phrase “Your Lord is the Self-Sufficient,” or “wa Rabbuka al-Ghaniyy,” means completely free of need, possessing all, as earlier said He provides for all and is not in need of provision. Combined with “Full of Mercy,” or “Thoo al-rahmah,” Owner of profound mercy, it presents a balance echoed throughout this surah, where the Lord who took mercy upon Himself and perfected His word in justice is not a needy ruler who must keep subjects at any cost.\n\nThe next sentence makes that independence concrete: “If He wills, He can do away with you and replace you with whoever He wills,” or “in yasha’ yuthhibkum wa yastakhlif min ba‘dikum ma yasha’.” The verb “yuthhibkum” means to make go away, to remove, and “yastakhlif” means to bring others as successors, just as earlier He uprooted wrongdoers completely. The ability to remove and replace was shown in history when arrogant nations were destroyed and other peoples took their place, as when the Quran earlier reminded, “We had made them more established than you yet We destroyed them for sins and replaced them with other peoples.” No generation is indispensable to Allah’s purpose; guidance will continue because there exists a people entrusted who will never disbelieve.\n\nThe likeness given is humbling: “just as He produced you from the offspring of other people,” or “kama ansha’akum min thurriyati qawmin akhareen.” You yourselves arrived as successors. You were produced from previous peoples’ descendants, given a place to live and a place to be laid to rest, originating from a single soul. Your presence is itself an instance of replacement and mercy.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think humanity’s existence guarantees continuation. The verse says continuation depends on will and mercy. Another error is to imagine that threat of replacement is meant to instill despair. Its purpose is to awaken gratitude and responsibility before ranks are assigned. Since Allah is both Self-Sufficient and Full of Mercy, the call is not to earn His need but to accept His mercy by choosing the spacious opened heart over the tight chest, following the straight path made clear to the mindful, so that if replacement happens it is not because we were worthless to Him but because we chose the darkness from which one can never emerge.",
  "134": "“Indeed, what you have been promised will certainly come to pass. And you will have no escape” brings the warning of replacement and ranking to its inevitable conclusion. After reminding that your Lord is Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy, able to do away with you and replace you as He produced you from others, the verse now states the certainty of the promised outcome. The phrase “what you have been promised,” or “ma too‘adoon,” includes both sides of the promise that has echoed throughout the surah: gathering for the Day about which there is no doubt, the tremendous Day whose torment the Prophet feared, the absolute triumph for those spared, the absolute loss for losers whose burdens weigh on backs, the Home of Peace for the mindful, and the Fire for the alliance of wrongdoers. The Arabic emphasizes with “la-aatin,” surely coming, certainly arriving, without doubt or delay beyond its appointed term.\n\nThe second phrase closes every loophole: “And you will have no escape,” or “wa ma antum bi-mu‘jizeen.” The word “mu‘jiz” means one who frustrates, incapacitates, renders helpless, escapes. Humans cannot outrun, outwit, or outlast the gathering, just as earlier you cannot escape being returned to Allah when angels take the soul without neglecting, and just as no vision can encompass Him while He encompasses all vision. Even if a generation is replaced, the promise pursues each individual beyond the replacement, because each rank is personal and your Lord is not unaware.\n\nThis verse answers the quiet rationalization that allowed earlier peoples to forget reminders and become proud when doors of everything opened: that time itself would dilute the warning. The Quran has already said every destined matter has a set time to transpire and you will soon know, and that the worldly life is no more than play. Now it condenses that to certainty without escape.\n\nA common self-deception today is to treat the promise of reckoning as theoretical, as if awareness of a distant appointment does not require preparation now. This verse shows promise is not theory; it is appointment with an organizer who varies signs so you will comprehend, whose word is perfected in truth and justice and cannot be changed, and who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing of whether you responded. Knowing there is no escape makes present choices meaningful: establishing prayer, being mindful, invoking morning and evening seeking His Face, and guarding food and speech become preparations for a coming that cannot be evaded, turning certainty from a threat into a guide toward spaciousness rather than constriction.",
  "135": "“O my people! Persist in your ways, for I ˹too˺ will persist in mine. You will soon know who will fare best in the end. Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed” gives the Prophet a dignified final word in this confrontation, after the declaration that what you have been promised will certainly come and you will have no escape. The opening, “O my people! Persist in your ways,” or “ya qawmi i'maloo ala makanatikum,” literally work according to your position, your standing, your ability. It is not permission to do wrong but a firm challenge: continue as you choose, upon the condition you claim is correct, for a limited time until the set matter arrives.\n\nThe response is balanced: “for I too will persist in mine,” or “inni amil.” The Prophet declares he will continue acting according to guidance, upon the clear proof from his Lord, turning his face to the Originator, following what is revealed, eating what His Name is mentioned upon, shunning sin open and secret, and walking with the light given to the dead heart made alive. The two persistences are left to run their courses toward their ends, which will reveal their true value, as earlier said every matter has a set time and you will soon know, and as earlier promised the triumph belongs to the mindful.\n\nThe suspense is then stated: “You will soon know who will fare best in the end,” or “sawfa ta‘lamoona man takoonu lahu aqibatu al-dar.” “Aqibah” means final outcome, end, consequence in the true Home. The House of Peace will show whose end is best, while the Fire will show whose alliance was ruinous.\n\nThe closing seals the law: “Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed,” or “innahu la yuflihu al-zalimoon,” a refrain already sounded when the worst wrong of fabricating lies was named and when all matters were said to be settled by the Best of Judges. Wrongdoers may plot, whisper with decorated speech, and appear to dominate, but lasting success, or “falah,” which means prosperity, flourishing, attainment of goal, will never belong to them.\n\nA misunderstanding is to read this challenge as abandonment or harshness. It is actually clarity after prolonged varying of signs, advising after warning, and turning away from mockery. When truth has been detailed for people who know and remember, continued debate with elegant deception only hardens hearts. Leaving each to their rank of deeds until the inevitable coming is both just and merciful, giving time for the open heart to find spaciousness before replacement arrives.",
  "136": "“The pagans set aside for Allah a share of the crops and cattle He created, saying, ‘This ˹portion˺ is for Allah,’ so they claim, ‘and this ˹one˺ for our associate-gods.’ Yet the portion of their associate-gods is not shared with Allah while Allah’s portion is shared with their associate-gods. What unfair judgment!” exposes a concrete example of the wrongdoing that will never succeed, following the challenge for each side to persist and see whose end is best. After theological arguments about guardians, revelation, and ranks, the Quran turns to everyday economics where shirk showed itself in dividing harvest and herds.\n\nThe practice described was that the polytheists would divide their produce, saying with words, “This portion is for Allah,” or “hatha lillah,” intended for guests, poor, or general good, “and this for our associates,” or “wa hatha li-shuraka’ina,” intended for idols, shrines, priests, and festivals. The phrase “so they claim,” or “bi-za‘mihim,” indicates this division had no authority from the Originator and Maintainer; it was a claim without knowledge. The actual handling revealed favoritism: “Yet the portion of their associate-gods is not shared with Allah while Allah’s portion is shared with their associate-gods.” If the idol’s portion was damaged by drought or loss, they would replace it from Allah’s portion, but never the reverse. And if something fell toward one side, they would assign it according to partiality to the idols, showing where their hearts truly leaned.\n\nThe verdict is sharp: “What unfair judgment!” or “sa’a ma yahkimoon,” evil is what they judge, what they decide. The word “saa’a” indicates ugliness and evil of the decision itself. The injustice is multiple: they take Allah’s creation, crops He made rain produce and cattle He originated from a single soul and made fit for labour, then they presume to apportion Him a share as if He were one partner among others, then they treat even that share as secondary.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think shirk is only in bowing. This verse shows it appears in allocating rights, wealth, and allegiance. When wealth is divided by whim rather than by the perfected Word that explains what is forbidden and permitted and commands paying dues at harvest without waste, the division testifies to misplaced reverence. The cure the Quran will later give is to return to the straight path of justice in measure, speech, and covenant, where worship is for Allah alone as Creator of all things and Maintainer of everything.",
  "137": "“Likewise, the pagans’ evil associates have made it appealing to them to kill their own children—only leading to their destruction as well as confusion in their faith” continues exposing the fruits of the unfair judgment just described, moving from crops and cattle to the most painful offering. After showing how division of produce favored idols over Allah, the verse shows the next step that the same decorated whispering takes: “Likewise, the pagans’ evil associates have made it appealing to kill their children,” or “za yyana li-katheer min al-mushrikeen qatla awladihim shurakaa’uhum.” The “shurakaa” here can mean the associated beings, whether devils among humans and jinn, idol custodians, or influential chiefs who legitimized the practice, who made infanticide seem noble, pious, and necessary. Reports indicate some Arabs buried infant daughters alive for fear of shame or poverty, and others killed sons for fear of sharing food, dedicating them as offerings to idols.\n\nThe phrase “have made it appealing,” or “zayyana,” is the same verb used for Satan beautifying misdeeds and for deeds made appealing to every people. Evil is not presented as ugly; it is adorned as honor, religious devotion, or responsible parenting. The Quran says this beautification was “only leading to their destruction,” or “li-yurdoohum,” to ruin them, to bring them to perdition, because murder of children destroys families and societies, and “as well as confusion in their faith,” or “wa li-yalbisu alaihim deenahum,” to cloak their religion in confusion, to mix and obscure it until truth cannot be distinguished, just as earlier faith was tarnished when mixed with falsehood.\n\nThe verse adds the divine frame: “Had it been Allah’s Will, they would not have done such a thing.” As earlier, had He willed they would not be polytheists, His allowance of their deed is not approval but wise testing that exposes where allegiance is placed. Then the Prophet is told, “So leave them and their falsehood,” or “fa-tharhum wa ma yaftaroon,” similar to leave them and their deceit, not entering endless dispute over fabrications while yet continuing to recite what the Lord forbade and commanded.\n\nA modern reader may think such cruelty belongs to the past alone. The underlying pattern persists whenever a society beautifies destroying the innocent for supposed advantage, confusing economics with morality. The Quran answers by calling it ruin and confusion, not wisdom, and by soon commanding “Do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for you and for them,” reminding that provision belongs to the One who splits seeds and who alone is judge between whim and justice.",
  "138": "“They say, ‘These cattle and crops are reserved—none may eat them except those we permit,’ so they claim. Some other cattle are exempted from labour and others are not slaughtered in Allah’s Name—falsely attributing lies to Him. He will repay them for their lies” details another layer of invented religion that followed the division of portions and the beautified killing of children. After warning that false associates lead to ruin and confusion, the Quran quotes their self-made laws. The phrase “These cattle and crops are reserved—none may eat them except those we permit,” or “hathihi an‘amun wa harthun hijr la yat‘amuha illa man nasha’u,” shows a claim of sanctity, or “hijr,” meaning restricted, taboo, dedication to idols. They declared certain animals and fields sacred, permitting only chosen men, priests, or tribal elites to benefit, excluding women and common people. The phrase “so they claim,” or “bi-za‘mihim,” again marks it as assertion without revelation, following assumption and whim, not the detailed Book.\n\nThe verse lists two further inventions. “Some other cattle are exempted from labour,” or “wa an‘amun hurrimat zuhooruha,” meaning they forbade riding or carrying burdens on special camels or other cattle dedicated to idols, leaving them to wander as honored yet useless, while elsewhere Allah says some cattle are fit for labour and forbids following Satan’s footsteps. “And others are not slaughtered in Allah’s Name—falsely attributing lies to Him,” or “wa an‘amun la yathkuroon isma Allahi alaiha iftira’an alaih,” describes animals slaughtered for associate-gods while claiming Allah ordained this division. The closing, “He will repay them for their lies,” or “sa-yajzeehim bi-ma kanu yaftaroon,” parallels earlier promises of repayment and severe humiliation for plotting, confirming that inventing religion has consequences at the ranks according to deeds.\n\nThe earlier dietary clarification that only carrion, running blood, swine, and sinfully dedicated offerings are forbidden unless compelled by necessity shows how far these invented restrictions go beyond the perfected Word. The Quran’s correction will soon list the only truly forbidden foods and then enumerate the four pairs of livestock, asking with knowledge who can testify that Allah forbade these categories. A common misunderstanding is to think extra piety comes from forbidding more things. This verse shows extra prohibitions invented without knowledge are lies against Allah that lead to ruin and that He will repay, while true mindfulness appears in following what He explained, eating what is lawful with His Name, paying dues at harvest without waste, and not following whim that confuses faith.",
  "139": "“They also say, ‘The offspring of this cattle is reserved for our males and forbidden to our females; but if it is stillborn, they may all share it.’ He will repay them for their falsehood. Surely He is All-Wise, All-Knowing” quotes a further invented rule that governed even the wombs of livestock, completing the picture of a faith confused by whim. After declaring some cattle and crops reserved for whomever they permit and exempting others from labour, the same voices add this distribution for fetuses: live offspring is restricted to males and forbidden to females, yet if the fetus is stillborn, or “maitah,” dead before birth, then men and women share it together as food. The Arabic says, “ma fi butooni hathihi al-an‘am khalisatun li-thukoorina wa muharramun ala azwajina wa in yakun maitatan fahum fihi shuraka.” The detail shows how far legislation without revelation can go into microscopic distinctions that create social injustice and ritual inconsistency, even permitting what is elsewhere forbidden when it suits them.\n\nThe divine response is concise: “He will repay them for their falsehood,” or “sa-yajzeehim wasfahum.” “Wasf” means their describing, their attribution, their false characterization of Allah’s law. The same promise of repayment for lies was just mentioned, and here it is repeated for this specific lie. The closing names anchor the judgment: “Surely He is All-Wise, All-Knowing,” or “innahu Hakeemun Aleem.” The wisdom who perfected the Word in truth and justice and the knowledge who encompasses all vision are the proper sources for such distinctions, not the whim of tribal councils who follow assumption and turn hearts away.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next passage that calls such people lost because they murdered children foolishly out of ignorance and forbade what Allah provided, falsely attributing lies to Him. Both killing children and restricting womb contents stem from claiming divine authority without knowledge, leading to destruction and confusion. A common error might be to see such livestock rules as harmless folklore. The Quran treats them as serious because they transfer the right to declare lawful and unlawful from the Creator of all things to human desire, exactly the association that, if obeyed, makes one a polytheist. The corrective teaching soon asks challengers to bring witnesses who can testify that Allah forbade this, and invites all to hear what your Lord has truly forbidden, beginning with not associating anything with Him, honoring parents, and not killing children for fear of poverty, setting lawful and unlawful upon the only wise and knowing foundation.",
  "140": "“Lost indeed are those who have murdered their own children foolishly out of ignorance and have forbidden what Allah has provided for them— falsely attributing lies to Allah. They have certainly strayed and are not ˹rightly˺ guided” delivers the comprehensive verdict over the entire system of invented divisions from crops to wombs. After detailing how pagans assigned shares to idols unfairly, how associates beautified child killing and confused faith, and how they reserved cattle and crops and split live versus stillborn offspring between males and females, the Quran now summarizes the fruit of all those fabrications. The opening, “Lost indeed are those who have murdered their children,” or “qad khasira allatheena qataloo awladahum,” uses the same word for loss as earlier for those who deny the meeting until the Hour takes them by surprise. Loss here is utter bankruptcy: they destroyed the very lives entrusted to them, moved by folly, or “safahan,” foolishness lacking sound intellect, and by ignorance, or “bi-ghairi ilm,” without knowledge, the refrain that runs through these verses.\n\nThe second crime compounds the first: “and have forbidden what Allah has provided for them—falsely attributing lies to Allah,” or “wa harramoo ma razaqahum Allahu iftira’an alallah.” The One who produced gardens wild and cultivated, who made rain produce clustered grain and varied fruits to be looked at in ripening, and who made some cattle for labour and others too small to be eaten with His Name, also explicitly explained what is forbidden. To forbid beyond that explanation and attach the prohibition to Allah is a lie against the Provider who is Self-Sufficient yet Full of Mercy.\n\nThe closing evaluates both errors: “They have certainly strayed and are not rightly guided,” or “qad dalloo wa ma kanu muhtadeen.” Straying, or “dalal,” is departure from the straight path that has been made clear to the mindful, the path to the Home of Peace. The phrase “not rightly guided” is stronger than merely mistaken; it says they are not even among the guided at all.\n\nFor today, this verse warns against two recurring distortions: harming the vulnerable while claiming higher principle, and making religion stricter or looser by personal whim while attributing it to God. True guidance, the Quran will soon recite, forbids associating any partner, honors parents, provides for children rather than killing them, avoids indecencies open and secret, and safeguards life and property, all taught by the All-Wise, All-Knowing who will repay each rank exactly.",
  "141": "“He is the One Who produces gardens—both cultivated and wild—and palm trees, crops of different flavours, olives, and pomegranates—similar ˹in shape˺, but dissimilar ˹in taste˺. Eat from the fruit they bear and pay the dues at harvest, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful” returns to the provision that the fabricators had restricted, now praising its Creator after condemning its misuse. The verse opens with “He is the One Who produces gardens,” or “wa huwa allathee ansha’a jannatin ma‘rooshatin wa ghairi ma‘rooshatin,” meaning trellised gardens raised and supported and non-trellised wild growth, both by His production. This follows the earlier rain passage that made green stalks produce clustered grain, and palm clusters within reach, correcting the earlier claim that some cattle and crops were reserved mysteriously for idols.\n\nThe catalog continues: palm trees, crops of different flavours, olives and pomegranates, similar in shape yet dissimilar in taste, echoing the earlier gardens of grapevines, olives, and pomegranates similar in shape dissimilar in taste that were signs for believers. The similarity points to one Creator, the dissimilarity points to His wisdom in varying taste from one water and soil.\n\nThen comes the balanced command for human use: “Eat from the fruit they bear and pay the dues at harvest,” or “kuloo min thamarihi itha athmara wa aatoo haqqahu yawma hasadihi.” Eating is permitted and encouraged; there is no taboo that forbids lawful fruit to women or reserves harvest for idols. Paying the due, or “haqq,” at harvest refers to obligatory charity from produce, like the later Islamic zakat on crops, given at the time of cutting, when abundance makes generosity natural. The command pairs enjoyment with responsibility, ensuring provision circulates.\n\nThe final caution, “but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful,” or “wa la tusrifoo innahu la yuhibbu al-musrifeen,” guards against both overconsumption that harms health and neglect that lets harvest spoil, and against extravagance that withholds dues. Waste, or “israf,” means exceeding the proper bound, which is precisely the error of the fabricators who exceeded by forbidding lawful provision.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think piety requires either ascetic refusal of good fruit or wasteful show. The verse teaches the middle path of straightness: enjoy what the Originator produces, recognize the right of the poor at the very moment of harvest, and avoid waste, because the Creator who causes seeds to sprout and knows every leaf does not love the one who scatters His bounty without measure. This discipline prepares hearts for the detailed prohibitions soon to be recited about life, wealth, and justice.",
  "142": "“Some cattle are fit for labour, others are too small. Eat of what Allah has provided for you and do not follow Satan’s footsteps. Certainly, he is your sworn enemy” continues the correction of livestock myths after the instruction about gardens and dues. The previous verse praised the One who produces varied gardens and enjoined eating, paying dues, and not wasting. Now the focus shifts to animals, which the pagans had divided into reserved, exempted, and specially dedicated categories. The phrase “Some cattle are fit for labour, others are too small,” or “thamaniyatu azwaj” expanded here as “hamoolatan wa farsha,” literally carriers and spread, meaning camels and oxen that carry loads and sheep and goats spread low near the ground, describes a natural division based on created function, not on superstitious taboo. Carriers serve for riding and burden, small grazers serve for meat, milk, and wool, each useful according to the Maintainer’s design.\n\nThe permission follows: “Eat of what Allah has provided for you,” or “kuloo mimma razaqakum Allah,” similar to the garden permission, reinforcing that provision from the One who sends down rain and causes sprouting is meant for lawful use, not for idol portions or male-only reservations. Eating with mention of Allah’s Name, when He has already explained what is forbidden, ties the meal to recognition of the Lord who is Self-Sufficient and Full of Mercy.\n\nThe warning is then explicit: “and do not follow Satan’s footsteps. Certainly, he is your sworn enemy,” or “wa la tattabi‘oo khutuwati al-shaytan innahu lakum aduwwun mubeen.” Satan was described as beautifying misdeeds, and as whispering with elegant deception through human and jinn devils. His footsteps, or “khutuwat,” are step-by-step suggestions that start with small deviations in food laws, then lead to dividing crops unfairly, then to killing children, then to calling it religion. Following those steps was exactly what led the pagans to stray and be not rightly guided.\n\nThe statement “he is your sworn enemy,” or “aduwwun mubeen,” obvious, open enemy, means enmity that is not hidden; his trick is to make the enmity look like friendship. A common misunderstanding is to view dietary disputes as trivial. This verse shows they are frontlines in resisting evil suggestion. By eating what Allah provided within His explained limits, paying dues without waste, and refusing to follow beautified whisperings, believers walk the straight path that leads to the Home of Peace, while those who reserve, forbid, and kill by whim follow the enemy whose hostility has been declared from the beginning.",
  "143": "“˹Allah has created˺ four pairs: a pair of sheep and a pair of goats—ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Has He forbidden ˹to you˺ the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females? Tell me with knowledge, if what you say is true’—” opens the systematic dismantling of the pagans’ invented livestock prohibitions after the general permissions to eat gardens and cattle without following Satan’s steps. The phrase “four pairs,” or “thamaniyatu azwaj,” literally eight in pairs, introduces the livestock that Arabs knew best: sheep, goats, camels, and oxen, each with male and female, forming four pairs. This verse pairs sheep and goats together, while the next verse will pair camels and oxen, covering the eight types whose wombs the pagans had legislated about.\n\nThe question put to them is piercingly logical: “Has He forbidden the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females?” If Allah forbade cattle, which category is forbidden? Is it all males of sheep and goats, or all females, or what the wombs carry whether male or female, alive or stillborn? The pagans’ rules were inconsistent: they forbade live offspring to females yet allowed stillborn to both, exempted some cattle from labour, and reserved others. No consistent divine principle explains such divisions. By asking about males versus females versus womb-contents, the Quran shows the arbitrariness of their claims, echoing earlier accusations that they follow whims out of ignorance and attribute lies without knowledge.\n\nThe challenge finishes with an appeal to evidence: “Tell me with knowledge, if what you say is true,” or “nabbi’ooni bi-‘ilmin in kuntum sadiqeen.” Knowledge, or “ilm,” means revelation, sure proof, authentic, not assumption, decorated speech, or majority opinion. This matches the earlier demand to bring witnesses who can testify Allah forbade this. The burden shifts to the fabricators to produce a revealed text, not a custom.\n\nA common mistake is to assume that because a practice is traditional, it must have divine sanction. The Quran demands knowledge for declaring lawful and unlawful. Claiming such without it led earlier generations to ruin and is a form of the greatest wrong that never succeeds. The subsequent verse will extend the same question to camels and oxen and add, “Or were you present when Allah commanded this?” emphasizing that none witnessed such a decree because none was given, and that the straight path forbids associating partners and harming the vulnerable rather than inventing meat taboos.",
  "144": "“and a pair of camels and a pair of oxen. Ask ˹them˺, ‘Has He forbidden ˹to you˺ the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females? Or were you present when Allah gave you this commandment?’ Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah to mislead others without ˹any˺ knowledge? Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people” completes the challenge about livestock that began with sheep and goats. The four pairs are now fully enumerated: sheep, goats, camels, oxen, the eight kinds whose provision Allah described as hamoolah and farsha, carriers and small grazers. Repeating the same question for camels and oxen drives home that the pagans’ system collapses no matter which species is examined. Is the male forbidden, the female, or the fetus within? No coherent answer exists because no prohibition of that kind was ever revealed.\n\nThe middle question is new and decisive: “Or were you present when Allah gave you this commandment?” or “am kuntum shuhada’a idh wassakum Allahu bi-hatha.” “Shuhada” means witnesses present at the giving of a testament, or “wassiyah,” a binding command. The answer is clearly no. No human was present at a divine decree that never occurred, and the polytheists had neither a Book perfectly explained nor a prophet delivering its truth to support their reservation of cattle for certain males, stillborn sharing, or exemption from labour. The claim thus rests on no testimony.\n\nThe verse then issues the same verdict sounded throughout this surah about the greatest injustice: “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah to mislead others without any knowledge?” The addition “to mislead others,” or “li-yudilla al-nas,” highlights social harm: these lies not only ruin the liar but also harden hearts of others, make misdeeds appealing, and confuse faith. The closing, “Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people,” or “inna Allaha la yahdi al-qawma al-zalimeen,” explains why such people’s arguments never find the straight path. The straight path was made clear to the mindful, but those who persist in wrongdoing, turning their hearts away as they refused to believe at first, are left to wander.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat laws about worship and food as personal taste. The Quran treats declaring something lawful or unlawful in Allah’s Name as an act that must be based on knowledge, not whim, and doing so falsely while misguiding others places one among the most unjust. The correction soon follows in the recitation of what is truly forbidden.",
  "145": "“I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden to eat except carrion, running blood, swine—which is impure—or a sinful offering in the name of any other than Allah. But if someone is compelled by necessity—neither driven by desire nor exceeding immediate need—then surely your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” is the precise answer to the prolonged confusion about food that began with unfair divisions of crops and cattle. After asking whether Allah forbade the two males, the two females, or what is in the wombs, and after exposing that no witnesses were present for such commandments, Allah now tells His Prophet to state exactly what is forbidden by revelation. The opening, “I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden,” or “la ajidu fi ma oohiya ilayya muharraman,” limits authority to revelation, not assumption, majority opinion, or decorated whispering. What is restricted must be found in the perfected Word.\n\nThe list is short and memorable: carrion, or “maitah,” animal that died without proper slaughter, running blood, or “dam masfooh,” flowing blood poured out, swine, or “lahm al-khinzeer,” flesh of swine described as “rijs,” impurity, foulness harmful to body and heart, and a sinful offering slaughtered in the name of other than Allah, or “fisqan uhilla li-ghayri Allah bihi,” disobedient slaughter dedicated to an idol or spirit. This covers what is inherently impure and what is corrupt in purpose, matching earlier commands to eat only of what Allah’s Name is mentioned upon and not to eat of what is not.\n\nThe verse then softens with mercy: “But if someone is compelled by necessity—neither driven by desire nor exceeding immediate need—then surely your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “fa-man idturra ghaira baghin wa la adin fa-inna Rabbaka Ghafurun Raheem.” Compulsion, or “idturra,” like starvation without other food, lifts prohibition only to the extent of need, without desire for enjoyment and without transgressing the limit, echoing the earlier allowance “except when compelled by necessity.” The closing names assure that the Lord who is Self-Sufficient and Full of Mercy does not desire hardship but forgiveness.\n\nA common mistake is to expand this short list by adding many extra prohibitions without knowledge, as the fabricators did with stillborn sharing rules. The Quran shows purity seeks clarity from revelation alone. Another mistake is to think necessity makes everything permissible indefinitely. The conditions “neither driven by desire nor exceeding need” keep the allowance bounded. Remembering this balance protects faith from both laxity that follows Satan’s footsteps and rigidity that burdens the mindful who walk the straight path.",
  "146": "“For those who are Jewish, We forbade every animal with undivided hoofs and the fat of oxen and sheep except what is joined to their backs or intestines or mixed with bone. In this way We rewarded them for their violations. And We are certainly truthful” clarifies that not every restriction came from the same cause, following the clear listing of the only foods forbidden to Muslims generally. After explaining the universal four impure categories with allowance for necessity, the Quran now addresses the special restrictions on the Jews, lest someone think the general Islamic allowance was historically always the same or that the pagans’ invented taboos mirrored it.\n\nThe phrase “We forbade every animal with undivided hoofs,” or “kulla thee zufurin,” includes animals whose hoofs are not cloven, such as camels and ostriches, and according to scholars predatory birds and beasts. The second restriction, “the fat of oxen and sheep except what is joined to their backs or intestines or mixed with bone,” details the fat of cattle and sheep, forbidden except what adheres to the back, the entrails, or is mixed within bone, like the fat of the tail or within marrow. These restrictions were not due to impurity of the foods themselves but were a disciplinary measure: “In this way We rewarded them for their violations,” or “thalika jazaynahum bi-baghyihim.” “Baghy” means transgression, exceeding bounds, particularly the violations of the Sabbath, consuming usury, and distorting laws while knowing. The recompense matched the crime, limiting what had been lawful to teach restraint.\n\nThe verse seals authenticity: “And We are certainly truthful,” or “wa inna la-sadiqoon.” Allah’s account of past legislation is perfectly true, just as His Word is perfected in truth and justice and none can change it.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think that because Jews were forbidden those fats, Muslims should likewise forbid them without revelation. The verse distinguishes universal impurity prohibitions from temporary disciplinary prohibitions that Islam lifted as mercy, confirming that the blessed Book both completes favor and details everything, bringing ease. Another mistaken reading is to see disciplinary prohibition as arbitrary revenge. The Quran frames it as just recompense for baghy, aiming to reform, and then shows the Prophet’s mercy which says his Lord is infinite in mercy, indicating that a return to faith restores ease. This keeps the core principle: declaring lawful and unlawful belongs to the All-Wise, All-Knowing who knows best where His message is placed and who assigns ranks according to deeds.",
  "147": "“But if they deny you ˹O Prophet˺, say, ‘Your Lord is infinite in mercy, yet His punishment will not be averted from the wicked people’” gives the Prophet his stance toward deniers after clarifying food laws and the history of restrictions for Jews. The opening, “But if they deny you,” or “fa-in kadhabooka,” anticipates the response to the detailed recitation about what is truly forbidden and what is not. After long exposition, some may still call the Quran ancient fables, deny its signs, or reject the Messenger who follows only what is revealed and seeks no reward. In that persistence, the Prophet is told to say, “Your Lord is infinite in mercy,” or “Rabbukum thoo rahmatin wasi‘ah.” The phrase mirrors earlier announcements that Allah has taken mercy upon Himself and that He is Full of Mercy. “Wide mercy,” or “wasi‘ah,” means vast, encompassing, that reaches the spacious heart opened to Islam as well as the tight chest that could still be opened if it humbled itself, and that allowed necessity to lift prohibitions with forgiveness.\n\nThe same sentence carries the counterpart: “yet His punishment will not be averted from the wicked people,” or “wa la yuraddu ba’suhu an al-qawm al-mujrimeen.” “Ba’s” means violent punishment, force, severity, like the torment that can come from above or below or the humiliation that overwhelms the plotting wicked. “Will not be averted” means when that punishment’s appointed time arrives after warnings have been varied and messengers have come so no society is destroyed while unaware, it cannot be pushed back by elegance of speech or by multitude of allies. The mujrimeen are the same wicked whose way was made distinct.\n\nThis two-part sentence balances the entire surah’s arc. Mercy explains why removal and replacement are delayed, why hardship precedes rupture, why the door remains for the ignorant sinner who repents and mends, and why gardens and cattle remain permitted with dues. Punishment explains why perfidy cannot continue indefinitely and why what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to take mention of wide mercy as guarantee of impunity while persisting in fabricating lies, killing children beautified as piety, and forbidding what Allah provided. The verse corrects that mercy invites return but does not cancel justice for the wicked who choose to remain. The next verse shows how polytheists will try to misuse mercy and divine will as an excuse, saying had Allah willed they would not have associated, a misuse answered by asking for knowledge rather than assumption. The Prophet’s message therefore remains first a good news of wide mercy and then an honest warning that the same mercy does not avert the consequence from those who persist as wrongdoers.",
  "148": "“The polytheists will argue, ‘Had it been Allah’s Will, neither we nor our forefathers would have associated others with Him ˹in worship˺ or made anything unlawful’” quotes the excuse that inevitably follows the balanced message of wide mercy whose punishment cannot be averted from the wicked. After being told to say your Lord is infinite in mercy yet His punishment will not be averted, the Prophet is warned that opponents will misuse divine will to avoid responsibility. Their argument, “Had Allah willed we would not have associated,” or “law sha’a Allahu ma ashrakn,” treats allowance as approval. Because Allah allowed them to live without immediate uprooting and gave respite, they claimed their shirk and their invented food taboos must therefore be pleasing to Him.\n\nThe Quran answers historically: “Likewise, those before them rejected the truth until they tasted Our punishment,” or “kathalika kathaba allatheena min qablihim hatta zaqoo ba’sana.” Earlier peoples, from those destroyed before you who were more established in the land, also called truth a lie and clung to ancestral worship until they tasted punishment, or “ba’sana,” the same word as the punishment that will not be averted from the wicked. Their tasting proved that divine allowance during the period of varying signs was not a sign of correctness but a period of testing.\n\nThen the Quran demands evidence: “Ask them, ‘Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? Surely you follow nothing but assumptions and you do nothing but lie,’” or “hal indakum min ‘ilmin fa-tukhrijoohu lana in tattabi‘oona illa al-zann wa in antum illa takhrusoon.” Knowledge, or “ilm,” is sure proof from revelation or sound reasoning, while “zann” is assumption and “takhrusoon” is guessing. This echoes earlier diagnoses: they follow whims out of ignorance, they fabricate lies, most people follow assumption and lie. The misuse of divine will is itself an assumption without knowledge, because knowledge reveals that divine will for testing is accompanied by detailed commands, from eating with His Name to not killing children, that distinguish the road to be followed.\n\nA modern parallel is the argument that if God really disapproved, He would not let success happen. The verse corrects that fallacy, pointing to the appointed term for every matter. Allowance is not endorsement; the perfected Word and the ranks according to deeds await the gathering to reveal who truly succeeded and who was among the wrongdoers who never succeed.",
  "149": "“Say, ‘Allah has the most conclusive argument. Had it been His Will, He would have easily imposed guidance upon all of you’” gives the final word on the misuse of divine will that polytheists raised as an excuse. After they said had Allah willed we would not have associated or forbidden anything, and after the Quran answered that earlier deniers also lied until they tasted Our punishment and demanded they produce knowledge instead of following assumption, the Prophet is told to announce the true nature of Allah’s argument. The phrase “Allah has the most conclusive argument,” or “fa-lillahi al-hujjatu al-balighah,” means His proof reaches its limit, penetrates, and leaves no excuse. “Balighah” means far-reaching, sufficient, exhaustive. His argument is conclusive because it combines creation signs, from splitting seeds to precise sun and moon, innate fitrah that calls only Him in darkest distress, and revealed insights from the detailed, perfected Book that clarifies what is forbidden and commands mercy and justice.\n\nThe second sentence confirms His power while affirming wisdom: “Had it been His Will, He would have easily imposed guidance upon all of you,” or “fa-law sha’a la-hadakum ajma‘een.” Earlier verses said had He willed He would not have been polytheists, had He willed He could have guided all, and signs are only with Allah. The repetition stresses that the diversity of responses is not due to lack of divine ability but to divine wisdom that chose a test where hearts are opened or tightened according to their willing, where devils whisper with elegant deception to incline the disbelieving heart, and where straight guidance remains perfectly clear for the mindful.\n\nThis balances responsibility and reliance. Responsibility because proof is conclusive, so straying cannot be blamed on lack of evidence; reliance because guidance itself is Allah’s gift, not human self-creation, so seeking it requires asking the One who opens chests to Islam. A misunderstanding is to think conclusive proof means compulsion. The verse shows conclusive proof invites, explains, warns, and leaves room to persist until you know who will fare best in the end. Another error is to think human arguments can rival the divine argument. No assumption, no decorated speech, no division of crops for idols can match a proof that encompasses every leaf and grain, every term and resurrection, and that culminates in the Home of Peace for the guided and the Fire for those who followed whim and will not be averted from the wicked when the appointed time arrives.",
  "150": "“Bring your witnesses who can testify that Allah has forbidden this’ If they falsely testify, do not testify with them. And do not follow the desires of those who deny Our proofs, disbelieve in the Hereafter, and set up equals with their Lord” challenges the fabricators to substantiate their food taboos after the conclusive argument has been claimed for Allah. The previous verse said Allah has the far-reaching proof and could have guided all had He willed. Now the Quran applies that proof to practice: who can stand as witness that Allah prohibited the divisions of sheep, goats, camels, and oxen the way they claim? The command “Bring your witnesses,” or “hulumma shuhada’akum,” demands formal testimony, as earlier the Prophet asked for witnesses about Moses’s Book and about where lawful and unlawful were distinguished. Witnesses here mean people of knowledge whose testimony counts, not merely tribal elders.\n\nThe prediction follows: “If they falsely testify, do not testify with them,” or “fa-in shahidoo fa-la tashhad ma‘ahum.” The Arabic anticipates they may indeed bring lying witnesses willing to swear Allah forbade such livestock in such detailed ways, willing to say Allah revealed this division for males and that for females. The Prophet is warned not to be swayed by oath and ceremony if content contradicts revelation. Truth is not established by number of witnesses when those witnesses follow assumption and decorated lies.\n\nThe reason to reject them is then explained: “And do not follow the desires of those who deny Our proofs, disbelieve in the Hereafter, and set up equals with their Lord,” or “wa la tattabi‘ ahwa’a allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa allatheena la yu’manoona bil-akhirah wa hum bi-Rabbihim ya‘diloon.” Three traits mark them: denial of signs after insights have come and varied signs explained, disbelief in the meeting that motivates mindfulness, and equating others with their Lord despite knowing He is Originator and Maintainer. Such desires are whim, or “hawa,” earlier linked to following whims without knowledge, and following them leads away from the straight path even if polished witnesses accompany them.\n\nA common error is to accept elaborate religious claims because they are presented with supposed witnesses or tradition. The verse teaches to test witnesses against the detailed, perfected Word that the People of the Scripture recognized as truth from the Lord. Another mistake is to think rejecting false testimony means harshness; the verse says do not follow their desires, echoing earlier commands to turn away but continue to remind, leaving final judgment to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who will inform each of what they used to do.",
  "151": "“Come! Let me recite to you what your Lord has forbidden to you: do not associate others with Him ˹in worship˺. Honour your parents. Do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for you and for them. Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly. Do not take a human life—made sacred by Allah—except with legal right. This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will understand” gathers a decade of argument into a luminous code of true prohibitions, replacing the fabricated divisions of livestock with the authentic limits set by the Lord. The opening, “Come! Let me recite to you what your Lord has forbidden,” or “ta‘alaw atlu ma harrama Rabbukum alaikum,” is a warm invitation, not a scolding, after the challenge to bring witnesses failed. What follows is revelation, or “atlu,” recited, not human claim.\n\nThe first and foundational prohibition is the counterpart to the earlier refrain “there is no god except Him, Creator of all things so worship Him”: “do not associate others with Him,” or “alla tushrikoo bihi shay’an,” not a single thing as partner. This alone corrects the entire economy of shares for associate-gods that judged so unfairly.\n\nThe second is “honour your parents,” or “wa bil-walidaini ihsana,” here expressed as forbidding neglect of parents, implying utmost goodness toward father and mother, the closest human tie that was severed when ties were broken at the return. The third addresses the beautified killing that led to ruin: “Do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for you and for them,” or “wa la taqtuloo awladakum min imlaq nahnu narzuqukum wa iyyahum.” Provision belongs to the Provider who produces gardens and cattle and knows every grain; fear of poverty should not drive murder, as He guarantees sustenance while commanding responsibility.\n\nThe fourth covers sexual morality: “Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly,” or “wa la taqraboo al-fawahish ma zahara minha wa ma batana,” don’t even approach immorality, linking to shun sin open and secret and to the earlier command to shun all sin visible and hidden. The fifth protects life: “Do not take a human life made sacred by Allah except with legal right,” or “wa la taqtuloo al-nafs allati harrama Allahu illa bil-haqq,” establishing sanctity of life whose reckoning will be swift on the Day the trumpet is blown.\n\nThe closing, “This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will understand,” or “thalikum wassakum bihi la‘allakum ta‘qiloon,” uses “wassakum” for a firm entrusted counsel, and ties to reason. A common misunderstanding equates religion with arbitrary food taboos; the verse shows the true forbiddens protect monotheism, family, children, chastity, and life, so perhaps you will understand and choose the straight path instead of attractive deviation.",
  "152": "“And do not come near the wealth of the orphan—unless intending to enhance it—until they attain maturity. Give full measure and weigh with justice. We never require of any soul more than what it can afford. Whenever you speak, maintain justice—even regarding a close relative. And fulfil your covenant with Allah. This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will be mindful” continues the recitation of true prohibitions that began with the invitation “Come, let me recite what your Lord has forbidden.” The previous verse listed foundations concerning monotheism, parents, children, indecencies, and sacred life, sealing them with “so perhaps you will understand.” This verse moves to social trusts that protect the vulnerable and the market.\n\nThe first command is about the orphan’s wealth: “And do not come near the wealth of the orphan—unless intending to enhance it—until they attain maturity,” or “wa la taqraboo mala al-yateemi illa billati hiya ahsan hatta yablugha ashuddahu.” “Do not come near” is stronger than “do not consume,” warning not even to approach with intention to use. The only exception is “with what is best,” or “billati hiya ahsan,” intending to invest, grow, and protect it until the orphan reaches strength and sense. This teaches that guardianship is trust, not opportunity, mirroring earlier principles that the mindful guard what is entrusted and that your Lord is not unaware of what you do.\n\nThe second command concerns commerce: “Give full measure and weigh with justice,” or “wa awfoo al-kaila wal-meezana bil-qist.” “Awfoo” means to fulfill completely, and “qist” means just proportion. In a society where trade was life, cheating with short measures was a way many deviants misled by whims. Justice in the scale reflects truthfulness that the Word perfected in justice demands.\n\nThe verse then adds a principle of mercy: “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford,” or “la nukallifu nafsan illa wus‘aha.” Trying perfectly is not beyond capacity; what is required is sincere effort to be just within ability, not superhuman precision. This balances the stern commands with the earlier announcement that your Lord is infinite in mercy.\n\nThe third command concerns speech: “Whenever you speak, maintain justice—even regarding a close relative,” or “wa itha qultum fa‘diloo wa law kana tha qurba.” Justice in speech means truthful testimony, fair judgment, honest advice, even when kinship pressures to favor. The fourth seals all: “And fulfil your covenant with Allah,” or “wa bi-‘ahdi Allahi awfoo,” the covenant that includes sincere monotheism and the detailed Book itself. The closing “so perhaps you will be mindful,” or “la‘allakum tathakkaroon,” moves from understanding in the previous verse to remembering, showing that these social laws lead from comprehension to continued remembrance of the gathering and of the Home of Peace.",
  "153": "“Indeed, that is My Path—perfectly straight. So follow it and do not follow other ways, for they will lead you away from His Way. This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will be conscious ˹of Allah˺” concludes the code of prohibitions that began with coming to hear what is truly forbidden. After detailing monotheism, parents, children, indecencies, sacred life, orphan wealth, measure, speech, and covenant, the Quran now distils them into a single road. The phrase “Indeed, that is My Path—perfectly straight,” or “wa anna hatha sirati mustaqeeman,” uses “hatha” to point to the whole set just recited. It is My path, not a human invention, the same path called your Lord’s Path perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, and the Straight Path sought daily in prayer. The single straight path contrasts with scattered paths.\n\nThe command follows: “So follow it,” or “fa-ittabi‘oohu,” a verb of walking behind and staying upon, and “do not follow other ways,” or “wa la tattabi‘oo al-subul,” do not follow diverging roads. “Subul” are side paths, trails that branch off, decorated by devils’ elegant speech, majority assumptions, whim-based food taboos, and beautified killing of children. Each side way promises a shortcut but leads away, or “tafarraqa bikum,” scattering you, dividing and dispersing you from His Way. The image is of one central highway and many tempting footpaths that lure travelers into the wilderness where they wander like the disoriented one earlier described.\n\nThe motivation is mercy: “from His Way,” or “an sabilihi,” the way that leads to the Home of Peace with your Lord as guardian and to ranks according to deeds before the True Master.\n\nThe seal is, “This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will be conscious,” or “thalikum wassakum bihi la‘allakum tattaqoon.” The same verb “wassakum,” He has counseled and entrusted you, appeared after the first set of prohibitions with “so perhaps you will understand” and after the orphan and measure set with “so perhaps you will be mindful.” Now it ends with “taqwa,” conscious awareness that protects. The progression is deliberate: understand the principle, remember it in social dealings, and become guarded by it. A common mistake is to view divine commands as isolated rules. This verse shows they are a unified path. Following the path keeps measure just, speech fair, and covenant fulfilled, while following other ways follows Satan’s footsteps, whose enmity is clear and whose whisper beautifies misdeeds. The path’s straightness is itself a mercy, sparing the traveler dispersing confusion.",
  "154": "“Additionally, We gave Moses the Scripture, completing the favour upon those who do good, detailing everything, and as a guide and a mercy, so perhaps they would be certain of the meeting with their Lord” connects the newly recited code to the earlier Books, showing that the straight path just summarized is not a new invention. After saying that is My straight path so follow it and do not follow scattered ways, the Quran recalls that a similar detailed guidance was given to Moses. The phrase “Additionally, We gave Moses the Scripture,” or “thumma atayna Musa al-kitab,” places Musa’s book in sequence after the universal prohibitions, confirming the continuity earlier noted where the Torah was a light and guidance and where Allah’s Word is perfected in truth and justice.\n\nIts purpose is described in three phrases. First, “completing the favour upon those who do good,” or “tamaman ala allathee ahsana,” meaning perfecting blessing upon those who excel in devotion, like the good-doers rewarded earlier among prophets’ families. Second, “detailing everything,” or “wa tafseelan li-kulli shay’,” explaining all that needed explanation for their time, similar to how this Quran is perfectly explained as a judge. Third, “and as a guide and a mercy,” or “wa hudan wa rahmah,” the two fruits desired for every revelation, guidance to the straight way and mercy that opens the chest to Islam.\n\nThe ultimate hoped result is introspective: “so perhaps they would be certain of the meeting with their Lord,” or “la‘allahum bi-liqa’i Rabbihim yu’minoon.” “Liqa” is the meeting that losers denied until the Hour surprised them and that the previous chapters used to sort the attentive who respond from the dead who are only raised. Certainty, or “yaqeen,” is the same word used for Abraham being shown wonders so he would be among the certain. The Book was given not to be venerated on shelves but to produce certainty about being gathered before the True Master.\n\nThis verse links back to the challenge that scriptures were only revealed to two groups before and we were unaware, a claim the next verse says can no longer be made. By mentioning Musa’s Scripture here, the Quran says the new blessed Book follows the same mercy pattern, inviting its readers to follow it as earlier peoples were invited, to avoid being among those who turn away and taste overwhelming humiliation, and to walk the single straight path with consciousness of the meeting that the Book was meant to make certain.",
  "155": "“This is a blessed Book We have revealed. So follow it and be mindful ˹of Allah˺, so you may be shown mercy” presents the Quran’s own identity after recalling Moses’s Scripture, offering it as the timely completion for this community. The phrase “This is a blessed Book,” or “wa hatha kitabun anzalnahu mubarak,” repeats the earlier description “This is a blessed Book We have revealed confirming what came before it.” Blessed, or “mubarak,” means abundant in blessing, growth, continuity, as earlier the rain producing clustered grain and the gardens similar yet dissimilar all pointed to blessed increase that lifts the worldly life from mere play. That blessing already described gardens and cattle; now the greatest blessed provision is the Book itself, whose recitation, reflection, and practice produce guidance and mercy.\n\nThe command is then twofold: “So follow it and be mindful,” or “fa-ittabi‘oohu wa ittaqoo.” “Ittabi‘oohu” means follow it step by step, staying on the straight path just described as My path perfectly straight, not merely admiring it. “Ittaqoo” means be mindful, guarding consciousness as earlier categories required for understanding, remembering, and being conscious of Allah. Following without mindfulness can become empty imitation; mindfulness without following has no road. Together they create the opening of the heart that the earlier verse said Allah grants to whom He wills.\n\nThe promised fruit is tender: “so you may be shown mercy,” or “la‘allakum turhamoon.” “Turhamoon” is passive, you may be given mercy, enveloped by it, as the Lord who is Self-Sufficient yet Full of Mercy took mercy upon Himself and announced He is infinite in mercy. Mercy here includes spaciousness of the chest, the light that makes a dead heart walk among people, the forgiveness for necessity after ignorance, and the Home of Peace in the end.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think the Book’s blessing is merely its presence in a home. The verse says blessing is activated by following and by mindfulness, leading to mercy. Another mistake is to think mercy is automatic without following. The conditional “so you may” shows mercy follows the path, not other scattered ways. This verse therefore serves as the surah’s direct invitation after the long recitation of true prohibitions, before the warnings that no excuse will remain for saying Scriptures were only for others and before the question whether they await angels or the Lord’s signs while belief after signs will no longer benefit. The time to follow the blessed Book is while following still counts, before the gathering where ranks are assigned and no escape is possible.",
  "156": "“You ˹pagans˺ can no longer say, ‘Scriptures were only revealed to two groups before us and we were unaware of their teachings’” removes the excuse that would otherwise be offered after the presentation of Moses’s Scripture and the blessed Quran. The previous verse said follow this blessed Book and be mindful so you may be shown mercy. Now the Quran anticipates the line the Meccans might use on the Day of Gathering: we were not given a Book like the Jews who received the Torah and the Christians who received the Gospel; we were the unaddressed, illiterate people without revelation, unaware, or “ghafileen,” of their study. The phrase “two groups before us,” or “ta’ifatayni min qablina,” refers to Jews and Christians, the two main communities known for receiving a Book. “We were unaware of their teachings,” or “wa in kunna an dirasatihim la-ghafileen,” means we did not study, we did not know, we were heedless of what their Books contained, perhaps due to language and access.\n\nBy revealing a blessed, detailed Book in Arabic, revealed to warn the Mother of Cities and around it and to confirm what came before, Allah removes that excuse entirely. No one in Mecca can now say guidance was culturally distant. The argument that revelation was for others is silenced by a revelation addressed directly to them in their tongue, confirmed by those among the People of the Scripture who recognized it as they recognize their children.\n\nThis verse connects to the earlier principle that no society is destroyed while its people are unaware. Awareness has now been provided through a Book that details everything and is a guide and mercy so they might be certain of the meeting. The varied signs have been turned so the way of the wicked becomes distinct and the mindful can comprehend.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think lack of prior exposure justifies continued neglect after exposure. The Quran here draws a line: prior unawareness may have been a claim before, but after the arrival of a clear, blessed Book, responsibility begins. For contemporary readers, the lesson extends. When a clear proof has reached a person in a language they understand, continuing to treat faith as play or to divide it by whim is no longer excusable by saying guidance belonged to others. The invitation is to treat the present moment as the time to follow while following is still accepted, before signs arrive when belief will no longer benefit those who did not believe earlier.",
  "157": "“Nor can you say, ‘If only the Scriptures had been revealed to us, we would have been better guided than they.’ Now there has come to you from your Lord a clear proof—a guide and mercy. Who then does more wrong than those who deny Allah’s revelations and turn away from them? We will reward those who turn away from Our revelations with a dreadful punishment for turning away” follows immediately after removing the first excuse about only two groups receiving Books. The second anticipated excuse was a boastful regret: had we been given the Book, we would have been more upright than the Jews and Christians who differed and altered. The Quran says that possibility has now been tested. “Now there has come to you from your Lord a clear proof—a guide and mercy,” or “faqad ja’akum bayyinatun min Rabbikum wa hudan wa rahmah.” “Bayyinah” is an illuminating, manifest proof, as earlier described insights from your Lord, and “huda wa rahmah” repeats the pair already used for Moses’s Scripture, showing continuity of mercy.\n\nThe arrival of this proof frames the judgment: “Who then does more wrong than those who deny Allah’s revelations and turn away from them?” This returns the greatest-wrong question asked earlier about fabricating lies and about claiming false revelation. Here the greatest wrong includes both denying, or “kathaba,” and turning away, or “sadafa,” actively turning aside, shunning, causing others to turn, echoing those who turn others away and distance themselves and who take faith as play. Denial is inward rejection; turning away is outward action that blocks.\n\nThe consequence is precise: “We will reward those who turn away from Our revelations with a dreadful punishment for turning away,” or “sa-najzee allatheena yasdifoon an ayatina soo’a al-athabi bima kanu yasdifoon.” The wordplay of “yasdifoon… bima kanu yasdifoon” highlights that punishment corresponds to the deed of persistently turning away. “Soo’ al-athab” means evil, dreadful torment, appropriate for those who saw the path perfectly straight made clear to the mindful yet chose scattered ways.\n\nA common contemporary self-deception is to imagine hypothetical better guidance while neglecting actual present guidance. The verse dismantles that fantasy: guidance has come, clear, detailed, and merciful, witnessed by those who recognize it, so the excuse no longer stands. The practical invitation is to value the proof at hand, to follow it while following leads to mercy, before the Day signs arrive when new belief no longer benefits the one who did not believe earlier.",
  "158": "“Are they awaiting the coming of the angels, or your Lord ˹Himself˺, or some of your Lord’s ˹major˺ signs? On the Day your Lord’s signs arrive, belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier or those who did no good through their faith. Say, ‘Keep waiting! We too are waiting’” presents the final delay the Meccans demanded after the excuses about scriptures were removed. Throughout this surah, deniers asked why no angel came, why no sign was sent, and said they would believe if a sign of their choosing came. The Quran now asks what they are actually waiting for: “Are they awaiting the coming of the angels,” as they requested with “Why has no visible angel come,” “or your Lord Himself,” in overwhelming majesty, “or some of your Lord’s major signs,” like the sun rising from the west or the decisive trumpet blast. Authentic hadith list among major signs the Dajjal, the beast, and the sun rising from the west, signs that occur so close to the Hour that choice ends.\n\nThe answer is a universal law of faith and time: “On the Day your Lord’s signs arrive, belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier or those who did no good through their faith.” The Arabic “yawma ya’tee ba‘du ayati Rabbika la yanfa‘u nafsan imanuha lam takun amanat min qablu ow kasabat fi imaniha khaira” sets two conditions where belief fails after signs: a person who never believed before, and a person who believed but earned no good through that belief, letting faith remain nominal without prayer, mindfulness, just measure, or honoring life. This matches earlier warnings thatOnly the attentive respond while the dead are only raised, and that even if angels, dead speakers, and all signs were gathered, they still would not believe unless Allah willed. After the final sign, the period of testing that gives meaning to spacious versus tight chests, to remembrance and understanding, closes. What remains is the gathering to the True Master for assignment of ranks.\n\nThe Prophet is told to answer their wait with a wait: “Say, ‘Keep waiting! We too are waiting,’” or “qul intaziroo inna muntaziroon.” “Intizar” means expectant waiting, but the two waits are opposite: they wait for excuse, believers wait for promise, as earlier you will soon know who will fare best in the end.\n\nA common mistake is to postpone faith until a dramatic sign forces it. The verse shows forced faith after signs is not counted, just as the wisher before the Fire who begs to return is not returned. The time to believe and to earn good through that belief is while insights from your Lord are available, while the blessed Book invites following toward the Home of Peace.",
  "159": "“Indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are not responsible whatsoever for those who have divided their faith and split into sects. Their judgment rests only with Allah. And He will inform them of what they used to do” separates the Prophet’s mission from the fragmentation that arises when scattered ways are followed. After warning that waiting for compelling signs will be too late, the Quran addresses the result of ignoring the single straight path: division. The phrase “those who have divided their faith and split into sects,” or “allatheena farraqoo deenahum wa kanoo shiya‘an,” means they broke their religion into parts, taking some commands while abandoning others, and formed distinct factions, or “shiya‘,” groups each pleased with its fragment, as earlier warned “or split you into conflicting factions and make you taste the violence of one another.” The reference includes Meccan polytheists who split pure monotheism into shares for idols, Jews and Christians who differed over the Book after knowledge came, and any future community that takes the blessed, detailed, perfected Word and turns it into competitive sects rather than a single path.\n\nThe Prophet is told, “you are not responsible whatsoever for them,” or “lasta minhum fi shay’,” you are in nothing of them, complete disassociation. This echoes earlier limits that he is not a keeper, not a maintainer, not the one whose will decides guidance for all. His task remains as deliverer of good news and warner with insights, following what is revealed, turning away from mockery while still reminding.\n\nThe rest belongs to Allah: “Their judgment rests only with Allah. And He will inform them of what they used to do,” or “innama amruhum ila Allah thumma yunabbi’uhum bi-ma kanu yaf‘aloon.” The same informing that concludes every return to your Lord appears: He will tell them with complete knowledge free of forgetfulness, as earlier no soul is unaware to Him, no leaf falls without His knowledge, no measure is missed. This assignment to divine judgment means believers should not hasten to excommunicate or condemn hearts; they keep to the straight way, leave plotting to its self-destruction, and trust the Best of Judges.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think sectarian division is insignificant. The verse shows it follows departure from the single path and leads away from His Way, scattering what should stay united under “there is no god except Him, Creator of all so worship Him” and “that is My path perfectly straight so follow it.” The cure is returning to the recited code: pure monotheism, parental goodness, protecting children and lives, guarding orphan wealth, just measure, just speech, and covenant, all commanded so perhaps you will be mindful and conscious, preserving unity under the Lord’s guidance rather than under rival desires.",
  "160": "“Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold. But whoever comes with a bad deed will be punished for only one. None will be wronged” announces the arithmetic of mercy that governs the informing and judgment just mentioned as resting only with Allah. After describing division into sects and assigning their affair to the True Master, the Quran now reveals the scale on which deeds are weighed on the Day the weighing will be just. The good deed, or “hasanah,” is any act done with sincere faith unmixed with shirk, such as turning the face to the Originator, guarding prayer, paying dues at harvest without waste, or speaking justly even regarding kin, each rooted in obedience to the perfected Word. For such a deed the reward is “tenfold,” or “ashru amthaliha,” ten times its likeness, and authentic hadith explain Allah may multiply further up to seven hundred or more for some, according to sincerity and need. This multiplication exhibits the Lord who took mercy upon Himself, who is infinite in mercy and full of mercy.\n\nThe counterpart is measured restraint: “whoever comes with a bad deed will be punished for only one,” or “fa-la yujza illa mithlaha.” “Sayyi’ah” includes fabricated divisions of cattle, killing children beautified as piety, taking faith as play, whispering with elegant deception, and turning away from revelations. The recompense for a single such deed is only its like, not multiplied, and forgiveness remains possible, especially for one compelled by necessity without desire, since your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. This asymmetry proves generosity: good is cultivated like rain producing clustered grain multiplied, while evil is limited to its exact measure.\n\nThe closing guarantee, “None will be wronged,” or “wa hum la yuzlamoon,” echoes earlier assurances that each will have ranks according to deeds and that your Lord is never unaware of what they do, and that the Home of Peace is for those whose path led there because of what they used to do. No leaf is unrecorded, no grain in darkness missed, no sincere invocation morning or evening lost; likewise, no fabricated penalty exceeding the deed will be assigned.\n\nA common worry is that one’s few goods will be overwhelmed by many wrongs. The verse shows mercy already weights the balance: ten to one, with room for forgiveness to erase the one. The wise response is to come with good deeds at all, not to remain among those who wait for angels or final signs while doing no good through their faith, because after that arrival belief will no longer benefit. Coming early with even one sincere good keeps the scale heavy toward success.",
  "161": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Surely my Lord has guided me to the Straight Path, a perfect way, the faith of Abraham, the upright, who was not one of the polytheists’” gives the Prophet his own joyful testimony after detailing sects and the arithmetic of mercy. Throughout this surah, the straight path was described as your Lord’s path perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, and the Prophet was commanded to follow the guidance of Abraham and his fellow prophets. Now he is instructed to announce where he stands. The phrase “Surely my Lord has guided me to the Straight Path,” or “inna Rabbani hadanee ila siratin mustaqeem,” personalizes the earlier imperative to follow the path; he has indeed been guided, not wandering as the disoriented one in the wilderness. The addition “a perfect way,” or “deenan qiyaman,” suggests a religion that is upright, established, sustaining, able to support life fully, unlike the scattered ways that divide.\n\nThe content of that way is named: “the faith of Abraham, the upright, who was not one of the polytheists,” or “millata Ibraheema haneefan wa ma kana min al-mushrikeen.” “Millah” is the creedal community, the path lived by Abraham when he turned his face to the Originator, rejected what sets, declared “I am not one of the polytheists,” and argued without fear of idols. “Haneefan” recalls that same declaration, inclining to truth away from every crook. The verse therefore links the final universal message back to the archetypal monotheist shown wonders so he would be certain. The blessed Book that warns the Mother of Cities is not a break from that faith but its confirmation and completion, as the Book was said to confirm what came before.\n\nA common misconception is to picture Islam as a novel deviation for Arabs. The Quran frames it as the ancient, upright, straight religion itself, perfected, not invented, resting on the same pure monotheism that made security and guidance belong to those who do not tarnish faith with falsehood. For the listener who heard the code forbidding shirk, honoring parents, protecting children and lives, guarding orphans and measures, this verse names the overall home for those commands: Abraham’s upright faith. Embracing it places a person among those who will have the Home of Peace with their Lord as Guardian because of what they used to do, rather than among those who divided faith and split into sects awaiting the angels.",
  "162": "“Surely my prayer, my worship, my life, and my death are all for Allah—Lord of all worlds” deepens the testimony of the straight path into a total dedication that follows naturally from identifying the path as Abraham’s upright faith. After saying my Lord has guided me to that path, the Prophet is told to declare the scope of his devotion. The phrase lists four layers: “my prayer,” or “salatee,” the formal, regular act commanded to be established and guarded as preparation for being gathered; “my worship,” or “nusuki,” which includes all devotional rites, sacrifice, pilgrimage, charity, and every act of drawing near, as well as the animal sacrifice whose proper dedication was disputed in the food laws; “my life,” or “mahyaya,” the ongoing days spent invoking morning and evening seeking His Face, earning ranks according to deeds; and “my death,” or “mamati,” the final return when the soul is taken without neglect and brought back to its True Master.\n\nThe summary is “all for Allah—Lord of all worlds,” or “lillahi Rabbil-‘alameen,” the exact name that opened this surah with all praise for Allah, Lord of all worlds, and that closed the uprooting of wrongdoers with all praise for Allah, Lord of all worlds. Dedicating all to that Lord means no share is given to associate-gods, neither in crops nor in cattle nor in vows nor in fear. This mirrors Abraham’s declaration “I have turned my face to the Originator” and the earlier command “He is Creator of all things so worship Him alone, and He is Maintainer of everything.” Life and death are not two separate owners; both belong to the One who causes the dawn to break, who originates from a single soul, who causes seeds to sprout and brings forth living from dead.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to compartmentalize religion to prayer times alone while living the rest of the day by other guides. This verse corrects that fragmentation, showing that straightness requires integrating worship, daily living, and even dying under one Lordship. It also answers those who divided religion and split into sects; their way fragments dedication among many ways, while the guided way gathers prayer, rites, life span, and deathbed into a single orientation toward the Lord whose Word is perfected and who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. Living that dedication yields the reward tenfold for good while protecting from the dreadful punishment for turning away, and it keeps the heart among the grateful who recognize the Giver behind every garden and every star that guides.",
  "163": "“He has no partner. So I am commanded, and so I am the first to submit” seals the total dedication just declared, where prayer, worship, life, and death were made for Allah alone. The first phrase, “He has no partner,” or “la shareeka lahu,” is the purest negation of shirk, encompassing every form of partnership previously exposed: no share in creation, for He is Creator of all things; no share in provision, for He provides for all and is not in need of provision; no share in legislation of lawful and forbidden, for His Word is perfected; no share in rescue from darkness on land and sea; and no share in being sought as judge or guardian. This mirrors the earlier testimonies “there is no god except Him” and “say, Allah’s guidance is the only true guidance and we are commanded to submit to the Lord of all worlds.”\n\nThe second phrase places this creed within prophetic obedience: “So I am commanded, and so I am the first to submit,” or “wa bi-thalika umirtu wa ana awwalu al-muslimeen.” The “so” indicates this command flows logically from His having no partner. The Prophet was earlier told to say “I have been commanded to be the first to submit and not be one of the polytheists” and “I will not follow your desires, for then I would be astray.” Here the primacy of submission, or “awwal al-muslimeen,” means among his people and era he leads by example, the foremost in turning the face to the Originator, as Abraham was the model of the upright. It also reflects humility: he does not claim superiority over earlier submitters like Noah and Abraham; he claims priority in his own community’s response.\n\nThis verse resolves the long debate about shares for associate-gods. No partner means no portion of crops, no dedicated cattle withheld from labour, no stillborn shared, no child sacrificed to beautified murder, no oaths to fabricated intercessors. All belongs to the Lord of all worlds, whose mercy is infinite yet whose punishment will not be averted from the wicked.\n\nA common misconception is that affirming “no partner” is mere doctrine without action. The Quran binds it to command and submission, to practice in prayer and sacrifice, life and death. Another error is to think being first to submit earns rank before others regardless of deed. The verse links primacy to obedience; ranks remain according to deeds, and even foremost submission is itself a gift of being guided, for Allah guides whomever He wills and opens that heart to Islam while leaving others constricted as they chose tightness.",
  "164": "“Should I seek a lord other than Allah while He is the Lord of everything? No one will reap except what they sow. No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another. Then to your Lord is your return, and He will inform you of your differences” asks the final rhetorical question about Lordship after comprehensive dedication to the Lord without partner. Earlier the Prophet was taught to ask “Will I take any guardian other than Allah, the Originator of the heavens and earth,” and “Should I seek a judge other than Allah while He has revealed the detailed Book.” Now the equivalent is Lordship itself: “Should I seek a lord other than Allah,” or “a-ghaira Allahi abghi rabban,” when He is “Lord of everything,” or “Rabb kulli shay’.” Rabb means nurturer, owner, master, the Maintainer of everything who split seeds, broke dawn, guides by stars, originated from one soul, produces gardens, and maintains all. To seek another lord besides such a universal Rabb contradicts reason as starkly as fearing idols that cannot benefit or harm.\n\nThe verse then establishes three moral laws that flow from that sole Lordship. First, “No one will reap except what they sow,” or “wa la taksibu kullu nafsin illa alaiha,” no soul earns but against itself; deeds are its own harvest, like the tenfold reward for a good deed and single recompense for a bad deed where none is wronged. Second, “No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another,” or “wa la taziru waziratun wizra ukhra,” no burdened bearer carries another’s load, answering the losers who will bear their sins on their backs and the plotters who thought burdens could be shifted. Intercession fails without Allah’s permission, and each rank is according to what was done, because the Lord is never unaware.\n\nThird, “Then to your Lord is your return, and He will inform you of your differences,” or “thumma ila Rabbikum marji‘ukum fa-yunabbi’ukum bima kuntum fihi takhtalifoon.” Differences, or “ikhtilaf,” include the very disputes this surah varied signs to resolve: about shares for idols, forbidden cattle, the meeting, and the straight path. Informing will be with sure knowledge, for We were never absent, and ranks will settle who strayed by following assumption.\n\nA mistaken hope is that seeking another lord or shifting burdens offers escape. The verse shows no escape; the self-sufficient Lord of everything holds every earner to its own earning and then gathers all to inform with perfect justice, swift in reckoning yet all-forgiving for the repentant.",
  "165": "“He is the One Who has placed you as successors on earth and elevated some of you in rank over others, so He may test you with what He has given you. Surely your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” closes the entire surah with its governing pattern of rank, test, and mercy, returning to themes sounded at the opening. The phrase “placed you as successors on earth,” or “khala’ifa al-ard,” means He made generations follow generations, entrusted with stewardship of earth, much as earlier He said He can do away with you and replace you with whoever He wills as He produced you from other peoples. Each generation is a successor charged with the straight path now summarized as pure worship, honorable dealings, and remembrance.\n\nThe next phrase acknowledges difference: “and elevated some of you in rank over others,” or “wa rafa‘a ba‘dakum fawqa ba‘din darajatin.” Ranks in provision, knowledge, strength, and trial have varied throughout this surah, from Abraham raised for his argument to the poor believers defended morning and evening, from kings like David and Solomon to the humble like Jonah delivered from darkness. The purpose follows: “so He may test you with what He has given you,” or “li-yabluwakum fi ma atakum.” “Yabluwakum” means to test, to try, to prove, as earlier He tested some by means of others so some would say has Allah favoured these, and as He varied abundance to see if humbling would occur. What you are given, garden or cattle, insight or office, is the very material of the test.\n\nThe closing pair of names holds the balance that has governed warnings and promises throughout: “Surely your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “inna Rabbaka saree‘u al-iqab wa innahu la-Ghafoorun Raheem.” “Swift in punishment” echoes the swiftest Reckoner whose judgment cannot be escaped, while “All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” echoes the mercy taken upon Himself and the infinite mercy offered to those who shun sin open and secret, follow the blessed Book, and keep prayer. A common misunderstanding is to read elevation as proof of divine favoritism that excuses neglect. The verse corrects that: elevation is a loan to be tested, and the swift reckoning ensures no favouritism. Another error is to think punishment’s swiftness contradicts mercy; the Quran binds them, showing punishment is swift for the persistent wicked who turn away after the conclusive argument, while forgiveness is certain for those who return, so the final posture is hope tempered by responsibility before the inevitable gathering where you will return and be informed of your differences."
};
